window.COMPANY_BRAIN_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title":"BizBash","sidebarTitle":"","description":"What bizbash.com actually is: editorial, print, lists, awards, webinars, and the live events that sit under the brand.","headings":["What the site is","Product stack","Recognition programmes","Live events","Team as published on About Us","What not to create from this site","Next"],"body":"BizBash is the events-media brand inside Connect Meetings & Events. The public site is bizbash.com. Captured 12 August 2026 . Founded in 2000 . Acquired by Connect Meetings in 2020 . VP BizBash: Michela Giovannotto. Sales inquiries published as bizbash.sales@informa.com . Company Brain previously treated BizBash as a one-line media partner plus a 2025 rate card. The website is a full product: daily editorial, two print issues a year, a recognition calendar, webinars, a supplier directory, and its own live events. Rate-card dollars stay on Media and amplification. This page is the brand. What the site is Daily digital coverage for meeting and event professionals. The About page describes the audience as planners, vendors, suppliers, business owners, PR professionals, and aspiring industry leaders. Editorial verticals on the live nav: Vertical What it covers --- --- Event production and planning Experiential marketing, fabrication, programming, rentals, corporate events, meetings, trade shows Event design Décor, corporate swag Event catering Food and beverage trends Event tech AI for meetings, tools, virtual events Event venues Hotels and new-venue guides by city Sporting events Brand activations and sports-event coverage That last vertical is why Sports Innovation Forum and The Women's Summit sit on the BizBash side of the business. Product stack As listed on About, Get Featured, Build Your Brand, Publications, Webinars, and On Demand. Surface What it is Where money is documented --- --- --- Daily site Editorial and sponsored content Media kit BizBash Buzz Newsletter, three times a week 2026 kit: 40K event professionals, \\$4,000 BizBash Sports Newsletter 2026 kit: \\$4,000; more than 1,600 sports planners. 2025 kit did not publish a price Print magazine Two issues a year. Latest on the site: Spring 2026 Venue and Supplier Guide ; Fall 2025 25th Anniversary Issue 2026 kit still prices Spring/Fall issues at \\$7,000+ while body copy describes 2024/2025 issues. Do not treat that as a confirmed 2026 magazine rate Webinars Live (CMP-credit capable per About) and on-demand at bizbash.com/webinars. Upcoming on capture: 13 and 19 August 2026 Media kit webinars start at \\$25,000 On Demand Hub for past webinars and white papers White papers / surveys / e-books \\$25,000+ in the 2026 kit Directory directory.bizbash.com — supplier and venue listings. The homepage is JavaScript-heavy; no listing counts were retrievable on capture Not in the 2025 kit Build Your Brand Advertiser intake at bizbash.com/build-your-brand-with-bizbash. Offers a 2026 media kit and editorial calendar behind a form 2026 kit is now on Media and amplification. 2025 figures remain as comparison Get Featured Editorial pitch inbox at bizbash.com/get-featured. Asks for pitches within two weeks of an event, with 3-5 images and a vendor list Not a paid product Publications bizbash.com/publications Print issues Events listing bizbash.com/events Live-event register-now list About Us claims 500,000 average monthly readership. The 2025 Events \\ Travel \\ Sports media kit cites 107K monthly uniques and 231K monthly views for BizBash. Both figures are published. Do not blend them. Logged on Known issues. Recognition programmes These are how the brand creates inventory and why a supplier asks to \"get on a BizBash list.\" Calendar taken from Get Featured, 12 August 2026. Programme 2026 timing published on the site --- --- Industry Innovators Catering (Jan), Vendors & Suppliers (Feb), Planners / Designers / Producers (Mar), Event Technology (Apr), Experiential Agencies (May), Brands (Jun) 40 Under 40 Nominations closed 9 January 2026. Publishing April. Live 2026 class is on the site Fresh Faces Nominations in June. Publishing July. 2026 class is live Sports Power Players Nominations in August. Publishing September Most Innovative Meetings Nominations in September. Publishing November Event Experience Awards 14th annual. Entries open; a site article says submit by 1 October . Hosted at informaconnect.com/bizbash-event-experience-awards. Key-dates and new-categories URLs returned 404 on capture The Spring 2026 magazine issue carries the new 40 Under 40 class and the 13th Annual Event Experience Awards winners. The live awards site is already selling the 14th . Live events Three BizBash-branded live events are on the 2026 calendar. None of them are appointment marketplaces. Event Dates Venue Internal page Public site --- --- --- --- --- BizBash Innovation Forum 23-25 Aug 2026 Tampa Convention Center Internal informaconnect.com/bizbash-innovation-forum The Women's Summit 27-28 Oct 2026 The Woodlands Internal informaconnect.com/the-womens-summit Sports Innovation Forum 28-30 Oct 2026 The Woodlands Internal informaconnect.com/sports-innovation-forum The BizBash site also lists Connect Marketplace (24-26 August, Tampa) as a register-now event. That is the Connect flagship, not a BizBash product. Innovation Forum occupies the same building the day before Marketplace opens. Brand About: bizbash.com/about-us. 2025 media kit: Turtl. More URLs on Public event sites. Team as published on About Us About Us is a public roster. It does not always match Master People. About Us title Company Brain / Master People --- --- Sarah Kloepple, Deputy Editor Directory: Editorial Director . Bylines on bizbash.com still say Editorial Director Orlon Daniels, Sales Operations Manager Directory: Customer Success Manager , reporting to Andrew Dysart. Event sites have also used Senior Operations Leader / Manager — see Known issues Chris Conwell, VP, Media Sales Directory: VP, Group Media John D'Adamo, Regional VP, Sales Not in the Company Brain directory Jami Laub, Director of Group Media Not in the Company Brain directory Matt Johnson, Michela Giovannotto, Jordan Bouldin, Madison Kobb, Amanda Dennihy, Abby Ramsey, Bennett Carpenter, and Alex Conwell appear on both. Claire Hoffman, Michele Laufik, and Shannon Thaler are listed as Senior Contributing Editors and already sit on the o","path":"/company/bizbash"},{"title":"Brands","sidebarTitle":"Brand portfolio overview","description":"Connect, BizBash, TSNN, and Connect Travel - what each brand is, who runs it, and how it shows up in Salesforce.","headings":["How brands appear in the data","Media partnerships across events","Brand-facing contact routes","Next"],"body":"Four brands sit under Connect Meetings & Events. They share one Salesforce org, one sales organisation, and one event operations team, but they address different audiences and carry different products. The meetings marketplace brand. Owns the flagship Connect Marketplace plus every regional marketplace and specialty event. This is the appointment-event business. Public hub: meetings-events-tradeshows. Events media and awards brand. Daily site bizbash.com, print, lists, Event Experience Awards, webinars, and its own live events. Michela Giovannotto, VP BizBash. Trade Show News Network. Daily site tsnn.com, newsletter suite, Top 250, and TSNN Awards. Group content led by Danica Tormohlen, VP of Group Content. The travel-sector arm. Live events: eTourism Marketplace and eTourism Summit. Ashleigh Trout is VP, Travel. How brands appear in the data Brand is not a single field. It surfaces in three places, and mixing them up is a common analysis error. Field Object Values Use for --- --- --- --- Organization c OpportunityLineItem Meetings, Travel, BizBash, TSNN Splitting revenue by business unit. This is the reliable one. Product Type c OpportunityLineItem Association, Sports, BizBash, Marketplace, Webinar, and 50\\+ others Product-level mix Event Name c OpportunityLineItem Spring Marketplace, MEGA, Winter Marketplace, and others Attributing revenue to a specific event Connect and BizBash grew up as separate businesses and their account, contact, and lead data has not been fully merged. As of the current analysis there are 26 account-team rep assignments covering 20,876 advertiser accounts on the BizBash side. That gap is the whole point of the data consolidation project. Do not assume BizBash advertiser accounts have an owner. Media partnerships across events BizBash, TSNN, and Corporate Event News appear as media partners on most Connect event sites. They are internal brands acting as media partners to our own events, which is intentional but occasionally confuses external readers of a sponsor list. Brand-facing contact routes Route Address --- --- General event inquiries (informa.com contact pages) mac.kitchin@informa.com General inquiries (connectmeetings.com contact pages) info@connectmeetings.com Sponsorship inquiries, regional events sales@connectmeetings.com BizBash and Sports Innovation Forum sponsorship bizbash.sales@informa.com Cancellations connect.cancellations@informa.com Every event site currently publishes two different contact pages with overlapping but inconsistent rosters and different email domains. Both are preserved rather than reconciled - see Known issues. Next What bizbash.com actually sells: editorial, print, lists, awards, and live events.","path":"/company/brands"},{"title":"Business model","sidebarTitle":"Business and revenue model","description":"How the reverse tradeshow, hosted buyer programme, and supplier packages actually make money.","headings":["The core mechanic","Supplier package structure","Where the rest of the revenue comes from","Track qualification","Buyer economics we quote to sponsors","Next"],"body":"The commercial model is a reverse tradeshow: suppliers pay for scheduled appointments with hosted buyers. This page explains how that makes money. The core mechanic A traditional tradeshow sells floor space and hopes buyers walk by. We invert it. Event planners apply to the hosted buyer programme. Qualification is real: a typical bar is generating 50 or more rooms on peak nights, ability to rotate destinations nationally or regionally, and a verifiable two-year event history. Applications take about ten minutes and require a refundable deposit, usually \\$200. Qualified hosted buyers get roundtrip airfare, hotel for the event nights, meals, all networking events, and the education programme, at no cost. In exchange they commit to a full appointment schedule. Suppliers buy tiered packages priced by appointment count. At the flagship these run from Ice Breaker at \\$4,250 for 12 appointments up to Deal Closer at \\$5,950 for 40 appointments with guaranteed top-five requests. Appointments are matched, often AI-assisted, and scheduled in advance. Nobody arrives hoping for a conversation. Suppliers arrive with a diary. The buyer deposit is refunded on completing the assigned appointments, and forfeited on no-shows. Rebooking for next year happens onsite in a dedicated rebooking lounge. The deposit is the enforcement mechanism for the whole model. If hosted buyers skip appointments, suppliers do not renew, and renewals are 79 percent of supplier revenue. Attendance compliance is a commercial control, not an admin detail. Supplier package structure Pricing published for Connect Marketplace 2026. Regional events start at \\$4,250 for 12 appointments. Package Price Appointments Notes --- --- --- --- Deal Closer \\$5,950 40 Top five requests guaranteed, preferred listing, 15 available Elite Connecter \\$5,250 30 Marked most popular, 35 available Growth Accelerator \\$4,850 24 Ice Breaker \\$4,250 12 Entry tier, also the regional-event starting price Power Pass (Supplier) \\$1,950 0 Access and education only, no scheduled meetings Power Pass (Buyer) \\$350 0 Planners and buyers only Where the rest of the revenue comes from Advertising attached to an event: custom video, lead insights, digital media, event targeting, onsite ads. Priced from roughly \\$3,000 for onsite ads up to \\$9,950 for the Texas Meeting Planners Guide. BizBash issues, marketplace guides, the Sports Facilities Guide, pre-show and post-show emails. Typically \\$5,000 to \\$7,000 plus per placement. Hosted meals, afternoon amp-ups, hosted buyer amenity shop, closing night party. Priced through the sponsorship prospectus, not published. Non-hosted registrations, Power Passes, and services line items. In Salesforce these map to OpportunityLineItem.Product Category c , which takes four values: Digital , Event , Event Amplification , and Special Projects . Business unit is Organization c : Meetings , Travel , BizBash , TSNN . Track qualification Marketplace tracks are how we keep the room valuable. Each has its own bar, on top of the general minimum of 50 or more peak rooms, national rotation ability, and a two-year verifiable history. Track Scale Qualification highlights --- --- --- Corporate 400\\+ corporate event professionals Director, VP, or C-suite; six to seven figure budgets Association 300\\+ association event professionals 50\\+ rooms on peak Citywide - 87% manage \\$1M\\+ budgets; 1,000\\+ rooms or 100,000\\+ sq ft minimum Specialty 300\\+ specialty professionals Social, military, and fraternal group planners Sports 200\\+ sports event professionals Competitive sporting events and sports-themed meetings International (RTO) 150\\+ international tourism professionals Receptive tour operators booking inbound North America travel; up to \\$600 flight stipend per company Business Travel - 1 in 3 buyers controls \\$10M\\+ travel budgets; 80%\\+ manager level or above Diversity 100\\+ target community Faith-based, fraternal, nonprofit, education, LGBTQ\\+, multicultural, and women's group planners Informa - Informa PLC colleagues only; registration fees waived Buyer economics we quote to sponsors From the flagship Why Sponsor page: 61 percent of hosted buyers manage budgets over \\$250K, 31 percent manage \\$500K or more, 34 percent generate 1,000 or more room nights per event, 45 percent require 250 or more peak rooms, and 22 percent require 500 or more. Next How a deal moves through connect-prod: stages, New vs Existing, IOs, and Closed Won.","path":"/company/business-model"},{"title":"Connect Travel","sidebarTitle":"Connect Travel","description":"The Connect Travel brand and how its destination-marketing events differ from the core meetings marketplace.","headings":["Distinction from the Connect marketplace portfolio","Where to find the detail"],"body":"Connect Travel is the travel-sector brand within Connect Meetings & Events. Its documented live-event products are eTourism Marketplace and eTourism Summit . Portfolio hub: Events, Travel and Sports. Internal event facts: Connect Travel events. Public URLs: Public event sites. Distinction from the Connect marketplace portfolio Connect marketplace Connect Travel --- --- Recruits association, corporate, sports, specialty, and related event planners Recruits destination-marketing professionals and related travel-industry audiences Suppliers sell destinations, venues, hospitality, and event services Suppliers serve destination-marketing and tourism needs Core format is the reverse-tradeshow appointment marketplace Portfolio includes both a hosted marketplace and a paid summit Where to find the detail Published dates, venues, formats, prices, and unresolved source conflicts. How Travel appears in OpportunityLineItem.Organization c and related fields. Do not apply Connect hosted-buyer eligibility or package assumptions automatically to Connect Travel. Use the event-specific source and preserve the known eTourism conflicts.","path":"/company/connect-travel-brand"},{"title":"Connect Meetings & Events","sidebarTitle":"Connect Meetings & Events","description":"The core Connect marketplace brand, its audience, event model, and commercial role.","headings":["What the brand does","What belongs elsewhere"],"body":"Connect is the core appointment-event brand within Connect Meetings & Events. It operates the flagship and regional marketplace portfolio documented on this site. What the brand does - Recruits and qualifies event planners as hosted buyers. - Sells appointment packages and sponsorships to hospitality and event-industry suppliers. - Matches buyer sourcing needs to supplier capabilities before the event. - Runs education, networking, marketplace appointments, and onsite rebooking. What belongs elsewhere Topic Canonical Company Brain page --- --- Dates, venues, and event format Event calendar Flagship and regional portfolio Events overview Published packages Packages and pricing Supplier support process Exhibitor Support Hosted-buyer support process Hosted Buyer Support Internal and Salesforce contexts normally use supplier . Customer-facing materials may use exhibitor . The distinction is documented in the Glossary.","path":"/company/connect"},{"title":"Mission and value proposition","sidebarTitle":"Mission and value proposition","description":"The verified operating proposition for suppliers, hosted buyers, and internal teams, without inventing a corporate mission statement.","headings":["The value exchange","What differentiates the model","Related pages"],"body":"No approved corporate mission statement is present in the current canonical source set. This page therefore documents the verified operating proposition , not a fabricated slogan. The value exchange Audience Value received Commitment or commercial exchange --- --- --- Suppliers Pre-scheduled access to qualified event planners, plus optional sponsorship and media reach Purchase appointment packages, sponsorships, or advertising Hosted buyers Event access, qualified supplier discovery, education, networking, and published travel or hotel coverage Meet the applicable eligibility criteria and complete the assigned appointment schedule Staff A repeatable commercial and operating model across events, media, and brands Protect source accuracy, customer commitments, data governance, and escalation boundaries What differentiates the model - Appointments are matched and scheduled before the event rather than left to floor traffic. - Hosted-buyer qualification protects the value of the supplier appointment package. - Events, media, sponsorships, and amplification provide complementary commercial routes. - Rebooking and existing business convert at materially higher rates than new-logo acquisition. The most important operating principle is alignment: qualified buyers attend their appointments, suppliers receive the access sold to them, and staff communicate only verified terms. Related pages The detailed commercial mechanics and source-dated package examples. How staff apply the proposition when supporting suppliers.","path":"/company/mission-value"},{"title":"Who we are","sidebarTitle":"Who we are","description":"Informa Connect, Connect Meetings & Events, and where this business sits inside Informa PLC.","headings":["Where we sit","What the business does","Events","Media and advertising","Customers","Scale markers","Where the business is run from","Next"],"body":"Connect Meetings & Events is the business unit this knowledge base documents. It sits inside Informa Connect, which sits inside Informa PLC. Where we sit Informa PLC is the parent group. Informa Connect is the division. Connect Meetings & Events is our business unit inside that division, and it is what this Company Brain documents. The reporting line into the group runs through Andrew Mullins , an Informa executive external to Connect, to Matt Johnson , Managing Director, who owns Connect Meetings & Events end to end. When an event site says \"operated by Informa Connect Division of Informa PLC\", that is us. Customer-facing material uses the Connect brand; internal systems and email use @informa.com . What the business does Two commercial engines run side by side. Events Marketplace-style appointment events across the United States. Suppliers buy appointment packages and sponsorships; planners attend as sponsored hosted buyers. Nine Connect marketplace and sports events run in the 2026-2027 season, anchored by Connect Marketplace in Tampa, plus two Connect Travel destination-marketing events (eTourism Marketplace and eTourism Summit) and BizBash Innovation Forum. Public URL index: Public event sites. Media and advertising Digital, print, and email advertising sold through BizBash, TSNN, and Connect Travel, plus event amplification products attached to event packages. Managed in AdBook and synced to Salesforce. Customers The sales model is supplier-side . We sell to the people who want to be found, and we recruit the people doing the finding. Side Who they are What they do with us --- --- --- Suppliers (paying customers) CVBs and DMOs, hotels and resorts, convention centres and venues, AV and production companies, event technology vendors, airlines, gifting and merchandise companies, experiential agencies Buy appointment packages, sponsorships, booths, and advertising Buyers (recruited, mostly sponsored) Association, corporate, sports, specialty, and international event planners Attend as hosted buyers, take pre-scheduled appointments, source destinations and services In Salesforce both sides live on the Account object and are separated by Type and Primary Category c . Supplier accounts are identified by Primary Category c LIKE 'Supplier-%' - roughly 13,800 accounts across 29 supplier categories. Scale markers These are the figures published on our own event sites and derived from connect-prod . Treat published marketing figures as approximate; treat CRM figures as point-in-time. Marker Figure Source --- --- --- Attendees at flagship Connect Marketplace 3,000\\+ MICE professionals Event site, 2026 Actively sourcing buyers at flagship 900\\+ (Why Attend page) or 1,100\\+ (Why Sponsor page) Event site - the two figures conflict, see Known issues Suppliers at flagship 1,800\\+ solution-ready suppliers Event site, 2026 Supplier accounts in CRM ~13,800 across 29 categories connect-prod Supplier deals closed won since Jan 2025 492 deals, \\$7.85M, average ~\\$16K connect-prod opportunity analysis Supplier win rate ~78% won versus lost; ~69% including expired connect-prod Renewal versus new business Renewals 84.6% win at ~\\$18.8K average; new logos 67.2% win at ~\\$10.1K connect-prod Inbound versus outbound win rate 77.9% inbound versus 71.5% outbound connect-prod The renewal numbers are the single most load-bearing fact about this business: existing business converts at a materially higher rate and at nearly twice the deal size. Rebooking is the revenue engine, not new logo acquisition. Where the business is run from Location Who is there --- --- US Norcross - 3740 Davinci Court Leadership, most of Sales, Events, Operations, part of Marketing US Norcross - 15 Technology Parkway South Event Experience Sales US Chicago - 300 S. Riverside Plaza Strategic Director US Home (remote) Sales, Marketing, Customer Success, contractors and freelancers Next How the reverse tradeshow, hosted buyer programme, and supplier packages make money.","path":"/company/overview"},{"title":"Revenue and renewals","sidebarTitle":"Renewals and existing business","description":"How revenue is represented, why renewals are commercially important, and which fields staff should use.","headings":["Revenue engines","Why renewals matter","Salesforce representation"],"body":"Revenue is booked through event packages, sponsorships, and media. Most of it comes from existing accounts. Revenue engines Connect Meetings & Events earns revenue through event appointment packages, sponsorships, media and advertising, event amplification, and related registration or service lines. The detailed product categories live on Business and revenue model. Why renewals matter The current connect-prod analysis shows that Existing Business converts at a materially higher rate and larger average value than New Business. These are point-in-time CRM findings, not permanent targets. Opportunity type Observed win rate Observed average --- --- --- Existing Business 84.6% Approximately \\$18.8K New Business 67.2% Approximately \\$10.1K Rebooking is a core commercial workflow. It is not merely an onsite convenience. Salesforce representation - Opportunity.Type distinguishes New Business and Existing Business . - Product Year c is the year the product is for, not necessarily the close-date year. - OpportunityLineItem.Organization c is the reliable business-unit split. - Cash reporting excludes Barter c = true . - Historical analysis must account for edits after Closed Won. Use Metrics and definitions for current query definitions and Sales process for lifecycle detail. Do not quote these aggregate findings as a forecast for a named account or event. Query the live org for current operational decisions.","path":"/company/revenue-renewals"},{"title":"Sales process","sidebarTitle":"","description":"How a Connect deal moves: two sales processes, New vs Existing, IOs, and what happens after Closed Won.","headings":["What we sell, and to whom","Two processes, one picklist","How a deal is built","After Closed Won","Renewals and rebooking","What agents must not do from this page","Next"],"body":"This page is the staff view of how we sell. Field names and SOQL live on the data model and metrics. The Canonical register entry is Sales process and business processes (Andrew Dysart, quarterly), pointing at the Salesforce Knowledge Base catalogue. That catalogue currently has zero rows . Until it is populated, this page is grounded on the Record Types & Business Processes note and the Customer FAQs. Do not treat this page as a substitute for the empty Business Processes catalogue. Inferences on the Record Types page are labelled as such in the source. Do not promote them to policy. What we sell, and to whom The model is supplier-side . We sell to hospitality and travel suppliers. We recruit planners as hosted buyers. See Business model. In Salesforce a deal is an Opportunity . Every Opportunity carries IO Number c (Insertion Order). Product detail lives on OpportunityLineItem , not on the Opportunity header. Opportunity Product Type c is 100% null — do not analyse it. Brand / business unit on the line: Organization c = Meetings, Travel, BizBash, TSNN. Product category on the line: Digital, Event, Event Amplification, Special Projects. Two processes, one picklist Record types are used sparingly. Differentiation is mostly picklists. The StageName picklist holds two merged sales processes that share terminal stages. Process (as inferred in the Salesforce KB) Open stages Shared close --- --- --- Event / marketplace Prospecting → Interested → Proposal → Verbal Closed Won, Closed Lost, Expired Media / advertising Made Contact & Set Meeting → Proposal Creation (BANT Known) → Proposal Sent → Negotiation Closed Won, Closed Lost, Expired Stages that sit across both and matter commercially: Stage Role --- --- Previous Year Purchase Renewal pipeline. Existing suppliers being worked for the next product year. MSA Signed / SOW Signed Contract-focused, typically larger deals. Closed Won Booked. IsClosed = true , IsWon = true . Expired Timed out without a decision. Closed but not lost. Exclude from win-rate denominators. Closed Lost Lost. Reason Lost c is blank on 421 of 424 lost deals — do not expect a structured reason. The full stage ladder, including IsClosed / IsWon , is on the data model. Opportunity.Type — New Business vs Existing Business — is the most predictive commercial field in the org. Renewals win at 84.6% and average around \\$18.8K; new logos win at 67.2% and average around \\$10.1K. Rebooking is the engine. How a deal is built Supplier accounts: Primary Category c LIKE 'Supplier-%' . Territory is derived from BillingState , not a territory object. Do not assume a BizBash advertiser account has an owner. IO Number c is required and unique. CloseDate is required. Set Type (New vs Existing) correctly — it drives every renewal analysis. Appointment packages, sponsorships, media, amplification. Product Year c is the year the product is for , not the close-date year. A December 2026 close can be product year 2027. From the Customer FAQ library: agreements are sent and signed via DocuSign; once signed, participation or the campaign is treated as secured and fulfilment begins. After signature, suppliers often receive an \"Action Required: Attendee Details\" DocuSign for who is attending. Not every opportunity uses AdBook. Check AB2 Order Created c = true before relying on any AB2 field. Cash at line level is AB2 ABPayType c = 'PaidFor' . Salesforce Opportunities are the source of booked revenue. FinancialForce holds invoices with limited field access — prefer Opportunity billing fields ( Billing Terms c , Invoice Number c , Opportunity Paid In Full c ). Customer-facing payment questions: see the FAQ on invoices (BizBashPayNow / Square). Do not quote a specific amount or schedule. After Closed Won Amount can be edited after the deal is won. For a historically accurate figure check Change Made After Closed Won c and Initial Close Won Date c . Cash revenue excludes Barter c = true . Default reporting filters: Account Status c != 'Inactive' , StageName NOT IN ('Expired', 'Closed Lost') , IsDeleted = false . Renewals and rebooking Existing Business is worked from Previous Year Purchase and from onsite rebooking. The business-model page records a dedicated rebooking lounge at the event. There is no separate \"renewal object\" — it is the same Opportunity model with Type = Existing Business and the next Product Year c . Use Account spend rollups ( Total Spend This Product Year c and the five prior years) rather than re-aggregating opportunities. The dlrs package maintains them. What agents must not do from this page Ask Do --- --- A specific rate, discount, or expired promo Escalate to the account's sales owner. FAQ confidence on discounts is 0.70 and marked \"when not to use\". Payment terms, installments, invoice amounts Escalate to Stephen Han (Finance). Do not quote terms from this site. Contract changes, T&Cs, legal Escalate to Legal routing. Full attendee or buyer contact list Brand-and-title samples only, after a package is secured. Which stage a named deal is in Query connect-prod . This site does not hold record data. Sales owners: Connect marketplace — Suzi Schnell. BizBash — Michela Giovannotto. Media and travel — Andrew Dysart. Next Connect, BizBash, TSNN, and Connect Travel — how each one shows up in Salesforce.","path":"/company/sales-process"},{"title":"Company structure","sidebarTitle":"","description":"The organisational relationship between Informa PLC, Informa Connect, Connect Meetings & Events, its brands, and its operating teams.","headings":["Reporting and operating structure","How to navigate the organisation"],"body":"Connect Meetings & Events sits in the Informa group alongside its brands and operating teams. This page is the structure map. Reporting and operating structure The four brands share the connect-prod Salesforce org and a common operating organisation. Brand is therefore not a reliable single-field partition in reporting. How to navigate the organisation Need Use --- --- Understand business areas Departments Route a decision or exception Who owns what Understand reporting lines Organisation chart Find a staff contact Staff directory Split commercial data by brand OpportunityLineItem.Organization c , documented on Brands Ownership and reporting are different. Use Who owns what for decisions and the Organisation chart for management lines. Do not infer one from the other.","path":"/company/structure"},{"title":"TSNN","sidebarTitle":"","description":"What tsnn.com actually is: trade-show news, newsletters, Top 250, TSNN Awards, and how the public site disagrees with the 2025 media kit.","headings":["What the site is","Product stack","TSNN Awards 2026","Fifty Over 50","Team as published","What not to create from this site","Next"],"body":"TSNN is Trade Show News Network , the exhibition-industry news brand inside Connect Meetings & Events. The public site is tsnn.com. Captured 12 August 2026 . About Us: world's leading news and online resource for the trade show, exhibition and event industry since 1996 . Owned by Informa. Longest-running media brand dedicated to the trade show industry. Group content: Danica Tormohlen, VP of Group Content. Company Brain previously treated TSNN as a one-line media partner plus a 2025 rate card. The website is a full news product: daily editorial, a newsletter suite, a Top 250 ranking, annual awards, a trade-show calendar, podcasts, and an advertiser kit that is still labelled 2025 . Rate-card dollars stay on Media and amplification. This page is the brand. What the site is Daily digital coverage for show organisers, exhibitors, and suppliers. Nav verticals on capture: Vertical URL --- --- Event planning tsnn.com/trade-shows-conferences/event-planning Event management tsnn.com/trade-shows-conferences/event-management Event tech tsnn.com/trade-shows-conferences/event-tech Exhibitors tsnn.com/trade-shows-conferences/exhibitors Trade show marketing tsnn.com/trade-shows-conferences/trade-show-marketing Event design tsnn.com/trade-shows-conferences/event-design Sustainability tsnn.com/sustainability Venues and destinations tsnn.com/venues-destinations People and career tsnn.com/people-career-development Commentary tsnn.com/latest-commentary About Us also lists daily news, a monthly podcast, TSNN Top 250 Trade Shows, Experts & Opinions, and a newsletter suite. Product stack As listed on About, Advertise, Top 250, Awards, and the calendar pages. Capture 12 August 2026. Surface What it is Where money is documented --- --- --- Daily site Editorial and sponsored coverage Media kit The Industry This Week Weekly Tuesday newsletter. Advertise page: 10,000+ subscribers 2025 kit: 13K event professionals, \\$4,000. Not named in the 2026 kit extract Breaking News At least three times a month. Advertise page: 12,000+ subscribers Not priced separately in the 2025 kit Association Show News 1st and 3rd Thursdays. Advertise page: 8,000+ subscribers 2026 kit: \\$4,000; more than 8,000 subscribers. 2025 kit named it without a separate price ExpoFiles News Monthly, 2nd Thursday. Advertise page: 25,000 subscribers Not in the 2025 kit by this name People News / Tech News Named on the Advertise page as part of the newsletter suite Not itemised in the 2025 kit Podcasts Monthly per About; Show & Grow Marketing Playbook series live on capture Not in the 2025 kit TSNN Top 250 Annual US ranking by net square footage, attendance, exhibitors, location. Latest analysis on the site: 21 May 2026 Not a rate-card product Trade show calendar Advertise as 4,000+ B2B exhibitions. Hosted at thetradeshowcalendar.com/tsnn Featured listings sold via Advertise TSNN Awards 16th annual in 2026. See below Sponsorships via Andrew Dysart; no public price Media kit Advertise still offers \"the 2025 TSNN Media Kit\". A 2026 Events \\ Travel \\ Sports PDF is now filed Source-dated products on Media and amplification About Us claims more than 160,000 registered website users, more than 140,000 monthly newsletter subscribers, more than 10,000 LinkedIn group members, and 10,000-plus Twitter followers. The Advertise page restates 160,000 website visitors and subscribers , 30,000 Twitter reach via TSNN Rachel and TSNN com US , and 10,000 LinkedIn members. The 2025 Events \\ Travel \\ Sports kit cites 53K monthly users , 175K monthly views , and 42.9K newsletter subscribers for TSNN, and prices The Industry This Week at 13K . Do not blend these. Logged on Known issues. TSNN Awards 2026 From the 3 August 2026 nominations article. Milestone Published date --- --- Nominations open Monday 3 August 2026 Entries close Wednesday 30 September 2026 Finalists Thursday 15 October 2026 Ceremony Monday 16 November 2026, Harley-Davidson Museum, Milwaukee, during IAEE Expo! Expo! Third consecutive year co-located with IAEE as a dual awards programme. Nominations are open to US trade and consumer show professionals for events held 16 October 2025–30 September 2026. The article lists 2025 winners across Industry Icon, show-of-the-year, design, engagement, exhibitor programmes, marketing, tech, DEI, giving back, and sustainability. Limited sponsorships: contact Andrew Dysart. Fifty Over 50 Connect Marketplace runs a TSNN Fifty Over 50 reception. That is documented on the Marketplace page and the 2026 sponsorship deck. A dedicated Fifty Over 50 explainer was not found on tsnn.com during the 12 August 2026 capture. On 27 August 2026 the live site linked a 2026 series article, Part six: Meet the 2026 Event Industry 50 Over 50. That is editorial coverage, not an eligibility explainer. Do not invent a submission URL from it. Public brand URLs: tsnn.com · About · Advertise and editorial calendar · trade-show calendar · thetradeshowcalendar.com/tsnn · TSNN Awards · 2025 Events \\ Travel \\ Sports kit Turtl. Event homepage index: Public event sites. Team as published Two public rosters. They do not match each other, and they do not fully match Master People. Surface Published --- --- About Us Editorial: Danica Tormohlen, Abby Ramsey. Sales: John Rice , Sales & Business Development, 617-201-7088. Marketing: Jordan Bouldin. Ad production: Orlon Daniels. Office: 15 Technology Parkway South, Suite 250, Norcross, Georgia 30092 Advertise Contact John Rice, VP of Sales Contact Us Office: 300 S. Riverside Plaza, Suite 1600, Chicago, IL 60606 , 312.876.0004. Business: Kimberly Collie, Matt Johnson, Andrew Dysart. Sales: Chris Conwell, VP, Group Media — John Rice is not listed. Editorial: Danica Tormohlen plus contributing writers (Loren Edelstein, Michele Lautik , Shannon Cherry, Claire Hoffman, Matt Swenson). Social: Abby Ramsey. Marketing: Jordan Bouldin. Graphics: Clint Poy. Production: Orlon Daniels, Customer Success Manager Directory / Master People Conflict --- --- John Rice: Contractor ,","path":"/company/tsnn"},{"title":"BizBash Innovation Forum","sidebarTitle":"","description":"The three-day BizBash conference at Tampa Convention Center, 23-25 August 2026, the day before Connect Marketplace.","headings":["Who it is for","How it is not Sports Innovation Forum","What was not on the site","Next"],"body":"23-25 August 2026 - Tampa Convention Center, Tampa Bay, FL. Three days. Application-based. No public package price on the live site. Captured 12 August 2026 from informaconnect.com/bizbash-innovation-forum. Public URLs recaptured 27 August 2026 . This is the missing BizBash live event. It is a conference — case studies, masterclasses, panels — not a reverse tradeshow. It occupies the same building as Connect Marketplace 2026, which opens the next morning (24-26 August). Badge pickup starts 13:00 on 23 August . Plan Your Visit links a \"Regiception\" page, same vocabulary as the Connect events. The other two BizBash live events remain on Sports and summits. Who it is for Application-only passes, published on How to Attend. Standard passes have no published price . Premium passes say contact Michela Giovannotto. Pass Reserved for What the page says is included --- --- --- Brand Experience Pass In-house brand event and marketing professionals only All sessions; brand-side networking; access to solution providers Standard Supplier Pass Event-industry suppliers All sessions; logo on the event website; networking with in-house event leaders Independent Producer Pass Sole proprietor, LLC, or independent contractor, not full-time at a brand, agency, venue, or supplier All sessions; networking with brands, agencies, and other independents Prime / Elite / Premier Sold as sponsorship One registration plus a commercial slot, panel seat, or sponsored case study. Logo on the site. Attendee list (names, companies, emails) emailed two weeks after the event Agents must still never share an attendee or buyer contact list. The Premier-tier \"attendee list\" is a sold sponsorship deliverable, not something a chatbot may quote, preview, or invent. How it is not Sports Innovation Forum The How to Attend and Plan Your Visit meta descriptions still talk about \"sports industry leaders\" and \"sports marketing and event professionals.\" The Speakers page meta says \"August 25-27 in Miami.\" The hero, venue block, and Plan Your Visit body are 23-25 August, Tampa Convention Center . Preserve both. Logged on Known issues. The pass names (Brand Experience, Independent Producer, Prime / Elite / Premier) are the same pattern as Sports Innovation Forum, which is why the leftover sports copy is easy to miss. What was not on the site - No published dollar prices for Brand, Supplier, or Independent passes - Speakers page rendered empty on 12 August 2026 - Agenda exists as a URL; this page does not reproduce session times Public resources: How to attend · Agenda · Day 1 · Day 2 · Day 3 · Speakers. Brand home: bizbash.com. More URLs on Public event sites. Sponsorship and pricing: Michela Giovannotto, or bizbash.sales@informa.com . Next Sponsorships, media, and event amplification sold against the calendar.","path":"/events/bizbash"},{"title":"Event calendar","sidebarTitle":"Event calendar","description":"Every live Connect event for the 2026-2027 season with dates, venues, and format.","headings":["The season at a glance","The shape of a Connect event","Recurring operational deadlines","Standing commitments across all events","How events appear in Salesforce","Next"],"body":"Twelve live events run across the 2026-2027 season: nine Connect marketplace and sports events, two Connect Travel destination-marketing events, and BizBash Innovation Forum. The Live Events wiki in Notion is the canonical source for every fact on this page; customer-service agents answer only from these records. Calendar captured from informaconnect.com and bizbash.com on 12 August 2026 . Public homepages and resource URLs recaptured 27 August 2026 — see Public event sites. Those marketing URLs are Working pointers, not Canonical event facts. The season at a glance Event Dates Location Venue Type Public site --- --- --- --- --- --- BizBash Innovation Forum 2026 23-25 Aug 2026 Tampa, FL Tampa Convention Center BizBash conference informaconnect.com/bizbash-innovation-forum Connect Marketplace 2026 24-26 Aug 2026 Tampa, FL Tampa Convention Center Flagship marketplace informaconnect.com/connect-marketplace The Women's Summit 2026 27-28 Oct 2026 The Woodlands, TX Woodlands Waterway Marriott Summit (add-on track) informaconnect.com/the-womens-summit Connect Texas \\+ Medical 2026 28-29 Oct 2026 The Woodlands, TX Woodlands Waterway Marriott Regional marketplace informaconnect.com/connect-texas Sports Innovation Forum 2026 28-30 Oct 2026 The Woodlands, TX Woodlands Waterway Marriott Innovation forum informaconnect.com/sports-innovation-forum Connect West 2026 4-5 Nov 2026 Scottsdale, AZ The Westin Kierland Resort and Spa Regional marketplace informaconnect.com/connect-west Connect South \\+ Faith 2026 9-10 Nov 2026 Branson, MO Branson Convention Center Regional marketplace informaconnect.com/connect-south Connect Winter Marketplace 2026 18-20 Nov 2026 Palm Beach, FL Palm Beach County Convention Center Regional marketplace informaconnect.com/connect-winter-marketplace eTourism Marketplace 18-20 Nov (year not on hero) Palm Beach, FL Hilton West Palm Beach Connect Travel marketplace informaconnect.com/etourism-marketplace Connect North 2027 22-24 Feb 2027 Cincinnati, OH - Regional marketplace informaconnect.com/connect-north Spring Marketplace 2027 21-23 Apr 2027 Las Vegas, NV The Wynn Flagship marketplace informaconnect.com/connect-spring-marketplace eTourism Summit 2027 21-23 Apr 2027 Las Vegas, NV Wynn Las Vegas Connect Travel conference informaconnect.com/etourism-summit Two venue clusters. Tampa Convention Center hosts BizBash Innovation Forum on 23-25 August and Connect Marketplace on 24-26 August. The Woodlands Waterway Marriott hosts Women's Summit, Sports Innovation Forum, and Connect Texas \\+ Medical in late October. The Women's Summit runs as an add-on track inside Sports Innovation Forum rather than as a separately ticketed event, and their published date ranges overlap only on 28 October. The shape of a Connect event Nearly every event follows the same three-beat structure, which is worth internalising because it drives the whole operational calendar. Regiception (combined registration and reception), a first-timer orientation at larger events, a welcome education session, a first block of marketplace appointments, and an opening reception hosted by the destination. Breakfast and education, a morning appointment block, lunch and learn, an afternoon appointment block, then an open night in the host city. This is where most of the business gets done. Grab-and-go breakfast, a final appointment block, and departures. Flagships add a closing concert or the annual Connect 5K. Two-day regional events compress this into days one and two. Flagship events extend it with tradeshow experiences, speaker showcases, hosted meals, and a rebooking lounge. Recurring operational deadlines Deadline type Pattern Examples --- --- --- Supplier hotel room block closes 3-4 weeks before the event Connect Marketplace 3 Aug; Connect Texas 5 Oct; Connect West 14 Oct; Connect South 16 Oct; Winter Marketplace 27 Oct Standard cancellation cutoff 57 days before Refund minus 10% administration charge; nothing refunded inside 56 days Hosted buyer cancellation cutoff 30 days before Full refund or credit at 30\\+ days; inside 7 days, no refund plus one hotel night fee Hosted buyer deposit \\$200, refundable Returned on completing the assigned appointment schedule Two different cancellation policies are published on nearly every event site - one on the Hosted Buyers page, one in the Terms & Conditions. They almost certainly apply to different registrant types, but no site states that explicitly. Never quote one without checking which registrant you are talking to. Logged on Known issues. Standing commitments across all events - Sustainability : reusable water bottles via Fill it Forward, onsite recycling, eco-friendly and locally sourced food, food donation to local charities, quiet and prayer rooms, recyclable signage, carpet reuse at flagships. - Code of conduct : zero tolerance for harassment, discrimination, or violence at any live or digital touchpoint, covering attendees, speakers, sponsors, exhibitors, and staff. - Governing law : New York, per Terms & Conditions, regardless of where the event is held. - Media partners : BizBash, TSNN, and Corporate Event News appear across the portfolio. How events appear in Salesforce Concept Object or field --- --- A specific event instance Marketplace c - roughly 10 to 15 per year, with Event Type c , Start Date c , End Date c , Event Status c A hosted buyer registration Registration c , linked to Contact, Account, Campaign, and Marketplace c Registration lifecycle Registration Stage c : Registering, Pending Confirmation, Registered, Fully Registered, Cancelled, Declined, Verbal, Stopped Event revenue attribution OpportunityLineItem.Event Name c and boolean flags such as IsSpringMarketplaceProduct c Registration counts Rollup fields on Marketplace c such as Fully Registered Count c Fully Registered is the only stage that means confirmed. Registering and Pending Confirmation are not attendance. Next Homepages, VisitCloud registration, agendas, maps, hotel blocks, and Turtl decks. Co","path":"/events/calendar/overview"},{"title":"Flagship marketplaces","sidebarTitle":"Flagship marketplaces","description":"Connect Marketplace Tampa 2026 and Spring Marketplace Las Vegas 2027 - the two flagship events.","headings":["Connect Marketplace 2026","Scale","The six tracks","Attendance and pricing","Rhythm","Sponsors","Contacts and policy","Public site and resources","Spring Marketplace 2027","Tracks","Signature moments","Event amplification pricing","Sponsors","Public site and resources","Next"],"body":"Two events carry the flagship designation. They are materially larger than the regionals, run three days rather than two, and are the only ones with full track structures, speaker programmes, and published sponsorship rosters. Connect Marketplace 2026 24-26 August 2026 (Monday to Wednesday) - Tampa Convention Center, Tampa Bay, FL - US Eastern time. Tagline: Where Events Business Gets Done . Value proposition: High-Value Connections. Zero Wasted Time. Public site: informaconnect.com/connect-marketplace. The flagship. 3,000\\+ MICE professionals across three days of curated meetings, networking, and education. Host destination is Visit Tampa Bay. Closing night concert is the DJ duo Two Friends. BizBash Innovation Forum occupies the same building on 23-25 August , opening the afternoon before Marketplace. It is a BizBash conference, not a Marketplace track. Forum homepage: informaconnect.com/bizbash-innovation-forum. Scale Measure Figure --- --- Total professionals 3,000\\+ Actively sourcing buyers 900\\+ (Why Attend) or 1,100\\+ (Why Sponsor) - the two pages conflict Suppliers 1,800\\+ solution-ready Exhibitor exposure 2,000\\+ event decision-makers including C-suite Confirmed speakers 23 across four session formats Education Up to 14-15 hours of CMP-credit content The six tracks Track Scale Who qualifies --- --- --- Corporate 400\\+ Corporate meeting planners. Example organisations: Cisco, DHL, Google, Mastercard, NHL, PNC, PWC, Subway Association 300\\+ Association meeting planners. Example organisations: American Psychological Association, CollegeBoard, Habitat for Humanity, National Kidney Foundation Specialty 300\\+ (75\\+ planners) Social, military, and fraternal group planners Sports 200\\+ Competitive sporting events and sports-themed meetings. Examples: American Junior Golf Association, Spartan Race, USA Pickleball, USSSA, Varsity Spirit International 150\\+ Receptive tour operators booking inbound North America travel. Up to \\$600 flight stipend per company Informa - Informa PLC colleagues only, registration fees waived General buyer minimums across tracks: 50 or more peak rooms, ability to rotate nationally, and a two-year verifiable event history. Attendance and pricing - Up to 45 marketplace appointments with qualified suppliers - Roundtrip airfare, three nights hotel, and meals covered - \\$200 refundable deposit, returned on completing the assigned appointments - 10\\+ hours curated networking, 15\\+ hours education - Application takes about ten minutes, then Hosted Buyer Experience Team review, then pairing with a dedicated contact - Eligibility categories: Association, Corporate, International, Specialty, Sports Package Price Appointments --- --- --- Deal Closer \\$5,950 40, top five requests guaranteed, preferred listing, 15 available Elite Connecter \\$5,250 30, marked most popular, 35 available Growth Accelerator \\$4,850 24 Ice Breaker \\$4,250 12 - Buyer Power Pass \\$350 - planners and buyers only, no pre-scheduled meetings - Supplier Power Pass \\$1,950 - no pre-scheduled meetings Rhythm - Appointment blocks : 25 and 26 August, 10:00-12:00 and 15:00-17:00 each day - Tradeshow experiences : 24 August 13:00-16:00; 25-26 August 12:00-15:00 - Signature moments : Discover the Bay opening reception hosted by Visit Tampa Bay; TSNN Fifty Over 50 reception; Women in Sports Waterfront Walk; annual Connect 5K Run and Walk on day three; closing night concert - MPAC (Meeting Professional Audiovisual Certificate) training runs onsite; required sessions Mon 24 Aug 10:00-12:00 and Tue 25 Aug 12:45-13:45 Sponsors Host is Visit Tampa Bay. Thirty named 2026 sponsors including Experience Scottsdale, I LOVE NY, Kalahari Resorts, MatchPoint, Meetings Michigan, Reno Tahoe, Turning Stone, Travel Wisconsin, Visit Fort Worth, VisitLEX, Visit Kansas City, Visit Omaha, Visit San Antonio, Visit Dallas, Visit Milwaukee, Explore Branson, Visit Detroit, Visit Frisco, Arkansas Department of Tourism, Visit Anaheim, fielddrive, ChargeFuze, Map Your Show, Enjoy Illinois, PHLCVB, and GroupSync. Media partners are BizBash, TSNN, and Corporate Event News. Contacts and policy --- --- General inquiries mac.kitchin@informa.com Registration help suzi.schnell@informa.com Cancellations connect.cancellations@informa.com Supplier hotel block closes 3 August Cancellation Full refund minus 10% admin charge at 57\\+ days; no refund inside 56 days Public site and resources Captured 27 August 2026 . Full table on Public event sites. These URLs are Working marketing pointers. Resource URL --- --- Homepage connect-marketplace How to attend how-to-attend Hosted buyer application VisitCloud 3iyy5ec2sal59 Supplier package purchase VisitCloud 3qgcu41z4szt3 Agenda agenda · schedule at a glance Show map show-map · floor-plan PDF · Map Your Show Plan your visit plan-your-visit Event app event-app 2026 sponsorship deck Turtl connect-marketplace-2026 2025 media kit Turtl Events \\ Travel \\ Sports Why sponsor / exhibit why-sponsor · why-exhibit Supplier hotel block idem.events/r/connect-marketplace-supplier Connect 5K Formstack Hosted buyer T&Cs (PDF) uploads PDF Marketing toolkit marketing-toolkit Three unresolved discrepancies on this event: the buyer count differs between the Why Attend and Why Sponsor pages, CMP hours are cited as both 14 and 15, and a previously published \"9-minute appointment\" duration could not be verified anywhere on the current live site and has been removed pending confirmation with the events team. --- Spring Marketplace 2027 21-23 April 2027 - The Wynn, Las Vegas, Nevada. Over 2,000 event professionals. Public site: informaconnect.com/connect-spring-marketplace. The spring flagship. Keynote is Jesse Itzler (Marquis Jet, Zico, co-owner of the Atlanta Hawks). Eleven speakers confirmed at time of capture with more expected, including Adam Sacks of Tourism Economics, Lisa Messina of Visit Orlando, Mike Satterfield of HelmsBriscoe, and Ken Holsinger of Freeman. eTourism Summit 2027 publishes the same dates at the same hotel. S","path":"/events/connect-marketplace"},{"title":"Connect Travel events","sidebarTitle":"Connect Travel events","description":"eTourism Marketplace and eTourism Summit - the Connect Travel destination-marketing events listed under Events, Travel and Sports.","headings":["eTourism Marketplace 2026","How to attend","Scale claims on the live site","Hosted DMO cancellation","Contacts","Public site and resources","eTourism Summit 2027","How to attend","Sponsors (2027 site)","How these relate to the Connect calendar","Next"],"body":"The Events, Travel and Sports hub on informaconnect.com is a topic portal, not a single calendar. It splits into four verticals. Meetings and Sports are already covered on the regional and sports pages. Destination Marketing and Strategy is the vertical that was missing from this site: two Connect Travel events, owned by Ashleigh Trout. Captured from the live event sites on 12 August 2026 . Public URLs recaptured 27 August 2026 — see Public event sites. These are not hosted-buyer marketplaces for event planners. The hosted audience is destination marketers (DMOs and CVBs). Both events sit on the same dates as a Connect marketplace flagship or regional, at a neighbouring or identical venue. The sites do not say \"co-located\" in so many words. The registration copy and hotel-block URLs do. eTourism Marketplace 2026 18-20 November - Hilton West Palm Beach, Palm Beach. The hero does not print a year. Logo assets, the 27 October supplier room-block close, and the hotel booking URL ( connect-winter-supplier ) all match Connect Winter Marketplace 2026. Public site: informaconnect.com/etourism-marketplace. An appointment marketplace for destination marketers and the suppliers who sell to them - mar-tech, ad-tech, data, agencies, and other travel-marketing vendors. Format is the reverse-tradeshow mechanic aimed at DMOs rather than event planners: AI-matched nine-minute one-to-ones, education, and networking. How to attend Path Who What is covered Published price --- --- --- --- Hosted Destination Marketer DMO, director-level or above, or with direct purchasing authority. Not an agency, media, or mar-tech/ad-tech supplier. Complimentary roundtrip travel, hotel, waived registration. Up to 35 one-to-ones with travel-industry suppliers. Deposit typically \\$99-\\$200, refunded after completing the assigned appointments. \\$0 once approved Travel industry supplier Mar-tech, ad-tech, or other vendor whose primary business is selling a service to brands and businesses. Up to 35 one-to-ones with DMO leaders, education, networking. Speed-dating format. \\$6,250 Supplier hotel room block closes 27 October . Same close date as Connect Winter Marketplace. Scale claims on the live site Source page Figure --- --- How to Attend / homepage \"a select group of 50 top DMOs\" Who Attends \" 75 of the top DMO leaders\" and \" 75 high-performing solution providers\" Both versions are live. Do not pick one. Logged on Known issues. Hosted DMO cancellation Published on the Hosted DMO programme page. Same shape as the hosted-buyer policy on Connect events: - 30+ days, before transport is booked: full refund or credit to a later event - 30 days or fewer, transport booked: deposit may not be refunded; cancel transport through eTourism's Travel Services Manager - 7 days or fewer: no refund, plus one hotel night fee Contacts Published on the event contact page. Topic Person Address --- --- --- Sales and supplier Ashleigh Trout, VP eTourism ashleigh.trout@informa.com General inquiries and hosted DMO registrations Hannah Miller, Meetings Manager hannah.miller@informa.com Registration assistance Erden Mohl, Customer Success Specialist erden.mohl@informa.com Public site and resources Resource URL --- --- Homepage etourism-marketplace How to attend how-to-attend Hosted DMO programme dmo-leaders Agenda / schedule agenda · schedule at a glance Sponsors / advertise sponsors · advertise Marketing toolkit marketing-toolkit eTSY Awards etsy-awards How to Attend Register Now (both roles on that page) VisitCloud 0vuudv97bjcrg Second homepage VisitCloud CTA VisitCloud 2137bv9pn7avg Hotel block (shared with Winter Marketplace) idem.events/r/connect-winter-supplier The page meta description and the contact-page social description still say Washington, D.C. The hero, venue line, and body copy say Palm Beach / Hilton West Palm Beach . A recruiting widget on the Hosted DMO page still lists 21-23 October 2024 . The Open Graph description is leftover from an earlier city. Do not quote D.C. as the 2026 venue. eTourism Summit 2027 21-23 April 2027 - Wynn Las Vegas, Las Vegas, NV. Same dates and hotel as Spring Marketplace 2027. Public site: informaconnect.com/etourism-summit. A destination-marketing conference rather than an appointment marketplace. Positioning on the homepage: Where Innovation Meets Inspiration . \"300+ marketing innovators.\" Registration includes unlimited access to the Connect Spring Marketplace tradeshow floor , which is the strongest published evidence that the two events share a campus. It also runs the eTSY Awards and Emerging Tourism Stars programmes. How to attend Published on How to attend for the 2027 edition. Path Who Includes Published price --- --- --- --- Marketers Attractions, destinations (DMOs, CVBs, TDCs), hotels, state or regional travel associations Sessions, daily networking, event app, Spring Marketplace tradeshow floor Early bird \\$1,600 with code TRAV1 . Basic \\$1,895 Travel industry suppliers Marketing agencies, tech companies, and other travel-sector vendors Same, plus Spring Marketplace access across tracks Early bird \\$3,100 with code TSUM1 . Basic \\$3,495 These are paid conference badges, not hosted packages. Do not describe Summit attendees as hosted buyers. Sponsors (2027 site) Platinum: Ad+genuity, Steller, Ampersand, Atmosphere TV. Bronze: digilant. Sponsorship inquiries to ashleigh.trout@informa.com . The sponsors page rendered. The schedule-at-a-glance and contact-us pages returned Informa corporate boilerplate only on 12 August 2026 - treat the schedule as unpublished on those URLs. Agenda day pages: Day 1, Day 2, Day 3. Awards: awards, Emerging Tourism Stars. Registration on How to Attend: marketers VisitCloud 1b54fgl2er79w ; travel industry suppliers 1uj6ljoxh5tbw . Several 2026 Louisville leftovers are still live on the 2027 Las Vegas site. The homepage meta description says the industry is \"gathering in Louisville.\" The Destination Marketing hub CTA says both \"headed to Wynn Las Vegas in 2027\" and \"Joi","path":"/events/connect-travel"},{"title":"Events portfolio","sidebarTitle":"Portfolio overview","description":"How the Connect marketplace, specialty-event, Connect Travel, and BizBash event portfolios fit together.","headings":["Classification rules"],"body":"Use this page to choose the correct event family. Use the Event calendar for dates, venues, and event-specific operational facts. Public homepages, registration, agendas, maps, and decks are on Public event sites. Connect Marketplace and Spring Marketplace: the largest appointment-marketplace events. Texas, West, South + Faith, Winter Marketplace, and North. eTourism Marketplace and eTourism Summit for destination-marketing audiences. Sports Innovation Forum, Women's Summit, and related specialty programming. The BizBash event that overlaps Connect Marketplace in Tampa. Appointment packages, sponsorships, media, and event amplification associated with the portfolio. Live homepages, VisitCloud registration, agendas, maps, hotel blocks, and Turtl decks. Classification rules Dimension Use --- --- Brand Connect, Connect Travel, BizBash, or TSNN Format Marketplace, summit, forum, or programme track Audience Suppliers, hosted buyers, destination marketers, sports-event professionals, or media audiences Commercial route Appointment package, sponsorship, media, amplification, or registration Event dates, venues, eligibility, appointment counts, and cancellation policy come only from the event pages or the Live Events wiki they mirror. Do not reconstruct them from project pages, CRM names, or marketing snippets.","path":"/events/overview"},{"title":"Public event sites","sidebarTitle":"Public event sites","description":"Live homepages, registration, agendas, maps, hotel blocks, sponsorship decks, and media kits for Connect, BizBash, Connect Travel, and TSNN.","headings":["Brand and portfolio hubs","Event homepages","Connect Marketplace 2026 resources","Spring Marketplace 2027 resources","Regional marketplace resources","Connect Texas + Medical 2026","Connect West 2026","Connect South + Faith 2026","Connect Winter Marketplace 2026","Connect North 2027","Connect Travel resources","eTourism Marketplace","eTourism Summit 2027","Sports, Women's Summit, and BizBash Innovation Forum","Sports Innovation Forum 2026","The Women's Summit 2026","BizBash Innovation Forum 2026","Decks and kits (cross-brand)"],"body":"Staff lookup of public marketing URLs captured with Firecrawl on 27 August 2026 . Use this page to open the live site, a registration form, a floor map, or a deck. Do not treat these URLs as Canonical for dates, venues, eligibility, appointment counts, or cancellation policy. Event facts still come only from the calendar and the event pages it points to, or from the Live Events wiki those pages mirror. Public sites contradict themselves. Report both versions. Do not pick a winner. - Source status: Working. Public Informa Connect, BizBash, TSNN, VisitCloud, Turtl, and hotel-block pages. - Capture: 27 August 2026. Tracking parameters on VisitCloud URLs have been stripped. Query strings on Turtl and Formstack links were not required to load the resource. Brand and portfolio hubs Surface Public URL What it is --- --- --- Connect Meetings hub informaconnect.com/meetings-events-tradeshows Meetings, events, and tradeshows portal for the Connect portfolio Events listing informaconnect.com/meetings-events-tradeshows/events Hub events index Events, Travel and Sports informaconnect.com/events-travel-sports Topic portal covering Meetings, Sports, and Destination Marketing BizBash bizbash.com Events-media brand home BizBash About bizbash.com/about-us Public roster and audience claims BizBash events bizbash.com/events Live-event register-now listing Build Your Brand bizbash.com/build-your-brand-with-bizbash Advertiser intake; 2026 kit is behind this form Get Featured bizbash.com/get-featured Editorial pitch Publications bizbash.com/publications Print issues Webinars bizbash.com/webinars Live and on-demand BizBash directory directory.bizbash.com Supplier and venue listings TSNN tsnn.com Trade Show News Network home TSNN About tsnn.com/about-us Public roster and audience claims TSNN Advertise and editorial calendar tsnn.com/advertise-and-editorial-calendar Advertiser kit intake TSNN trade show calendar tsnn.com/trade-show-calendar On-site calendar page Trade show calendar (hosted) thetradeshowcalendar.com/tsnn TSNN-branded exhibition calendar TSNN Awards informaconnect.com/tsnn-awards Awards site linked from the Meetings hub 2026 Fifty Over 50 series (example article) Part six Found on tsnn.com on 27 August 2026. Not a dedicated eligibility explainer 2025 Events \\ Travel \\ Sports media kit Turtl story Cross-brand 2025 kit linked from Connect event nav as Advertise Newsletter subscribe (Connect): cloud.mail.connectmeetings.com/Subscribe. eTourism insights: cloud.mail.connectmeetings.com/InspiredInsights. BizBash Sports newsletter: cloud.mail.bizbash.com/SportsNewsletter. Event homepages Internal pages remain the staff source. Public homepages are for sending a customer or opening the live site. Event Internal page Public homepage --- --- --- Connect Marketplace 2026 Flagship informaconnect.com/connect-marketplace Spring Marketplace 2027 Flagship informaconnect.com/connect-spring-marketplace Connect Texas + Medical 2026 Regional informaconnect.com/connect-texas Connect West 2026 Regional informaconnect.com/connect-west Connect South + Faith 2026 Regional informaconnect.com/connect-south Connect Winter Marketplace 2026 Regional informaconnect.com/connect-winter-marketplace Connect North 2027 Regional informaconnect.com/connect-north eTourism Marketplace Connect Travel informaconnect.com/etourism-marketplace eTourism Summit 2027 Connect Travel informaconnect.com/etourism-summit Sports Innovation Forum 2026 Sports informaconnect.com/sports-innovation-forum The Women's Summit 2026 Sports informaconnect.com/the-womens-summit BizBash Innovation Forum 2026 Innovation Forum informaconnect.com/bizbash-innovation-forum Event Experience Awards (BizBash recognition programme, not a marketplace): informaconnect.com/bizbash-event-experience-awards. Connect Marketplace 2026 resources Captured from the live site crawl on 27 August 2026. Resource URL --- --- Homepage connect-marketplace How to attend how-to-attend Hosted buyer programme hosted-buyer-experience Hosted buyer application VisitCloud 3iyy5ec2sal59 Supplier package purchase VisitCloud 3qgcu41z4szt3 Buyer Power Pass Same hosted-buyer form 3iyy5ec2sal59 on How to Attend Supplier Power Pass Same supplier form 3qgcu41z4szt3 on How to Attend Agenda agenda · Day 1 · Day 2 · Day 3 Schedule at a glance schedule-at-a-glance Show map show-map Interactive tradeshow map connect2026.mapyourshow.com — linked from Show Map Tradeshow floor map (PDF) CNMP26 floor-plan PDF Plan your visit / Know Before You Go plan-your-visit Know Before You Go webinar Wistia Event app event-app Transportation guide transportation-guide Restaurant recommendations tampa-bay-restaurant-recommendations Visit Tampa Bay visit-tampa-bay ADA information ada-information FAQs faqs Marketing toolkit marketing-toolkit Why attend / who attends why-attend · who-attends Tracks tracks hub · Corporate · Association · Specialty · Sports · International · Informa International track form app.ingo.me/q/30apt Why sponsor why-sponsor 2026 sponsorship deck Turtl: connect-marketplace-2026 Why exhibit why-exhibit Event amplification event-amplification 2026 sponsors sponsors Speakers speakers Media partners media-partners Advertise / 2025 media kit Turtl 2025 kit Supplier hotel block idem.events/r/connect-marketplace-supplier MPAC onsite registration VisitCloud 3pzthpcog1ghz Connect 5K sign-up Formstack Two Friends closing-night recommend Formstack Spot My Photos myphotogallery.co Hosted buyer explainer Turtl hosted-buyer-program Convince-your-boss letter — hosted buyer (PDF) uploads PDF Convince-your-boss letter — supplier (PDF) uploads PDF Hosted buyer T&Cs (PDF) uploads PDF Event terms terms-and-conditions Contact contact-us · contact Sustainability sustainability Code of conduct informaconnect.com/code-of-conduct The 2026 sponsorship deck on Turtl is the public version of the inventory already summarised on Event sponsorships. The 2025 media kit on Turtl remains a public 2025 copy. Internal media starting prices are","path":"/events/public-sites"},{"title":"Regional marketplaces","sidebarTitle":"Regional marketplaces","description":"Connect Texas, West, South and Faith, Winter Marketplace, and Connect North.","headings":["Connect Texas \\+ Medical 2026","Connect West 2026","Connect South \\+ Faith 2026","Connect Winter Marketplace 2026","Connect North 2027","Next"],"body":"Five regional marketplaces run between October 2026 and February 2027. They share a common format - a compressed two- or three-day version of the flagship - and a common entry price of \\$4,250 for 12 supplier appointments . Their websites are flatter than the flagship sites, with no dedicated speakers, tracks, or why-exhibit pages. Homepages recaptured on 12 August 2026 . Dates, venues, and published prices were unchanged from the earlier crawl. Public URLs recaptured 27 August 2026 — see Public event sites. New facts: Connect South now labels Association Corporate Faith on the hero; Connect North now names Canada in the catchment; Winter Marketplace body copy still places the event in \"the nation’s capital.\" The Destination Marketing vertical on the same hub is documented separately under Connect Travel events. Connect Texas \\+ Medical 2026 28-29 October 2026 - The Woodlands Waterway Marriott Hotel & Convention Center, The Woodlands, TX. Two days. Public site: informaconnect.com/connect-texas. - Hosted buyer : complimentary. Roundtrip airfare, two nights hotel, all networking events, 3\\+ hours education, AI-matched appointments. Qualification: located in Texas, generates 50\\+ rooms on peak, able to rotate nationally or regionally. - Supplier : from \\$4,250 for 12 one-to-one appointments. - Club Connect : the points-based hosted buyer rewards programme, redeemable for priority seating, hospitality suite access, and airport-hotel transportation. - Sponsors : Visit The Woodlands (also hosts the opening night reception) and GroupSync. Sponsorship inquiries to derek.rodriguez@informa.com . - Room block closes : 5 October. - Public resources : How to attend · Agenda · Hosted buyers · Plan your visit · Sponsors · Marketing toolkit · supplier VisitCloud 1lnfom93345oj · hosted buyer 1xej4ozp2jlko · hotel idem.events/r/connect-texas-medical-attendee The Plan Your Visit page describes Frisco, TX as the destination while every other page says The Woodlands. This is leftover template content from a different Connect event and has not been corrected at source. Also note the \"\\+ Medical\" branding has no corresponding track or track page. Connect West 2026 4-5 November 2026 - The Westin Kierland Resort and Spa, Scottsdale, AZ. Two days. Hashtag connectwest . Public site: informaconnect.com/connect-west. - Hosted buyer : roundtrip airfare to Scottsdale, two nights hotel, opening reception and networking, 3\\+ hours education, up to 25 appointments. Qualification: located in Western states, 50\\+ rooms on peak, able to rotate. - Supplier : opening reception and networking, 3\\+ hours education, up to 25 appointments with qualified hosted buyers. - Appointments described as AI-powered and matched, in focused nine-minute meetings. - Signature moments : pre-conference adventures in Scottsdale, Regiception, \"Secret Stress Management Skills in the Sonoran Desert\" welcome session, closing cocktails. - Room block closes : 14 October. Badge pickup for returning attendees from 15:00 on 4 November. Photo service by Spot My Photos. - Public resources : How to attend · Agenda · Schedule at a glance · Hosted buyers · Plan your visit · Sponsors · Marketing toolkit · 2025 media kit Turtl · supplier VisitCloud 0oic42irthmc4 · hosted buyer 3m6t2ngy5j2rc · hotel idem.events/r/connect-west-suppliers. A prior-year Connect West 2025 Turtl story is still linked from the 2026 homepage. The sponsors page currently lists Visit Lake Tahoe as host with Lake Tahoe-area sponsors - almost certainly carried over from a prior year's Connect West held in South Lake Tahoe. Do not treat that list as the 2026 Scottsdale roster without verifying against the live site. Connect South \\+ Faith 2026 9-10 November 2026 - Branson Convention Center, Branson, MO. Two days. Public site: informaconnect.com/connect-south. - Homepage tracks : Association Corporate Faith. - Faith Track : a specialised pathway for planners of church retreats, ministry gatherings, conventions, and spiritual conferences. Qualification mirrors the general bar - events serving religious groups, 50\\+ rooms on peak, national rotation ability. Day two opens with a morning devotional. - Hosted buyer : roundtrip airfare to Branson, two-night hotel, all networking events, 3\\+ hours education, up to 45 appointments. - Supplier : from \\$4,250 for 12 appointments. - Sponsors : City of Beaumont, Durham CVB, Experience Montgomery, Explore Branson, Grand Wayne Convention Center, Greensboro CVB, Kalahari, pc/nametag, Spot My Photos, Visit Laredo Texas, Visit Plano, MatchPoint, Visit South Padre, Visit Frisco, Discover Durham, GroupSync. - Room block deadline : 16 October. Event amplification contact andrew.dysart@informa.com ; sponsorship to sales@connectmeetings.com . - Public resources : How to attend · Faith Track · Agenda · Hosted buyers · Plan your visit · Sponsors · Marketing toolkit · supplier VisitCloud 0tqqbogmce70d · hosted buyer 1wov2p873j78w · hotel idem.events/r/connect-south-faith-supplier The Faith Track page heading says 9-11 November while every other page and the actual agenda say 9-10 November. Two days is treated as canonical. The Plan Your Visit page describes Fort Worth, TX - stale content from a different event, not used here. Connect Winter Marketplace 2026 18-20 November 2026 - Palm Beach County Convention Center, Palm Beach, FL. Three days. Positioning: The Marketplace Behind Meaningful Connections. Public site: informaconnect.com/connect-winter-marketplace. - Target audience : association and corporate planners based in the Washington DC area and New York, plus destinations, suppliers, and hospitality partners nationally. - Hosted buyer categories : Association, Corporate, DC Hosted Buyer, New York Buyers. Homepage eyebrow: Association · Corporate · DC · New York. - Registration paths : Hosted Buyer (travel and hotel covered, up to 45 appointments, 4\\+ hours education); Supplier (up to 40 guaranteed appointments); Buyer Power Pass / non-hosted (access and educatio","path":"/events/regional"},{"title":"Sports and summits","sidebarTitle":"Sports and summits","description":"Sports Innovation Forum and The Women's Summit - the BizBash-side event ecosystem.","headings":["Sports Innovation Forum 2026","Pass types","Community and sponsors","Programme themes","Public site and resources","The Women's Summit 2026","Programme","Sponsors","Venue context","Next"],"body":"Two co-located events sit outside the marketplace format. They are conference-and-community events rather than appointment marketplaces, they use application-based passes rather than published package pricing, and they run out of the BizBash side of the business. Public URLs recaptured 27 August 2026 — see Public event sites. Sports Innovation Forum 2026 28-30 October 2026 - The Woodlands Waterway Marriott Hotel & Convention Center, The Woodlands, TX. Three days. Public site: informaconnect.com/sports-innovation-forum. Three days focused on how sports marketing, planning, and events actually get done - decision-makers, the approaches they are putting into practice, and connections across the sports industry. Cutting-edge education, peer-to-peer learning, and high-energy networking rather than scheduled one-to-ones. Pass types All three are application and approval based, with no publicly listed price. Pass For --- --- Brand Experience Pass Brand-side event and marketing professionals - retail, F&B, travel, CPG, automotive, banking, entertainment, sports Industry Supplier Pass Experiential agencies, mar-tech and ad-tech, DMOs and CVBs, vendor and solution providers Free Agent Pass Independent event professionals - sole proprietor, LLC, or independent contractor, not employed full-time by a brand, agency, venue, or supplier Premium sponsorship passes (Prime, Elite, Premier) are priced through the sales team only. Inquiries to bizbash.sales@informa.com . Community and sponsors Fifteen sports organisations sit in the partner roster: NFL Players Association, Minnesota Vikings Football, Major League Rugby, New York City FC, San Jose Sharks, Athletes Unlimited, Dallas Mavericks, Houston Rockets, Delta Air Lines, Major League Soccer, United States Tennis Association, San Diego Wave FC, MLV NorCal, Barstool Sports, and On Location. Sponsor tiers: Host is Visit The Woodlands. Premier - No Name Group, Czarnowski Collective, Wolfgang Puck Catering, Bluewater. Elite - Brand Activation Services, FG PG, Gladiator Productions. Prime - AEG. Eleven partners including Brand Makers, SpotMyPhotos, Northern Lights Drone Shows, Quest Events, fielddrive, SponsorshipX, and Gather Voices. Programme themes Sessions published at time of capture include Big Sports in the Big Apple, Innovations in Sponsorship Marketing, Women Leading the Future of Motorsports, Stealworthy VIP Experiences, The Women's Sports Report, and Houston Sports Ecosystem. Speakers include Brittany Stone (Ashley Global Retail), Heather Vaughan (San Jose Sharks), and Corey Kinder (Cleveland Browns). Public site and resources Resource URL --- --- Homepage sports-innovation-forum How to attend how-to-attend Plan your visit plan-your-visit Agenda agenda · schedule at a glance Sponsors sponsors Industry supplier packages industry-supplier-packages Marketing toolkit marketing-toolkit Brand Experience application VisitCloud 13brwu6q2be0f Industry Supplier application VisitCloud 0efunjr4ac9hg Free Agent application VisitCloud 2j6fahlrugvsf 2026 sponsorship story Turtl BizBash Sports bizbash.com/sporting-events Sports newsletter cloud.mail.bizbash.com/SportsNewsletter Several agenda slots were still marked \"Coming Soon\" at capture, no speaker names were attached to individual sessions, and the day-one agenda displayed early-morning times that are almost certainly a timezone display artefact. Do not treat the captured agenda as final. The Women's Summit 2026 27-28 October 2026 - The Woodlands Waterway Marriott Hotel & Convention Center. Public site: informaconnect.com/the-womens-summit. An intimate forum for honest dialogue, shared perspective, and strategic insight on the state and future of women in sports. Sessions cover leadership, investment, and the path forward, with curated networking, an onsite happy hour, and a dinner in partnership with the headlining partner, Czarnowski Collective. Structurally important : The Women's Summit is not separately ticketed. It runs as an add-on track inside Sports Innovation Forum . Attendees register for a Sports Innovation Forum pass - Brand Experience, Industry Supplier, or Free Agent - and select The Women's Summit as an add-on. Public how-to-attend: the-womens-summit/how-to-attend. Agenda: Day 1 · Day 2. Programme Day one runs a full afternoon and evening: HERstory in the Making, a Czarnowski-led session, Minute-To-Win-It, The Real Talk Roundtable on wellness, advocacy and growth, and Beyond Good Intentions on allyship, closing with happy hour and dinner. Day two is a shorter morning: breakfast roundtables, a fishbowl activity, More Than a Game on Houston's women's sports fandom, and a content round-up. Sponsors Major League Rugby, Minnesota Vikings, Dallas Mavericks, NFLPA, United States Tennis Association, MLV NorCal, New York City FC, San Diego Wave FC, Athletes Unlimited, Houston Rockets. Two open items. The homepage claims \"16 Brands in the Room\" but only four were confirmed by name in the capture - treat 16 as approximate. And the Summit's 27-28 October dates overlap the parent Sports Innovation Forum's 28-30 October dates on only one day; both are published as stated and have not been reconciled. Venue context The Woodlands is a master-planned destination founded in 1974, set in and around 28,000 tree-filled acres roughly 28 miles north of downtown Houston. Published distances vary by page - one states 20 miles from George Bush Intercontinental Airport and 30 miles from downtown Houston. Three of our events use this venue in a single October week. Neither event publishes a named staff contact. Both contact pages are JavaScript-rendered and returned only generic Informa corporate boilerplate. Route sponsorship and pricing questions to bizbash.sales@informa.com . Next The Tampa conference, 23-25 August 2026, the day before Connect Marketplace.","path":"/events/sports-and-summits"},{"title":"Supporting exhibitors after the event","sidebarTitle":"After the event","description":"Internal guidance for supplier follow-up, lead handling, rebooking, feedback, and return measurement.","headings":["Follow-up cadence","Lead handling","Rebooking","Measuring return","Feedback","Contacts"],"body":"Audience: Connect staff and approved internal agents. This page supports coaching and follow-up; it does not authorise staff to access or alter a supplier's own CRM. Most pipeline value from a Connect event is realised in the following six to twelve weeks. Staff should frame the marketplace as the start of the supplier's sales cycle, not the finish. Follow-up cadence A personalised message to every buyer the supplier met, referencing something specific from the appointment. Attach the digital pack and any custom offer the supplier promised in the meeting. Second-touch outreach on the buyers who did not respond. A shorter, more direct message on a specific date range or opportunity. Move active conversations into proposals and quotes. Update the supplier's CRM with opportunity stage and expected close date. Nurture the room. Buyers who did not book this year are pipeline for next year. Lead handling - Consolidate the supplier's appointment notes, walk-up conversations, and any leads captured through amplification products into a single source of truth - Score based on fit and timing, not just enthusiasm. Buyers with defined dates and rooms on peak convert; interested browsers do not - Tag the source event on every lead so the supplier can measure event-level performance later - Route into the supplier's CRM promptly. Leads that sit uncontacted for a week convert at a fraction of the rate Rebooking If the supplier did not rebook onsite in the rebooking lounge, do it in the first two weeks after the event. Two reasons: 1. Momentum from the onsite experience decays quickly and rebooking with fresh outcomes in mind is much easier 2. Popular tiers, especially Deal Closer at the flagship, cap at a small number of packages and can sell out Across the Connect portfolio, renewals close at roughly 85% at nearly twice the average deal size of net new business. Rebooking is the revenue engine. Measuring return A basic supplier ROI framework: Metric How to measure --- --- Cost per qualified conversation Package price divided by qualified appointments (exclude no-shows and poor-fit matches) Pipeline generated Sum of opportunity value tagged to the event within 90 days Closed-won revenue Sum of closed-won opportunities tagged to the event within 12 months Payback period Months to recoup total event spend, including travel and amplification Feedback Connect collects post-event feedback from suppliers and hosted buyers. Complete the survey; it drives track composition, matching rules, and event selection for future seasons. Contacts - General inquiries : mac.kitchin@informa.com - Registration and post-event admin : suzi.schnell@informa.com - Cancellations and refunds : connect.cancellations@informa.com","path":"/exhibitor-support/after-the-event"},{"title":"Exhibitor appointment model","sidebarTitle":"Appointment model","description":"Internal explanation of how supplier and hosted-buyer requests become scheduled marketplace appointments.","headings":["Timeline","How matching works","Appointment mechanics","Getting the most from the supplier's requests"],"body":"Audience: Connect staff and approved internal agents. Use this page to explain the process; confirm event-specific timing, platform, meeting length, and guarantees before making a promise. The appointment schedule is the core value of a Connect marketplace package. Timeline The supplier's appointment count is locked in based on the supplier's tier. Higher tiers (Deal Closer at the flagship) also lock in a top-five requests guaranteed promise. The supplier's company is listed in the attendee directory and event app. Hosted buyers can browse suppliers and start marking requests. Both sides submit requests: suppliers pick the buyers they want to see, hosted buyers pick the suppliers they want to see. Requests are reconciled, often AI-assisted, into a mutual-interest appointment schedule. Preferred tiers get priority on their top requests. The supplier receives a personalised appointment schedule ahead of the event, showing each appointment time, location, and buyer. A concierge desk handles substitutions, no-shows, and last-minute swaps. How matching works Matching combines three inputs: 1. Explicit requests from suppliers and hosted buyers 2. Profile fit based on buyer track, geography, budget size, and supplier category 3. Tier priority so Deal Closer packages resolve first, then Elite Connecter, and so on Deal Closer suppliers at the flagship are guaranteed their top five requests. Lower tiers are matched on best-effort mutual interest. Hosted buyers commit to a full appointment schedule as a condition of their sponsored attendance. The \\$200 refundable deposit is returned only when they complete the assigned appointments, which is what keeps the schedule live. See Hosted buyer deposit for the mechanism. Appointment mechanics - Appointments are one-to-one between one supplier representative and one hosted buyer - Meetings are short, focused conversations (some events describe them as nine minutes ; confirm the exact duration for the supplier's event before quoting it internally) - Blocks run in fixed windows across the event days, for example the flagship runs 10:00-12:00 and 15:00-17:00 on days two and three - Missed appointments count as no-shows against the buyer deposit Appointment duration and count numbers vary between event pages and marketing materials. Do not promise a specific meeting length to internal stakeholders without checking the confirmation packet for that specific event. Getting the most from the supplier's requests - Submit requests early, before the deadline. Late requests match against whatever is left. - Prioritise quality over quantity : five buyers the supplier can genuinely close beats twenty long shots. - Use the profile fields (buyer track, geography, event size) to filter, not just company names. - If the supplier has a Power Pass representative in addition to a scheduled representative, position that person for walk-up conversations rather than scheduled appointment slots.","path":"/exhibitor-support/appointment-model"},{"title":"Exhibitor contracting, billing, and escalation","sidebarTitle":"Contracting, billing and escalation","description":"Internal boundaries and routing for exhibitor agreements, invoices, payment terms, discounts, complaints, and exceptions.","headings":["Non-negotiable boundary","Route the request","Evidence staff should capture"],"body":"Use this page to route sensitive commercial questions. It does not replace the agreement, invoice, Salesforce record, or responsible human owner. Non-negotiable boundary Staff and agents must not invent, change, or commit payment terms, discounts, contract language, cancellation outcomes, unpublished prices, or expired offers. Route the request Request Action --- --- Published package price Confirm the source date on Packages and pricing, then verify current availability with the sales owner Specific discount or expired promotion Escalate to the account's sales owner Agreement or contractual language Escalate to Legal routing and the sales owner Invoice amount, payment status, or payment schedule Escalate to Stephen Han (Finance). Do not quote invoice amounts or payment schedules from this site Cancellation or transfer Confirm the registrant type and event-specific source, then escalate any exception Complaint Escalate to the named account owner Evidence staff should capture - Event and product requested - Account and opportunity identifiers in Salesforce, without copying record data into this site - Published source and capture date for any quoted package - The exact question or requested exception - The sales or service owner receiving the escalation Use Who owns what for current routing and Known issues before interpreting cancellation language.","path":"/exhibitor-support/contracting-billing-escalation"},{"title":"Exceptions and escalation","sidebarTitle":"","description":"What staff must not promise, disclose, or decide when supporting exhibitors.","headings":["Staff guidance","Customer-safe information","Internal-only context","Limits and escalation","Related pages"],"body":"- Purpose: Stop an exhibitor exception at the right human. - Audience: Sales, customer success, and approved internal agents. - Canonical source: Guardrails, Who owns what, and Contracting, billing and escalation. - Owner: Account sales owner for commercial exceptions; Stephen Han for billing. - Source status: Canonical for the escalation boundary. - Last reviewed: 27 August 2026. Staff guidance Escalate rather than invent. Capture event, account identifiers in Salesforce, the published source and date, the exact ask, and the owner receiving the handoff. Do not copy record-level data into this site. Exception Goes to --- --- Specific discount or expired promotion Account sales owner Agreement or contractual language Legal routing and the sales owner Invoice amount, payment status, or schedule Stephen Han (Finance) Complaint Named account owner Unpublished rate Do not quote. Sales owner if a published alternative exists Customer-safe information That the request has been routed, and any already-published package or event fact you have verified. Internal-only context Why the exception exists, Salesforce identifiers, and internal owner gaps. Limits and escalation Staff and agents must not invent, change, or commit payment terms, discounts, contract language, cancellation outcomes, unpublished prices, or expired offers. Related pages Full exhibitor commercial boundary. Current routing table.","path":"/exhibitor-support/exceptions-and-escalation"},{"title":"Exhibitor FAQs","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Staff route to the canonical customer FAQ extract for supplier, media, and contracting questions.","headings":["Staff guidance","Customer-safe information","Internal-only context","Limits and escalation","Related pages"],"body":"- Purpose: Find an approved short answer for a recurring exhibitor or supplier question. - Audience: Sales, customer success, and approved internal agents. - Canonical source: Notion Customer FAQs database, extracted on Customer FAQs. - Owner: Mac Kitchin. - Source status: Canonical extract; rows remain In progress in source. - Last reviewed: Extracted 12 August 2026. Staff guidance Use the Customer FAQs extract. Prefer the Short answer . Surface confidence below 0.8. Stop when When not to use applies. Then confirm event facts on the Event calendar. Supplier, media, and contracting pairs: - Supplier packages, appointments, and events - Media, creative, and campaigns - Contracting, payment, and discounts Customer-safe information The Short answer field, quoted rather than paraphrased into a new policy. Internal-only context Frequency, rank, confidence scores, When-not-to-use annotations, and Knowledge Candidates items that are not on the extract. Limits and escalation Do not invent a thirtieth pair. Billing, discounts, invoice timing, and attendee lists require a human. See Exceptions and escalation. Related pages Canonical FAQ home. Which playbook page to open first.","path":"/exhibitor-support/faqs"},{"title":"Exhibitor fit and qualification","sidebarTitle":"Exhibitor fit and qualification","description":"Internal criteria for determining whether a supplier fits the Connect marketplace model and which event route to use.","headings":["Strong-fit supplier categories","Fit questions for staff","Route the supplier"],"body":"Use this page to make an initial fit assessment. It is not approval to quote a package, promise appointments, or confirm participation. Audience: Connect staff and approved internal agents. Confirm the specific event, package, availability, and current terms with the responsible sales owner before making a commitment. Strong-fit supplier categories - Convention and visitors bureaus and destination marketing organisations - Hotels, resorts, convention centres, and venues - Audiovisual, production, and event-technology providers - Airlines, ground-transportation providers, gifting companies, and experiential partners - Other suppliers with a credible product for qualified meeting and event planners Fit questions for staff Question Why it matters --- --- Does the supplier sell into meetings and events? The appointment audience must be commercially relevant. Can the supplier serve the geography or buyer track attached to the event? Regional and specialty portfolios have different audiences. Is the supplier seeking qualified appointments rather than general floor traffic? The Connect value proposition is scheduled access. Does the supplier have an offer and onsite representative able to make decisions? Short appointments work only when the supplier can act. Is the requested product currently published and available? Package and sponsorship availability is source-dated, not live inventory. Route the supplier Use for suppliers seeking scheduled buyer appointments. Use for visibility, media, onsite activations, or event-attached advertising. Use when the audience is destination-marketing or tourism specific. Route package, rate, exception, and availability decisions to the correct commercial owner. Do not treat a category match as confirmation of inventory, price, discount, contract terms, or appointment guarantees.","path":"/exhibitor-support/fit-and-qualification"},{"title":"Supporting exhibitors onsite","sidebarTitle":"Onsite experience","description":"Internal playbook for explaining the marketplace day, supporting appointment delivery, and handling onsite routing.","headings":["The shape of a Connect event","Working the appointment blocks","Between appointment blocks","Signature moments to know","Rebooking onsite","Onsite support"],"body":"Audience: Connect staff and approved internal agents. Event agendas vary; verify the live event page before quoting exact times or inclusions. Use this page to set supplier expectations and route common onsite questions. Most Connect events follow a similar three-beat structure. The shape of a Connect event Regiception (combined registration and reception), a first-timer orientation at flagships, a welcome education session, the first block of marketplace appointments, and an opening reception hosted by the destination. Breakfast and education, a morning appointment block, lunch and learn, an afternoon appointment block, then an open night in the host city. This is where most of the business gets done. Grab-and-go breakfast, a final appointment block, and departures. Flagships add a closing concert or the annual Connect 5K. Regional events compress this into days one and two. Flagships extend it with tradeshow experiences, speaker showcases, hosted meals, and a rebooking lounge. Working the appointment blocks Flagship appointment blocks run 10:00-12:00 and 15:00-17:00 on days two and three. Regional blocks are similar in shape but shorter overall. - Arrive early at the supplier's table or booth to reset between appointments - Keep to the clock . Appointments are short; running long punishes the next buyer, not just the supplier - Log outcomes immediately after each meeting. A rating and a two-line note is enough; the supplier will forget by dinner otherwise - Watch for no-shows and report them to the concierge desk so the buyer's schedule can be adjusted and their deposit compliance tracked Between appointment blocks The soft moments matter as much as the hard schedule: - Hosted breakfasts and lunches are curated by track and are the best places for extended conversations with buyers who would otherwise be capped at a short appointment - Opening receptions and open nights are usually hosted by the destination and are excellent for informal introductions - Education sessions can introduce the supplier to buyers who are not on the scheduled diary but are relevant to the supplier's market - The Connect 5K, morning walk clubs, and wellness activations are small-group settings that build memorable rapport Signature moments to know Event Signature moments --- --- Connect Marketplace Tampa 2026 Discover the Bay opening reception (Visit Tampa Bay), Connect 5K on day three, closing concert (Two Friends) Spring Marketplace Las Vegas 2027 Fifth annual Connect White Party, Morning Walk Club at 07:00 Regionals Destination-hosted opening receptions and open night in the host city Sports Innovation Forum \\+ Women's Summit Co-located tracks at the Woodlands Waterway Marriott See Flagship marketplaces and Regional marketplaces for full detail. Rebooking onsite Flagships run a dedicated rebooking lounge where returning suppliers can commit to the next season before leaving. Staff should encourage onsite rebooking when the supplier has internal approval. Renewals close at a materially higher rate and larger deal size than net new. Bring a signing authority or at minimum a rate sheet to the rebooking lounge. Deals not closed onsite regularly slip a full quarter before they land. Onsite support - Concierge / help desk : schedule changes, badge issues, appointment substitutions - Registration : badge pickup, walk-up power pass upgrades - Cancellations mid-event : connect.cancellations@informa.com","path":"/exhibitor-support/onsite-experience"},{"title":"Supporting exhibitors","sidebarTitle":"Staff overview","description":"Internal staff reference for supporting hospitality suppliers through fit, package selection, appointments, registration, onsite delivery, and follow-up.","headings":["Suppliers this section covers","How a Connect marketplace works","What's in this guide","Portfolio at a glance","Get in touch"],"body":"Use this section when advising or supporting a hospitality supplier before, during, or after a Connect marketplace. It explains the customer journey while preserving the internal verification and escalation boundaries staff need. Public supplier purchase for Connect Marketplace 2026: VisitCloud 3qgcu41z4szt3 . How to attend: connect-marketplace/how-to-attend. Sponsorship deck: Turtl. Other event forms and hotel blocks: Public event sites. Audience: Connect staff and approved internal agents. This is not a public exhibitor guide. Staff may adapt customer-safe facts in conversation, but must not share internal system details, source conflicts, unpublished rates, or ownership gaps. Suppliers this section covers Hospitality suppliers exhibiting at Connect marketplace events, including: - Convention and visitors bureaus (CVBs) and destination marketing organisations (DMOs) - Hotels, resorts, and convention centres - Audiovisual, production, and event technology vendors - Airlines, ground transportation, and gifting or experiential partners For staff supporting event planners attending on a sponsored basis, use Hosted Buyer Support. How a Connect marketplace works Event planners apply to the hosted buyer programme and are vetted for scale, sourcing authority, and event history. See Business model for the full qualification bar. Suppliers purchase packages priced by the number of one-to-one appointments. Regional events start at \\$4,250 for 12 appointments; flagships offer higher tiers with more appointments and preferred placement. Requests are collected from both sides and matched, often with automation support, before anyone travels. The supplier arrives with a schedule rather than relying on floor traffic. Appointment blocks run in fixed windows across the event days, alongside education, networking, and hosted meals. A dedicated rebooking lounge at the flagship lets returning suppliers commit to the next event before leaving. Renewals are the core of the business. What's in this guide Supplier categories, fit test, and which Connect events match which offerings. Appointment tiers, pricing at the flagships and regionals, and add-on amplification products. How matching works, when to submit requests, and how the schedule is built. Deadlines, hotel blocks, marketing collateral, and what to bring. Arrival, appointment blocks, hosted meals, and the flow of a marketplace day. Follow-up, lead handling, rebooking, and measuring ROI. Portfolio at a glance Nine live events run in the 2026-2027 season. Full detail with dates, venues, and format is on the Event calendar. - Flagships : Connect Marketplace Tampa 2026 and Spring Marketplace Las Vegas 2027. Three days, full track structure, largest supplier and buyer counts. - Regionals : Connect Texas, West, South \\+ Faith, Winter Marketplace, and Connect North. Two to three days, compressed format, regional buyer focus. - Sports and summits : Sports Innovation Forum and The Women's Summit. Not sure which event fits? Start with Who should exhibit, then compare buyer tracks on the individual event pages. Get in touch For exhibitor inquiries, contact the Connect sales team. General event inquiries for Connect Marketplace go to mac.kitchin@informa.com .","path":"/exhibitor-support/overview"},{"title":"Exhibitor packages and published pricing","sidebarTitle":"Packages and published pricing","description":"Internal reference for source-dated appointment packages, inclusions, and the limits on what staff may quote.","headings":["Flagship packages","Regional packages","What's included in every supplier package","Event amplification add-ons","Sponsorships","Booking and cancellation"],"body":"Audience: Connect staff and approved internal agents. Package figures below are published, source-dated reference points—not live inventory or authority to discount. Supplier packages are priced primarily by the number of one-to-one appointments with hosted buyers. Reception access, education, and meals are normally bundled as described by the applicable package source. Flagship packages Published for Connect Marketplace 2026. - Purchase form: VisitCloud 3qgcu41z4szt3 - Attendance guide: Connect Marketplace — How to attend - Related event: Spring Marketplace 2027 uses similar tiers Package Price Appointments Notes --- --- --- --- Deal Closer \\$5,950 40 Top five requests guaranteed, preferred listing. Limited to 15 packages. Elite Connecter \\$5,250 30 Marked most popular. Limited to 35 packages. Growth Accelerator \\$4,850 24 Ice Breaker \\$4,250 12 Entry tier. Power Pass (Supplier) \\$1,950 0 Access and education only, no scheduled meetings. Appointment counts and 9-minute meeting durations are published inconsistently on some event sites. Confirm the exact numbers in the supplier's agreement before promising the exhibitor a specific schedule length. Regional packages Regional marketplaces (Connect Texas, West, South \\+ Faith, Winter Marketplace, Connect North) share a common entry price of \\$4,250 for 12 appointments . Higher tiers exist at some regionals; check the individual event page for the current published options. Regional entry Price Appointments --- --- --- Standard supplier package \\$4,250 12 What's included in every supplier package - Access to all appointment blocks matching the supplier's tier - Opening reception and hosted networking events - Education sessions (3\\+ hours at regionals; 14 to 15 hours at flagships) - Marketplace listing in the attendee directory and event app - Onsite badge and access to hosted meals as scheduled Not included: the supplier's own travel, hotel room outside the supplier block, and any event amplification add-ons. Event amplification add-ons Advertising and content products attached to an event. Priced separately from the appointment package. Product Typical price --- --- BizBash Spring Issue \\$7,000\\+ BizBash Marketplace / Fall Issue (includes Connect Marketplace Guide) \\$7,000\\+ Sports Facilities Guide \\$7,000\\+ Texas Meeting Planners Guide \\$9,950 Winter Marketplace Guide \\$7,000\\+ Pre-show \\+ post-show emails \\$5,000\\+ Event targeting \\$6,000\\+ Onsite ads \\$3,000\\+ See Business model for how these map to Salesforce revenue categories. Sponsorships Hosted meals, afternoon amp-ups, the hosted buyer amenity shop, closing-night parties, and other sponsorship opportunities are sold through a per-event prospectus. Pricing is not published; request the current prospectus from the sponsorship contact for the event the supplier is targeting. Booking and cancellation Standard cancellation: full refund minus a 10% administration charge if cancelled 57 days or more before the event. No refund inside 56 days. Terms vary by event; always confirm against the Terms & Conditions on the specific event site. Supplier hotel room blocks typically close 3 to 4 weeks before the event. A supplier that misses the block may have to book independently at the available rate.","path":"/exhibitor-support/packages-and-pricing"},{"title":"Supporting exhibitor event preparation","sidebarTitle":"Preparing for the event","description":"Internal checklist for helping exhibitors manage deadlines, hotel blocks, collateral, appointments, and team readiness.","headings":["Key deadlines to track","Team readiness","Collateral to bring","Marketing amplification","Logistics"],"body":"Audience: Connect staff and approved internal agents. Use the applicable event source for exact dates; the timings below are patterns and source-dated examples. Use this checklist to help a supplier prepare for the event and protect the value of its appointment package. Key deadlines to track Deadline Typical timing What happens if the supplier misses it --- --- --- Complete company profile in the event platform Weeks before requests open The supplier may not appear correctly in buyer searches Submit appointment requests Before the published request cutoff The supplier matches on residuals, not preferences Book supplier hotel block 3 to 4 weeks before the event The supplier pays the applicable independent rate Standard cancellation cutoff 57 days before Inside 56 days, no refund Confirm onsite team roster Ahead of badge production Delayed or reissued badges onsite Exact dates vary by event. Confirm against the specific event site: Flagship marketplaces or Regional marketplaces. Recent supplier hotel block deadlines for reference: Connect Marketplace 3 August, Connect Texas 5 October, Connect West 14 October, Connect South 16 October, Winter Marketplace 27 October. Team readiness Advise the supplier to send people with decision authority on the offer being presented: rate cards, dates, complimentary offerings, and creative concessions. Appointments are short; buyers quickly evaluate whether the supplier can act. Match the supplier's team size to the supplier's appointment volume. One rep can realistically run a 40-appointment schedule at a flagship over three days, but they will be depleted by day three. Two reps splitting the schedule is more sustainable and gives the supplier a walk-up presence. Everyone should know: the buyer track breakdown, the supplier's top-priority requests, the supplier's standard offer, the supplier's walk-away terms, and where the concierge desk is for schedule changes. Collateral to bring - One-page leave-behind with rates, capacities, and dates. Not a full brochure. - Digital pack delivered by email or QR code during the appointment. Buyers rarely carry paper home. - Case studies relevant to the buyer track the supplier is meeting (corporate, association, sports, etc.). - Business cards or digital equivalent for the walk-up moments outside appointments. Marketing amplification If the supplier has bought event amplification add-ons (Packages and pricing), coordinate creative deadlines with the Connect team. Pre-show and post-show email campaigns typically need creative delivered several weeks ahead. Logistics - Book flights with schedule flexibility on the closing day; last appointment blocks run into the afternoon - Book the supplier hotel block before it closes; do not book outside for a small saving and then miss the block - Confirm shipping for booth materials, giveaways, or samples with the venue and Connect operations team - Check code of conduct and any dress guidance published for the specific event If the supplier is attending multiple events in a season, route an integrated media and sponsorship discussion to Connect sales rather than treating each event as an isolated purchase.","path":"/exhibitor-support/preparing-for-the-event"},{"title":"Exhibitor registration and onboarding","sidebarTitle":"Registration and onboarding","description":"Internal staff route from Closed Won through attendee details, event-platform setup, deadlines, and appointment readiness.","headings":["Standard staff path"],"body":"This page connects the commercial close to event readiness. Exact forms, cutoffs, and platform steps vary by event and remain owned by the live event process. Standard staff path Verify the Opportunity and line items in connect-prod . Do not use this site to infer a named customer's current status. The Customer FAQ library records a post-signature attendee-details step, often using DocuSign. Use the current event workflow rather than sending an unverified form. Confirm that the supplier organisation, attendees, package, and appointment entitlement appear in the applicable platform. Profile completion, request submission, hotel blocks, creative delivery, badge production, and cancellation dates come from the event source. Route the supplier to Appointment model and Preparing for the event. The current source set does not establish one universal supplier-registration workflow for every event. Do not fabricate a URL, platform, deadline, or automation path.","path":"/exhibitor-support/registration-onboarding"},{"title":"Exhibitor response library","sidebarTitle":"Response library","description":"Internal response routes, customer-safe sources, disclosure boundaries, and escalations for exhibitor questions.","headings":["Response routes","Customer-safe versus internal-only"],"body":"Use this page to choose the right source before replying to an exhibitor or supplier. Response routes Question Start with Then check --- --- --- Is this event a fit? Fit and qualification Event portfolio and sales owner What packages or prices are published? Packages and pricing Source date and current availability How are appointments built? Appointment model Event-specific platform and deadlines What happens before or onsite? Event preparation and Onsite experience Live event page Is there a standard answer? Customer FAQs Confidence and When-not-to-use rule Who decides an exception? Who owns what Guardrails and named account owner Customer-safe versus internal-only Published event facts, published package contents, the standard appointment model, preparation guidance, and approved FAQ language. Salesforce IDs and defects, webhook details, ownership gaps, unpublished rates, internal confidence scoring, private source locations, and unresolved analysis. Billing, invoices, legal, contract changes, complaints, specific discounts, named attendee lists, and unpublished sponsorship rates always require escalation.","path":"/exhibitor-support/response-library"},{"title":"What staff may communicate","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Customer-safe exhibitor information versus internal-only context.","headings":["Staff guidance","Customer-safe information","Internal-only context","Limits and escalation","Related pages"],"body":"- Purpose: Decide what may leave the building when answering an exhibitor. - Audience: Sales, customer success, operations, and approved internal agents. - Canonical source: Exhibitor Support playbooks and the Customer FAQs extract. - Owner: Page maintainers on each linked playbook; FAQ extract — Mac Kitchin. - Source status: Canonical for the boundary; event and package facts remain source-dated. - Last reviewed: 27 August 2026. Staff guidance Use this split before sending a reply. When in doubt, keep it internal and escalate. Customer-safe information - Published event facts from the Event calendar after checking Known issues - Published package contents and prices from Packages and published pricing, with source date - The standard appointment model - Preparation and onsite guidance on the playbook pages - Approved FAQ short answers Internal-only context - Salesforce IDs, defects, and webhook details - Ownership gaps - Unpublished rates and sponsorship prospectus pricing - Internal confidence scoring - Private source locations and unresolved analysis Limits and escalation Customer-safe wording on a page does not make the full page suitable to forward. See Exceptions and escalation. Related pages Routing table for exhibitor questions. Hard limits for anyone answering on Connect's behalf.","path":"/exhibitor-support/what-staff-may-communicate"},{"title":"Supporting hosted buyers after the event","sidebarTitle":"After the event","description":"Internal guidance for deposit questions, supplier follow-up, feedback, and returning hosted-buyer applications.","headings":["Deposit return","Supplier follow-up","Post-event feedback","Applying for the next event","What returning attendance looks like","Contacts"],"body":"Audience: Connect staff and approved internal agents. Deposit timing and return mechanism remain unconfirmed; do not invent or promise them. Use this page to explain the normal post-event path and route deposit, follow-up, feedback, and returning-buyer questions. Deposit return The current guidance says the hosted buyer's \\$200 deposit is returned after the assigned appointment schedule has been completed. Attendance is tracked at each appointment via the check-in process. If a supplier no-showed, the concierge desk logs it so it does not count against the hosted buyer. If the hosted buyer believes the deposit was forfeited in error, route the case to connect.cancellations@informa.com with the event name and registration details. Supplier follow-up Suppliers will typically reach out to the hosted buyer within a few days of the event with tailored proposals based on the hosted buyer's appointment conversation. To keep sourcing moving: - Reply to suppliers being progressed so they know where to focus - Politely decline suppliers that are not a fit so both sides can move on - Route serious proposals into the hosted buyer's normal sourcing workflow - Save appointment notes in the hosted buyer's normal sourcing system for future destination or venue searches Post-event feedback Connect sends a post-event survey . Complete it. Feedback directly shapes: - Which suppliers appear at future events - Track composition and qualification bars - Matching rules for next season's appointments - Programme design and hosted amenities Applying for the next event See the full Event calendar to pick the hosted buyer's next Connect event based on geography, track, and sourcing timing. Hosted buyer capacity is capped per track. Applying early improves the hosted buyer's odds of a slot in the hosted buyer's first-choice event. Refresh the hosted buyer's event portfolio, sourcing volume, and role so the Hosted Buyer Experience Team can vet the hosted buyer against current criteria. Returning hosted buyers with a strong attendance record are prioritised. The hosted buyer's track record from this event carries into the next application. What returning attendance looks like - Faster application review since the hosted buyer's history is on file - Priority consideration for oversubscribed tracks - Continued relationship with the hosted buyer's Hosted Buyer Experience contact where possible - Eligibility for programmes like Club Connect where they apply Contacts - Hosted Buyer Experience Team : application questions for the next event, paired contact carryover - Registration : suzi.schnell@informa.com - Cancellations and deposit issues : connect.cancellations@informa.com","path":"/hosted-buyer-support/after-the-event"},{"title":"Hosted-buyer application and approval","sidebarTitle":"Application and qualification","description":"Internal reference for application, vetting, deposit handling, approval, and appointment commitments.","headings":["The application","What Connect verifies","The deposit","When the deposit is forfeited","When the deposit is returned","The appointment commitment","After approval","Contacts"],"body":"Audience: Connect staff and approved internal agents. Use this page to explain the standard path; verify event-specific application links, cutoffs, and policy before responding. The application is short, but qualification is substantive. This page explains the staff view of both. The application The application takes about ten minutes to complete. The hosted buyer provides company details, role, event portfolio, sourcing scale, and preferred track. A \\$200 refundable deposit secures the application. Current guidance says it is returned when the assigned appointment schedule is completed. The team vets the hosted buyer's application against track criteria: role, sourcing volume, event history, rotation capacity. Approved hosted buyers are paired with a dedicated point of contact from the Hosted Buyer Experience Team who supports the hosted buyer through pre-event, onsite, and follow-up. What Connect verifies - Role and decision authority for sourcing - Event portfolio : two-year verifiable history - Sourcing scale : rooms on peak, budget size, or attendance depending on track - Rotation : national or regional as appropriate to the event Providing accurate information is important. Misrepresenting scale or authority is grounds for disqualification and can affect future eligibility across the Connect portfolio. The deposit The \\$200 deposit is described as refundable in full when the assigned appointment schedule is completed. It is not presented as an event fee. The deposit is the mechanism that keeps the marketplace commercially viable. Suppliers pay for a specific number of appointments, and those appointments only have value if hosted buyers actually show up. From the business model page: The deposit is the enforcement mechanism for the whole model. If hosted buyers skip appointments, suppliers do not renew, and renewals are 79 percent of supplier revenue. When the deposit is forfeited - No-shows at scheduled appointments - Late cancellation inside the hosted buyer cancellation window (typically inside 7 days of the event, plus a one-night hotel fee) - Failure to complete the assigned appointment schedule Two hosted-buyer cancellation policies are published across the portfolio: one on the Hosted Buyers page and one in the Terms & Conditions. Always confirm the event and registrant type before assuming that the general 30-day cutoff applies. When the deposit is returned - Full refund or credit is described for cancellation 30 or more days before the event - Full refund on completing the hosted buyer's assigned appointment schedule onsite The appointment commitment A hosted buyer commits to a full appointment schedule matched to documented sourcing needs. At the flagship this can be up to 45 appointments across the event. Regional schedules are usually shorter. Appointments are: - One-to-one with a supplier representative - Short and focused (some events describe them as nine minutes) - Matched in advance from mutual-interest requests, often AI-assisted - Tracked onsite ; missed appointments count against the hosted buyer's deposit See Onsite experience for how the day is structured around these blocks. After approval Once approved and paired with a Hosted Buyer Experience contact, the hosted buyer moves into the pre-event phase: submitting supplier requests, arranging travel, and preparing a sourcing brief. See Preparing for the event. Contacts - Hosted Buyer Experience Team : general application and support inquiries - Registration help : suzi.schnell@informa.com - Cancellations : connect.cancellations@informa.com","path":"/hosted-buyer-support/application-and-approval"},{"title":"Hosted-buyer commitments","sidebarTitle":"Hosted buyer commitments","description":"Internal reference for the appointment, attendance, profile, and communication commitments attached to sponsored attendance.","headings":["Commitments staff should explain","Deposit relationship","Related pages"],"body":"Hosted attendance is an exchange: Connect provides the published event support and the hosted buyer completes the applicable programme commitments. Commitments staff should explain - Provide accurate role, organisation, sourcing, and event-history information during application. - Submit supplier requests and maintain a useful sourcing profile by the applicable deadline. - Attend the event and complete the assigned appointment schedule. - Use onsite support when a supplier no-shows or an unavoidable issue affects attendance. - Follow the applicable cancellation and transfer process rather than abandoning the schedule. Deposit relationship The current content consistently describes a \\$200 refundable deposit as the appointment-attendance control. Source material conflicts on cancellation windows and does not confirm one universal return timing or mechanism. Staff must not promise a refund date, mechanism, or exception. Confirm the event-specific policy and escalate disputes through the Hosted Buyer Experience team. Related pages Application, vetting, approval, and deposit context. Internal Visit Create and Salesforce lifecycle.","path":"/hosted-buyer-support/commitments"},{"title":"Hosted-buyer coverage","sidebarTitle":"What is covered","description":"Internal reference for published hosted-buyer coverage, exclusions, special cases, and policy conflicts.","headings":["What Connect covers","What the hosted buyer covers","Special cases","International (RTO) buyers","Informa colleagues","Regional versus flagship","Club Connect","Cancellation and its cost","Contacts"],"body":"Audience: Connect staff and approved internal agents. Confirm coverage and booking rules for the specific event before advising a hosted buyer. Use this page to explain what Connect covers, what the hosted buyer covers, and where event-specific differences require verification. What Connect covers Coverage recorded across the current hosted-buyer material includes: - Roundtrip airfare through the approved booking route, or the published event-specific stipend where that model applies - Hotel accommodation for the official event nights - Official programme meals and refreshments - Event registration, education, and networking access included in the hosted-buyer programme - Pre-scheduled supplier appointments and the related appointment-platform access These are programme-level reference points, not a universal travel authorisation. Confirm the event, approved booking route, covered nights, and any cap before advising the hosted buyer. What the hosted buyer covers Airfare outside the published booking window when the hosted buyer books late or outside policy Nights outside the official event nights when the hosted buyer extends the stay Ground transportation to and from the hosted buyer's home airport Incidentals at the hotel (room service, minibar, spa, etc.) Optional excursions and pre- or post-conference adventures unless specifically included Companion or guest costs ; the hosted buyer package is for the qualified buyer only Special cases International (RTO) buyers International buyers attending the flagship as receptive tour operators receive a flight stipend up to \\$600 per company rather than direct flight booking. At Spring Marketplace, qualified RTOs receive complimentary hosted buyer registration. Informa colleagues Informa PLC colleagues attending under the Informa track have registration fees waived but travel and hotel arrangements follow standard Informa T&E policy, not the hosted buyer package. Regional versus flagship Coverage is broadly the same across regional and flagship events, but the shape differs: Item Flagship Regional --- --- --- Hotel nights covered Typically 3 nights Typically 2 nights Education content Up to 14 to 15 hours CMP-credit 3\\+ hours Appointments Up to 45 (flagship) Up to 25 (typical regional) Signature moments Multiple, including closing concert or Connect 5K Destination-hosted opening reception, open night Club Connect Some regional events run Club Connect , a points-based hosted buyer rewards programme. Points can be redeemed for perks such as priority seating, hospitality-suite access, and airport-hotel transportation. Ask the assigned Hosted Buyer Experience contact whether the applicable event includes it. Cancellation and its cost Hosted-buyer cancellation windows and refund rules are published differently across event sites. Always confirm the specific cutoff and any late-cancel fee before advising on non-refundable travel or hotel extensions. As a general reference: - 30 or more days before the event: full refund or credit - Inside 7 days of the event: no refund plus a one-night hotel fee - Deposit returned on completing the appointment schedule; forfeited on no-shows Contacts - Travel and hotel logistics : the hosted buyer's paired Hosted Buyer Experience contact - Registration questions : suzi.schnell@informa.com - Cancellation : connect.cancellations@informa.com","path":"/hosted-buyer-support/coverage"},{"title":"Hosted-buyer eligibility","sidebarTitle":"Who qualifies","description":"Internal reference for the general hosted-buyer baseline, track-specific fit, and limits of staff qualification guidance.","headings":["The general baseline","Tracks in the portfolio","Who Connect is trying to attract","Regional versus flagship qualification","What disqualifies applicants"],"body":"Audience: Connect staff and approved internal agents. This page supports initial assessment; the Hosted Buyer Experience Team makes qualification decisions. Hosted buyer eligibility is organised by track . Different Connect events run different tracks; a planner who fits one may not fit another. The general baseline Before any track-specific criteria, Connect looks for: - 50 or more rooms on peak nights across the events the hosted buyer sources - Ability to rotate destinations nationally or regionally rather than always sourcing the same city - Two-year verifiable event history The rotation requirement matters because destinations sponsor the room. A planner who is only ever going to book their home city has limited value to a national destination roster. Tracks in the portfolio Track Scale at flagship Key qualification detail --- --- --- Corporate 400\\+ Director, VP, or C-suite; six to seven figure budgets. Examples: Cisco, DHL, Google, Mastercard, NHL, PNC, PWC, Subway Association 300\\+ 50\\+ rooms on peak. Examples: American Psychological Association, CollegeBoard, Habitat for Humanity, National Kidney Foundation Citywide Spring Marketplace 87% of buyers manage \\$1M\\+ budgets; minimum 1,000\\+ rooms or 100,000\\+ sq ft Specialty 300\\+ (75\\+ planners) Social, military, and fraternal group planners Sports 200\\+ Competitive sporting events and sports-themed meetings. Examples: American Junior Golf Association, Spartan Race, USA Pickleball, USSSA, Varsity Spirit International (RTO) 150\\+ Receptive tour operators booking inbound North America travel. Flight stipend up to \\$600 per company at the flagship Business Travel Spring Marketplace 1 in 3 buyers controls \\$10M\\+ travel budgets; 80%\\+ manager level or above Diversity Spring Marketplace Community goal of 100\\+ diversity event professionals; faith-based, fraternal, nonprofit, education, LGBTQ\\+, multicultural, and women's group planners Faith Connect South \\+ Faith Planners of church retreats, ministry gatherings, conventions, and spiritual conferences Informa All events Informa PLC colleagues only; registration fees waived See the individual event pages for the tracks that run at each event: Flagship marketplaces, Regional marketplaces, Sports and summits. Who Connect is trying to attract From the flagship Why Sponsor page: - 61% of hosted buyers manage budgets over \\$250K - 31% manage \\$500K or more - 34% generate 1,000 or more room nights per event - 45% require 250 or more peak rooms - 22% require 500 or more If the planner's programme is comparable to these examples, the planner may fit the profile. Regional versus flagship qualification Regional events (Connect Texas, West, South \\+ Faith, Winter Marketplace, Connect North) apply the same general baseline as the flagship but usually add a geography filter : - Connect Texas : located in Texas - Connect West : located in Western states - Connect South \\+ Faith : no strict geography, but Faith Track eligibility applies - Connect Winter Marketplace : association and corporate planners based in the DC and NY areas, plus national destinations - Connect North : located in or sourcing for the Midwest and Northeast If a planner meets the general bar but not a specific regional geography filter, staff should recommend a more suitable regional or the flagship. Do not stretch a track fit; the vetting team makes the final decision. What disqualifies applicants - No two-year verifiable event history - Sourcing volume well below 50 rooms on peak with no clear growth trajectory - Purely local sourcing with no ability or intent to rotate - Supplier or agency roles rather than planner roles (agencies may qualify if they are sourcing on behalf of an end client with authority)","path":"/hosted-buyer-support/eligibility"},{"title":"Exceptions and escalation","sidebarTitle":"","description":"What staff must not promise, disclose, or decide when supporting hosted buyers.","headings":["Staff guidance","Customer-safe information","Internal-only context","Limits and escalation","Related pages"],"body":"- Purpose: Stop a hosted-buyer exception at the right human. - Audience: Hosted Buyer Experience, operations, and approved internal agents. - Canonical source: Guardrails, Who owns what, and the hosted-buyer playbooks. - Owner: Rachel Piper for hosted buyer experience; Bill Mathis for hosted buyer datasets; Stephen Han for billing. - Source status: Canonical for the escalation boundary. - Last reviewed: 27 August 2026. Staff guidance Escalate rather than invent a URL, refund, or travel promise. Exception Goes to --- --- Eligibility exception Hosted Buyer Experience — Rachel Piper's area Deposit dispute or cancellation exception Confirm event policy first, then human owner on Who owns what Billing or invoice Stephen Han (Finance) Complaint Named account or programme owner Named attendee or buyer contact list Do not share. Brand-and-title samples only Customer-safe information That the request has been routed, and any already-published eligibility, coverage, or event fact you have verified. Internal-only context Visit and Salesforce identifiers, webhook defects, and dual cancellation-policy wording. Limits and escalation Do not invent a public application URL. Two hosted-buyer cancellation policies are published across the portfolio — report both; do not pick a winner. Related pages Routing table for hosted-buyer questions. Current routing table.","path":"/hosted-buyer-support/exceptions-and-escalation"},{"title":"Hosted buyer FAQs","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Staff route to the canonical customer FAQ extract for hosted-buyer and attendee questions.","headings":["Staff guidance","Customer-safe information","Internal-only context","Limits and escalation","Related pages"],"body":"- Purpose: Find an approved short answer for a recurring hosted-buyer question. - Audience: Hosted Buyer Experience, operations, and approved internal agents. - Canonical source: Notion Customer FAQs database, extracted on Customer FAQs. - Owner: Mac Kitchin. - Source status: Canonical extract; rows remain In progress in source. - Last reviewed: Extracted 12 August 2026. Staff guidance Use the hosted buyer and attendee pairs on the Customer FAQs extract. Prefer the Short answer . Surface confidence below 0.8. Stop when When not to use applies. Confirm event facts on the Event calendar. Customer-safe information The Short answer field, quoted rather than paraphrased into a new policy. Internal-only context Frequency, rank, confidence scores, When-not-to-use annotations, and deposit-process discrepancies that must be reported as both versions. Limits and escalation Deposit disputes, cancellation exceptions, billing, complaints, named attendee data, legal terms, and unverified travel promises require human escalation. Related pages Canonical FAQ home. Which playbook page to open first.","path":"/hosted-buyer-support/faqs"},{"title":"Supporting hosted buyers onsite","sidebarTitle":"Onsite experience","description":"Internal playbook for appointment attendance, education, networking, deposit tracking, and onsite hosted-buyer support.","headings":["The shape of the hosted buyer's day","Appointment blocks","Beyond the appointments","Signature moments to know","Club Connect","Standing commitments across every event","Onsite support"],"body":"Audience: Connect staff and approved internal agents. Verify the live agenda before quoting times; use the concierge or Hosted Buyer Experience team for onsite exceptions. Use this page to set expectations and route common onsite questions. The programme is designed so appointments, education, and networking work together. The shape of the hosted buyer's day Regiception (combined registration and reception), first-timer orientation at flagships, a welcome education session, the hosted buyer's first appointment block , and an opening reception hosted by the destination. Breakfast and education, a morning appointment block, lunch and learn, an afternoon appointment block, then an open night in the host city. Grab-and-go breakfast and a final appointment block. Flagships add a closing concert or the annual Connect 5K. Regional events compress this into two days. Flagships extend it with tradeshow experiences, speaker sessions, and hosted meals. Appointment blocks Flagship appointment blocks run 10:00-12:00 and 15:00-17:00 on days two and three. Regional blocks are similar in shape but shorter overall. - The hosted buyer's schedule is published in the event app before the event - Each appointment is one-to-one with a specific supplier, in a fixed slot and location - Meetings are short and focused; keep to the clock - Attendance is tracked ; check-ins at the hosted buyer's appointments feed into the hosted buyer's deposit compliance - If a supplier is a no-show, report to the concierge desk so it does not count against the hosted buyer Missing appointments forfeits the hosted buyer's deposit and affects future eligibility. If something goes wrong, contact the concierge desk immediately rather than skipping. Beyond the appointments The programme intentionally schedules soft time around the hard blocks: - Hosted breakfasts and lunches are curated by track and are excellent for deeper conversations with suppliers or peers - Opening receptions are hosted by the destination and are the best introduction to the host city - Open night in the host city is unstructured; the hosted buyer chooses how to spend it - Education sessions provide CMP-credit content (up to 14 to 15 hours at flagships) - Wellness activations like the Connect 5K, Morning Walk Club (07:00 at Spring Marketplace), and quiet or prayer rooms are part of the standing programme Signature moments to know Event Signature moments --- --- Connect Marketplace Tampa 2026 Discover the Bay opening reception (Visit Tampa Bay), TSNN Fifty Over 50 reception, Women in Sports Waterfront Walk, Connect 5K, closing concert (Two Friends) Spring Marketplace Las Vegas 2027 Fifth annual Connect White Party, Morning Walk Club, invite-only hosted breakfasts Regionals Destination-hosted opening receptions, open night in the host city, sometimes Club Connect perks See Flagship marketplaces and Regional marketplaces for full detail. Club Connect Some regionals run Club Connect , a points-based rewards programme that unlocks perks such as priority seating, hospitality-suite access, and airport-hotel transportation. Ask the assigned Hosted Buyer Experience contact whether the applicable event includes it. Standing commitments across every event - Code of conduct : zero tolerance for harassment, discrimination, or violence at any live or digital touchpoint - Sustainability : reusable water bottles, recycling, locally sourced food, food donation to local charities - Quiet and prayer rooms available onsite - Governing law : New York, per Terms & Conditions, regardless of event location Onsite support - Concierge / help desk : schedule changes, appointment issues, deposit questions - Registration : badge pickup and issues - Hosted Buyer Experience Team : the hosted buyer's paired contact is onsite for the duration - Mid-event cancellation : connect.cancellations@informa.com If a hosted buyer finishes an appointment block early, encourage continued participation in the between-block moments such as coffee, walk-and-talks, and hosted lunches.","path":"/hosted-buyer-support/onsite-experience"},{"title":"Supporting hosted buyers","sidebarTitle":"Staff overview","description":"Internal staff reference for hosted-buyer eligibility, coverage, application, registration, appointments, attendance, and follow-up.","headings":["What staff should explain","Hosted-buyer profiles covered","The baseline bar","What's in this guide","The Connect portfolio"],"body":"Use this section when advising or supporting an event planner through the hosted-buyer lifecycle. It explains the programme while preserving internal qualification, system, and escalation context. Public hosted-buyer application for Connect Marketplace 2026: VisitCloud 3iyy5ec2sal59 . Programme page: hosted-buyer-experience. Turtl explainer: hosted-buyer-program. Other event forms: Public event sites. Audience: Connect staff and approved internal agents. This is not a public hosted-buyer guide. Staff may adapt customer-safe facts, but must not share internal IDs, webhook details, unresolved source conflicts, or unverified policy. What staff should explain Connect covers the published costs of attendance in exchange for the hosted buyer completing a committed appointment schedule with suppliers . In practical terms: - Roundtrip airfare, hotel for the event nights, and meals are covered - All networking events and the education programme are included - The hosted buyer takes a full schedule of one-to-one appointments matched to documented sourcing needs - A \\$200 refundable deposit is held and returned when the assigned appointments are completed, subject to the applicable event policy The deposit is the attendance-control mechanism for the programme. Missed appointments may forfeit the deposit and affect future eligibility. Hosted-buyer profiles covered Event planners with real sourcing authority for group business: - Corporate meeting and event planners - Association meeting professionals - Sports event organisers - Specialty planners: social, military, and fraternal groups - Faith-based , multicultural, LGBTQ\\+, and women's group planners - International (RTO) receptive tour operators booking inbound North American travel - Business travel buyers with meaningful travel-management budgets If the person represents an organisation selling services into the events industry, use Exhibitor Support instead. The baseline bar Across most tracks and events, the general qualification is: - 50 or more rooms on peak nights for the events the hosted buyer sources - Ability to rotate destinations nationally or regionally - A two-year verifiable event history Some tracks stack additional requirements on top - budget size, seniority, or event scale. Full detail is on Application and qualification. What's in this guide Tracks, planner types, and the profile Connect is looking for. Application steps, vetting, the deposit, and how the schedule commitment works. Travel, hotel, meals, networking, and education: what Connect covers and what the hosted buyer covers. Building the hosted buyer's supplier request list, travel arrangements, and pre-event admin. The shape of a Connect day, appointment blocks, and Club Connect rewards. Deposit return, follow-up, and returning next season. The Connect portfolio Nine live events run in the 2026-2027 season across the United States. See the Event calendar for dates, venues, and formats, and use the individual event pages to pick the fit for the hosted buyer's sourcing plan.","path":"/hosted-buyer-support/overview"},{"title":"Supporting hosted-buyer preparation","sidebarTitle":"Preparing for the event","description":"Internal checklist for helping hosted buyers prepare sourcing briefs, supplier requests, travel, and event administration.","headings":["Sourcing brief","Building the hosted buyer's request list","Travel arrangements","Pre-event admin","What to bring","Communicating with suppliers before the event"],"body":"Audience: Connect staff and approved internal agents. Use the assigned Hosted Buyer Experience contact and event-specific source for current travel and deadline instructions. Use this checklist to help a hosted buyer arrive with a clear sourcing brief, a workable request list, and completed pre-event administration. Sourcing brief Before supplier requests open, help the hosted buyer document what is being sourced. This shapes the appointment schedule. Which events does the hosted buyer need to source in the next 12 to 24 months? Capture dates, room-block sizes, meeting-space requirements, and budget range. First-tier city, second-tier city, resort, urban, warm-weather, drive market, direct flights required, accessibility considerations. Convention centre versus hotel-anchored, AV and production, unique venues for offsite events, F&B expectations. What matters most: rate, complimentary rooms, meeting space, F&B credit, attrition, cancellation flexibility. Building the hosted buyer's request list Once approved, the hosted buyer receives access to the attendee directory and event app with the available supplier list. Use category filters (destinations, hotels, venues, event tech, etc.) to focus. Suppliers marked as sponsors, hosts, or in the hosted buyer's track are more likely to match. Blue-sky requests are fine but low probability. Requests submitted after the deadline are matched against residual availability. Encourage the hosted buyer to submit early. Guide the hosted buyer toward a request list of roughly 1.5x to 2x the appointment count . Some requests will be unmatched because of interest, capacity, or fit, and the buffer helps the matching process build a full schedule. Travel arrangements - The hosted buyer's paired Hosted Buyer Experience contact will confirm the booking process for flights and the hotel block - Book within the published window and policy to avoid out-of-pocket costs - Coordinate ground transportation from the hosted buyer's home airport and any pre- or post-event stays - Confirm arrival and departure timing against day-one Regiception and day-three final appointment blocks Pre-event admin - Complete the hosted buyer's profile in the event platform so suppliers can find the hosted buyer - Upload a headshot if requested; used in the app and directory - Register for optional sessions : MPAC training, walk clubs, hosted excursions where offered - Note the code of conduct and any dress guidance - Save the concierge and cancellations emails in case anything changes late What to bring - Sourcing brief in a format the hosted buyer can quickly show a supplier: dates, sizes, must-haves - A method for capturing notes during appointments. The event app usually supports rating and note-taking; keep a backup - Business cards or digital equivalent - Comfortable shoes ; hosted buyers often walk more than expected between appointment rooms and networking spaces - A charger and battery pack for long days on the show floor Communicating with suppliers before the event Some suppliers will reach out before the event with introductory emails or pre-read material. This is normal. Hosted buyers are not obliged to reply to every message, but responding to priority targets can improve the appointment. Do not commit to specific dates, room blocks, or contract terms in pre-event outreach. Use the appointment to have that conversation properly, in person.","path":"/hosted-buyer-support/preparing-for-the-event"},{"title":"Hosted-buyer registration and operations","sidebarTitle":"Registration","description":"Internal lifecycle from Visit Create application through Salesforce registration, qualification, travel, appointments, and deposit handling.","headings":["Lifecycle","Registration Stage c","Deposit — do not collapse the versions","Invite transfers","What can go wrong in the sync","Next"],"body":"This is the detailed staff and agent path for a hosted buyer. Use the other Hosted Buyer Support pages for customer-safe explanations. Do not expose Salesforce IDs, webhook details, or known defects externally. Grounded on the Salesforce KB Visit Create integration (reviewed 26 June 2026), VisitRegistrationProcessor , Registration c field definitions, Visit Create workflows, Customer FAQs, and the public qualification page. Owners: hosted buyer experience — Rachel Piper; hosted buyer data — Bill Mathis; event delivery — Tinsley Conway. Lifecycle The planner applies through the Visit Create form for a specific expo. Customer-facing application URLs remain unconfirmed in this knowledge base—do not invent one. A refundable \\$200 deposit is collected as the no-show control. Visit sends a GET notification to the Salesforce Site endpoint /services/apexrest/visit/webhook . VisitWebhookReceiver pulls changed visitors, then enqueues VisitRegistrationProcessor . The processor skips anything that is not registration type 1ze3uz619 (Hosted Buyer). It looks up Marketplace c by Visit Expo Id c . If that field is blank on the marketplace, processing errors with \"No Marketplace c found for Visit Expo ID.\" Contact match is tiered: email → first+last name → first+last+company, then create (Hosted Buyer contact record type) under a matched or created Account ( Unknown Hosted Buyer Company fallback). Registration dedup is marketplace-scoped: Visit Visitor Id c + Visit Expo Id c → Registrant c + Marketplace c → email+ Marketplace c . Hosted Buyer Experience Managers vet role, two-year event history, sourcing scale, and rotation. Track bars are on Business model. Approved buyers are paired with an HBX contact. From the FAQ library: TripEasy sends the flight invite ( travelnoreply@tripeasy.com ); connectmeetings@its.com is the travel team. Hotel is booked via the provided link. Appointment-portal access opens closer to the event. Which platform (Map Your Show / MatchPoint / custom) applies to each event is still unconfirmed — leave that TODO. Appointments are pre-scheduled. Completing the assigned schedule is the condition for returning the deposit. Only Registration Stage c = 'Fully Registered' counts as confirmed attendance in metrics. Registration Stage c From the Salesforce KB Record Types page. Stage Meaning --- --- Registering Initial form started Pending Confirmation Awaiting qualification or approval Registered Confirmed, not all steps complete Fully Registered All requirements met (travel booked, and so on). This is the attendance count. Verbal Verbal commitment, not yet formally registered Cancelled / Declined / Stopped / Date Conflict Terminal Visit maps registrationState into Cancelled / Pending Confirmation / Registered . Fully Registered is a later human or process step, not the raw webhook state. Also on the record: Hosted Buyer Type c (Anchor Planner, End User, Third Party, Non-Hosted Buyer), Qualification Status c , Visit keys ( Visit Registration Key c , Visit Visitor Id c , Visit Order Id c , Visit Expo Id c ), Registrant c , Planner Account c , Marketplace c , Meeting Event c (Campaign). Registration c has 237 fields (225 custom). Do not invent a field. Use sf sobject describe if staff need a field that is not listed here. Deposit — do not collapse the versions Source What it says --- --- Customer FAQ (confidence 0.85) Refund check at the conclusion of 1:1 appointments; must attend all scheduled 1:1s Archived VIP playbook Refunded on-site at the end of the event; example \\$200 Public qualification page Full refund on completing the assigned schedule; 30+ day cancel = refund or credit; 7 days or fewer = forfeit plus one hotel night Public Terms & Conditions (see Known issues) 57+ days: refund minus 10% admin; within 56 days or no-show: full fee forfeited Report the version that matches the registrant type and the event page. Do not average them. Invite transfers FAQ and playbook: transfers to a qualified colleague (typically director-level or above) require rep approval . Reps phrase this as having received approval to transfer the invite. What can go wrong in the sync - Marketplace missing Visit Expo Id c . - Name-based Account matching creating near-duplicate Accounts when company strings vary. - Processor runs in the Salesforce Site (guest) context. - Monitor Visit Webhook Log c , Visit Webhook Activity c , and Setup → Apex Jobs. Do not put Visit API keys, named-credential passwords, or guest-user permission sets on this page. The Named Credential is Visit API . Never share a full hosted-buyer contact list. Brand-and-title samples only. Never treat Registered as attendance. Next What agents may and may not say, and how they should answer.","path":"/hosted-buyer-support/registration-and-operations"},{"title":"Hosted-buyer response library","sidebarTitle":"Response library","description":"Internal response routes, customer-safe sources, disclosure boundaries, and escalations for hosted-buyer questions.","headings":["Response routes","Customer-safe versus internal-only"],"body":"Use this page to choose the right source before replying to a hosted buyer or applicant. Response routes Question Start with Then check --- --- --- Does the planner qualify? Eligibility Event track and Hosted Buyer Experience team What is covered? Coverage Event-specific booking rules What must the buyer complete? Commitments Application and applicable cancellation policy Where is an application or registration? Application and approval Current event source; do not invent a URL What happens before or onsite? Preparation and Onsite experience Live agenda and assigned HBX contact Is there a standard answer? Customer FAQs Confidence and When-not-to-use rule Customer-safe versus internal-only Published eligibility, coverage, event facts, appointment expectations, approved application guidance, and approved FAQ language. Salesforce and Visit IDs, webhook routing, sync defects, internal confidence scores, private source locations, and unresolved ownership gaps. Deposit disputes, cancellation exceptions, billing, complaints, named attendee data, legal terms, and unverified travel promises require human escalation.","path":"/hosted-buyer-support/response-library"},{"title":"What staff may communicate","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Customer-safe hosted-buyer information versus internal-only context.","headings":["Staff guidance","Customer-safe information","Internal-only context","Limits and escalation","Related pages"],"body":"- Purpose: Decide what may be said to a hosted buyer or applicant. - Audience: Hosted Buyer Experience, operations, and approved internal agents. - Canonical source: Hosted Buyer Support playbooks and the Customer FAQs extract. - Owner: Hosted buyer experience — Rachel Piper. - Source status: Canonical for the boundary; event coverage remains event-specific. - Last reviewed: 27 August 2026. Staff guidance Use this split before sending a reply. Verify the event and registrant type before restating coverage or cancellation. Customer-safe information - Published eligibility from Who qualifies - Published coverage from What is covered - Event facts from the Event calendar after checking Known issues - Appointment expectations on Hosted buyer commitments - Approved application guidance and FAQ short answers Internal-only context - Salesforce and Visit IDs - Webhook routing and sync defects - Internal confidence scores - Private source locations - Unresolved ownership gaps Limits and escalation Do not forward a full Hosted Buyer Support page to a customer. See Exceptions and escalation. Related pages Routing table for hosted-buyer questions. Hard limits for anyone answering on Connect's behalf.","path":"/hosted-buyer-support/what-staff-may-communicate"},{"title":"Find the right answer, owner, or source","sidebarTitle":"Welcome","description":"The task-led starting point for Connect Meetings & Events staff and approved internal agents.","headings":["Start with a task","Essential references"],"body":"Use the Company Brain to reach the best available answer and the person or system that owns it. Start with the task in front of you rather than guessing how the organisation files it. Start with a task Qualification, packages, appointments, registration, event delivery, response guidance, and escalation. Eligibility, coverage, commitments, application, event support, response guidance, and escalation. Source-dated dates, venues, formats, policies, and known discrepancies. Route a question, approval, exception, or handoff to the responsible person or team. Salesforce, Visit Create, Company OS, metrics, dashboards, integrations, and system boundaries. Retrieval rules, disclosure limits, agent inventory, tooling, Elysia, and automation guidance. This site contains internal context. Use the customer-safe wording in the support playbooks; do not send a full page to a customer. Essential references Browse common staff questions when you do not know the right section or terminology. Grounded supplier and hosted-buyer answers with confidence and when-not-to-use guidance. Learn which page wins, how sources are classified, and when staff must escalate. Identify the system that owns a fact and check its source status and review cadence. Check unresolved conflicts, data-quality problems, ownership gaps, and unverified material. Review current initiatives without mistaking planned work for current-state guidance. The Company Brain is a derived mirror of Salesforce ( connect-prod ), Notion Company OS, and the Live Events wiki. The owning system remains authoritative.","path":"/"},{"title":"Agent retrieval rules","sidebarTitle":"","description":"How agents should retrieve, cite, and escalate from this site. Not a new-hire page.","headings":["Conventions used here","What this site is not","Next"],"body":"These rules are inherited from the Company OS AI retrieval policy. They apply to anything grounded on this site. People new to the business should start on How to use this brain instead. Every major information type has exactly one canonical home, recorded in the source-of-truth registry. Entries are marked Canonical, Working, or Deprecated. If the material you are relying on is Working or Deprecated, disclose that rather than presenting it as settled. Dates, venues, pricing, eligibility, and cancellation policy for any event must come from the Events portfolio or the Live Events wiki it mirrors. Never reconstruct an event fact from a project page, an email, or memory. Ground customer-facing answers on the Customer FAQs extract. Surface the confidence score when it is below 0.8 or blank. If the pair has a When not to use rule — discounts, invoice amounts, attendee lists — stop and escalate. Never quote specific rates beyond published package pricing. Never share full attendee contact lists — brand and title samples only. Never commit to payment terms, discounts, or contract changes. Escalate anything touching billing, legal, or a complaint to a human. See Guardrails. Ground each answer in a specific page and cite it. Findings mined from email carry confidence scores; surface the score when the confidence is below high. Several event sites publish contradictory dates, contacts, and cancellation policies. This site preserves both versions and flags them on Known issues. Report the conflict; do not pick a winner. Conventions used here Convention Meaning --- --- Backticked names like Total Spend This Product Year c A literal Salesforce API field name in connect-prod Product year The calendar year a product is sold for , not when the deal closed Area vs project Areas are ongoing departments; projects are time-bound and end Canonical / Working / Deprecated Truth levels from the source-of-truth registry A note in square brackets An unresolved discrepancy carried over from the source, deliberately not reconciled Field-level terms live on the Salesforce glossary. What this site is not It does not hold record-level data. No account lists, no contact details for customers, no deal values by name, no attendee rosters. Those live in Salesforce and are governed by access control there. This site documents structure, definitions, ownership, and process. Next API names, objects, and how everyday words map onto connect-prod.","path":"/operations/ai/agent-retrieval"},{"title":"Agent roster","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Agentforce agents in connect-prod, plus the Company Brain ingest job on this Mac.","headings":["What grounds them","Prompts and examples","Automation already in place","Recording a new agent or asset","Next"],"body":"Six agents are recorded in the AI & Agent Library on the Salesforce Agentforce platform in connect-prod , all with status Active , all owned by Mac Kitchin. Deployment status per Agentforce agent is an open confirmation item on the rollout project. A seventh asset, the Company Brain ingest agent, is a launchd job on this Mac. It is not Agentforce. Developer name Agentforce Employee Agent . The employee-facing agent for internal question-answering and task execution. The general-purpose front door for staff. Data quality and hygiene. Given that Reason Lost c is blank on 421 of 424 lost deals, this agent has the clearest brief in the roster. Salesforce Flow automation. Sits alongside the 92 active flows and 235 automation records already in the org. Analysis and notebook-style work against org data. Opportunity-focused: pipeline hygiene, deal context, and stage progression. Sales coaching against real conversation data - Chorus conversation intelligence is already in the org. Launchd job. Drop-folder and Mac-only AgentMail compile to Notion drafts over REST; Claude Code is used only for engagement replies and Published+Verified MDX promotion. What grounds them An agent is only as good as what it is allowed to read. Retrieval scope by agent type: Agent type May ground on Must not ground on --- --- --- Customer-facing Customer FAQs extract (29 pairs; registry still says ~31), Knowledge Articles, the Live Events wiki Raw email, project pages, marketing site copy, anything marked Deprecated, FAQ rows marked When not to use Employee-facing Company OS canonical sources, the Salesforce Knowledge Base, Supplier Knowledge Intelligence Anything marked Deprecated without saying so Data and ops connect-prod directly, plus the data model and metrics definitions Cached or derived figures presented as live The stale-facts problem is the central risk. Multiple event sites publish contradictory dates, hosts, venues, and cancellation policies - see Known issues. Restricting customer-facing chatbots to the Live Events wiki is the mitigation, and it only works if the wiki stays current. The refresh SOP is not optional. Prompts and examples Two prompt collections have been merged into one canonical index: the Prompt Library from the Salesforce Knowledge Base and Agent Prompts & Examples. Merging them fully is an open item on the Company OS project. Automation already in place The AI roster sits on top of substantial existing automation, not on a blank slate. --- --- Automation records 235 Active flows 92 Documented Salesforce automation is catalogued; Notion and Zapier automations are not yet documented Record every new workflow in the AI & Agent Library before launch, not after. Undocumented automation is how the 235-record catalogue became hard to reason about in the first place. Recording a new agent or asset The AI & Agent Library takes one row per asset with these properties: Property Values --- --- Asset Type Agent, Prompt, MCP Tool Platform Salesforce, Notion, Cursor, HubSpot, Other Status Active, and other lifecycle states Owner A person from Master People Docs Link The canonical documentation page Next The MCP servers, integrations, and platforms the AI stack runs on.","path":"/operations/ai/agents"},{"title":"Automation roadmap","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Nine automation candidates worth roughly 1,405 hours a year, prioritised.","headings":["The backlog","Why these three are P1","The renewal case for P2","Implementation patterns","Prerequisites before anything ships","Next"],"body":"Nine workflows were identified from the supplier email analysis, with an estimated total opportunity of ~1,405 hours a year . Each was scored on frequency, judgement required, and whether a grounded source already exists to answer from. The backlog Priority Workflow Implementation Est. hrs/yr --- --- --- --- P1 FAQ deflection - dates, logistics, packages Agentforce agent 250 P1 Campaign analytics and reporting Self-service plus agent 300 P1 Attendee details collection and follow-up Agentforce agent 200 P2 Renewal and rebooking outreach Agentforce agent 150 P2 Buyer list sample responses Agentforce agent 125 P2 Creative and media kit assembly Email template 120 P2 Payment reminders and dunning Salesforce Flow 110 P2 Hotel booking confirmation Salesforce Flow 80 P3 Editorial and print deadline and rate responses Self-service doc 70 Why these three are P1 The single largest time sink. Suppliers ask \"how did my campaign perform?\" and a rep assembles the answer by hand. Split into two parts: self-service reporting so the supplier can pull it themselves, plus an agent that assembles and explains the numbers for those who ask. Dependency : reporting dashboards in connect-prod are currently marked Working , not canonical, and are not indexed in Notion. A dashboard index page is the prerequisite. The two highest-frequency supplier questions in the entire corpus are \"when and where is Connect Marketplace 2026?\" (confidence 0.90) and \"what is included in a supplier package?\" (0.82). Both are pure lookups with an existing grounded source. There is no judgement in answering them, only latency. Dependency : the agent must be wired to the Customer FAQs library and the Live Events wiki, and to nothing else. The number-one question by frequency: \"what attendee details do I submit after registering?\" (0.86). Entirely mechanical - collect, validate, remind, escalate on non-response. It is also the moment where supplier data quality is either established or lost for the whole event cycle. The renewal case for P2 The P2 tier looks lower-value by hours, but renewal and rebooking outreach deserves a second look. Renewals win at 84.6 percent versus 67.2 percent for new business, and average \\$18.8K versus \\$10.1K. Separately, 79 supplier deals worth \\$660K expired without a decision - not lost, just never followed up. An automation that reliably touches every expiring deal before it times out is worth considerably more than its 150-hour estimate suggests, because the hours saved are not the point. The recovered pipeline is. Implementation patterns Pattern When to use it Examples here --- --- --- Agentforce agent Conversational, variable phrasing, needs grounded retrieval FAQ deflection, attendee details, renewal outreach, buyer list samples Salesforce Flow Deterministic, trigger-based, no natural language needed Payment reminders and dunning, hotel booking confirmation Email template Repeatable assembly of the same materials Creative and media kit assembly Self-service document Reference information a supplier can look up Editorial and print deadlines and rates Three of the nine are not AI at all. Reaching for an agent when a Flow or a well-written document would do is the most common way an automation programme burns credibility. Prerequisites before anything ships Customer FAQs and the Live Events wiki must be canonical and refreshed. An agent grounded on stale event facts is worse than no agent. No rates, no contact lists, escalate billing and legal. Defining evaluation and guardrail checks is an open item on the rollout project. Handoff requires knowing who owns the account. On the BizBash side that is currently 26 rep assignments against 20,876 accounts - see data consolidation. One row in the AI & Agent Library, with owner, status, and docs link. Next The four initiatives in flight, in the order they depend on each other.","path":"/operations/ai/automation-roadmap"},{"title":"Company Brain ingest agent","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Launchd agent on this Mac that compiles dropped files and Mac-only AgentMail into Notion drafts, then mirrors Company Brain MDX after Published and Verified.","headings":["What it does","Where it runs","Operator commands","Guardrails the agent must keep"],"body":"This is the always-on compile-at-ingest process for Connect Company Brain. It is a launchd job on Mac Kitchin's machine, not an Agentforce agent in connect-prod . File parse, guardrail gating, classification, and Notion draft writes are ordinary Python. Claude Agent SDK is used only for Mac email replies and for mirroring Company Brain MDX after a page is Published and Verified. The site remains a derived mirror. This agent proposes Notion drafts. It does not silently publish, invent facts, reconcile conflicts, or copy record-level customer data. What it does Input Action --- --- File in source/inbox/incoming/ Parse, guardrail gate, classify, move to processed/ or rejected/ Mail to company-brain@agentmail.to from mac.kitchin@informa.com Save real attachments into incoming/ ; reply in-thread Accepted file Master Docs row at Draft / Unverified , or a Live Events wiki child for event facts Master Docs Published and Verified Update the matching Company Brain MDX Mail from anyone other than Mac is ignored. Outbound copies from company-brain@agentmail.to are not treated as engagement. Inline signature images are ignored. Company Brain MDX is not written from Draft, In Review, or Published-but-Unverified rows. Both Status = Published and Verification Status = Verified are required. Where it runs --- --- Host This Mac (launchd LaunchAgent) Label com.connect.company-brain-ingest Plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.connect.company-brain-ingest.plist Code source/.grok/ingest/ Secrets source/.env (gitignored): ANTHROPIC API KEY , AGENTMAIL API KEY , NOTION TOKEN Notion drafts REST ( https://api.notion.com ). Master Docs rows at Draft / Unverified, owned by Mac Kitchin. Event facts go to a Live Events wiki child. No Claude. Claude Engagement replies and Published+Verified MDX promotion only. claude-sonnet-4-6 via https://api.anthropic.com . Does not inherit the local Claude Code model ( kimi-k2.5 ). Logs source/inbox/ logs/ (gitignored) Menu bar com.connect.company-brain-ingest-tray — ● Ingest when the job is running, ○ when it is not. The menu lists today and this week. KeepAlive and RunAtLoad are on. The process loops about every 60 seconds. Empty ticks do not call Claude. Accepted files become a local draft packet, then a Notion page over REST ( NOTION TOKEN ). A sidecar that fails the Notion write twice is left in inbox/ processed/ until notion attempts is reset. About every fifth tick the job reads Notion Status and Verification Status. The promote agent runs only for pages that are already Published and Verified . AgentMail attachments that return JSON metadata are fetched via download url ; metadata is never written into incoming/ . A systemd unit template lives at source/.grok/ingest/systemd/company-brain-ingest.service for a Linux host. This machine uses launchd. Operator commands From source/ : To stop the ingest job: launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.connect.company-brain-ingest . The menu-bar icon is a separate LaunchAgent ( com.connect.company-brain-ingest-tray ). Quit from the menu removes the icon only; it does not stop ingest. Restart ingest from the menu with Restart ingest job . Guardrails the agent must keep - Event dates, venues, eligibility, and cancellation policy land in Live Events, not as a silent overwrite of /events/ . - Conflicts stay dual-sourced on Known issues. - Rejects for pii roster , record crm , secrets , unpublished rates , empty , and unsupported never create a Notion page. - Do not quote unpublished rates. Do not attach source files to email. - Do not git commit or deploy from the daemon. Owner: Mac Kitchin. Escalate billing, contracts, discounts, and complaints per Who owns what.","path":"/operations/ai/company-brain-ingest"},{"title":"Elysia API","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Informa Elysia HTTP API: environments, OAuth, chat stream, agents, content, and how the local console talks to it.","headings":["Environments","Authentication","Chat (what we actually call)","Agents, content, apps","Local clients","Community vs Professional","Next"],"body":"Machine catalog: GET https://api.stage.ai.informa.com/v1/ai/openapi.json (OpenAPI 3.1.0). A copy lives at /Users/william.kitchin/repos/elysia-console/elysia-openapi-spec.json (info title FastAPI , version 0.1.0 ). Slimmer local catalog: elysia-console/endpoints.json and skills/elysia-api/references/endpoints.md . Grounded on those files, ~/Elysia API Docs/Elysia API Complete Documentation.md , the elysia-api skill, and elysia-console/HANDOFF.md . Last local review 12 August 2026 . Prefer the live OpenAPI on stage when a local copy disagrees. Environments Environment API base Token / IdP --- --- --- Stage (console default) https://api.stage.ai.informa.com https://idp.dev.ai.informa.com/oauth2/token Dev (complete API notes) https://api.dev.ai.informa.com same IdP domain; AWS eu-west-1 Prod Unconfirmed in the console handoff Unconfirmed Set ELYSIA API BASE and ELYSIA TOKEN URL rather than hard-coding. Authentication Use HTTP Basic ( client id:client secret ) or the form body. Response is a Bearer JWT ( expires in 3600 in the notes). Send Authorization: Bearer . Cloudflare Access ( ELYSIA CF USER / ELYSIA CF PASS ) is a second layer for docs and the console, not a substitute for the JWT. Required on chat: app ID ( appId / ELYSIA APP ID ). Chat (what we actually call) Console and Web App use: SSE stream. Required body fields in the skill: appId , query . Common optional fields: name of model , model ( azure / aws ), chat session , collection name , output type ( markdown / html ), response language , tokens , metadata . The console handoff: do not switch to /v2/ai/chat/completion . That path was previously unavailable on stage. The complete API markdown also lists /v2 and /v3 stream completion paths. If you need those, confirm against live OpenAPI. Do not pick a winner here. Related chat ops in the skill: PATCH /v1/ai/chat/archive , PATCH /v1/ai/chat/pin , DELETE /v1/ai/chat/conversation/session/ session id . The Elysia chat API is a plain completion endpoint. Native tool calling is not assumed. The Web App and console bridge drive an agent loop: the model emits a tool call, local MCP runs it, the result is sent back. Agents, content, apps From the skill and endpoint reference (v1 and often v2): Area Examples --- --- Agents GET/POST /v1/ai/agents , get/update/delete by id, status ( published / unpublished ), permissions, search, favourite, rating, brand-agent Content / ingestion POST /v1/ai-ingestion/process-documents , GET /v1/ai-ingestion/health , GET /v1/ai/content , delete files on an app collection Assets POST/GET/DELETE /v1/ai/assets/ asset type ( image , document , audio , video ) Apps GET /v1/ai/apps/list , GET /v1/ai/apps/info?id= Dashboard GET /v1/ai/dashboard/powerbi Health GET /v1/ai/health , GET /v1/ai/openapi.json Local clients Postman: elysia-apis-external.postman collection.json plus elysia-external-dev.postman environment.json in the console repo and ~/Elysia API Docs . Do not commit filled environments. MCP profiles the console can advertise (read-only by default): Salesforce , Visit Create , Power BI . Mutating tools need MCP ALLOWED TOOLS or MCP ALLOW ALL TOOLS=1 . Community vs Professional See the Guidebook summary on Elysia. Community is for internal testing (5,000 calls/month in the guidebook). Customer-facing Connect products need Professional access after the checklist. Integration support: elysia@informa.com . Next CLI 0.3.0: Claude Code through the Informa AI gateway, compression, models.","path":"/operations/ai/elysia-api"},{"title":"Elysia Code","sidebarTitle":"","description":"elysia-code 0.3.0: Informa CLI that points Claude Code at the AI gateway, with context compression and model routing.","headings":["Install","Commands","Context compression (0.3.0)","Models (0.3.0)","This machine","Next"],"body":"Elysia Code is Informa's CLI for Claude Code. It configures Claude Code to send traffic through the Informa AI gateway so you do not manage provider keys yourself. Governance, tracing, and billing apply to every model. Current version on this machine and in Notion: 0.3.0 . Grounded on Notion Elysia Code 0.3.0 (verification: verified), Dev/elysia-code-latest/elysia-code.py ( CLI VERSION = \"0.3.0\" ), the desktop Elysia Code.pdf download page, and ~/.claude/commands/elysia- .md . 12 August 2026. Do not copy workspace ID, config ID, user ID, or API key from the Notion credentials table onto this site. Install 1. Node.js 18+ and npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code . 2. Download from pilots.ai.informa.com/elysia-code. 3. Run setup-mac.sh or setup-windows.cmd . 4. elysia-code --init (prompts for workspace, config, user, API key). Or pass those flags non-interactively — still do not write the values into MDX. 5. claude to start. Compression is on by default from 0.3.0. Network: Zscaler plus Informa VPN. SSL or network errors: confirm both, restart the terminal. Quota from the Notion page: \\$200 / month , 100 requests / minute , reset on the first of the month. Increase: elysia-dev@informa.com . Updating: elysia-code --update . 0.2.0 users do not re-init; run --compression-enable if it did not flip on after the first command. Commands Command What it does --- --- elysia-code --init First-time gateway, SSL, compression, Claude Code elysia-code --update Upgrade CLI and compression; restarts the proxy elysia-code --config Config, model, private mode, usage elysia-code --models List models; show the active one elysia-code --model Switch model (restart Claude Code) elysia-code --private true\\ false Hide inputs/outputs from traces. Usage metadata still bills elysia-code --compression-enable Turn compression on elysia-code --compression-disable Direct to gateway elysia-code --compression-stats Status and recent proxy log elysia-code --help All flags Inside Claude Code (installed at --init ): Slash command What it does --- --- /elysia-config Config and live usage (must show the full CLI output) /elysia-model List or switch models /elysia-compression Stats, enable/disable. Dashboard: http://localhost:8787/dashboard Context compression (0.3.0) Enabled by default. Local layer strips build logs, repeated file reads, JSON payloads, tool output, and other noise before the model. Notion claims 60–95% fewer tokens on tool-heavy turns and about 30% lower session cost. Nothing is permanently lost; originals stay in a local cache. State lives under ~/.elysia ( compression.json , compression.log ). A LaunchAgent com.informa.elysia-compression.plist exists on this Mac. Models (0.3.0) All routed through the gateway. Default in the Notion page and PDF: zai.glm-5 . kimi-k2.5 , claude-sonnet-5 , minimax-m2.5 , claude-sonnet-4-6 , claude-sonnet-4-5 , claude-haiku-4-5 , gpt-5-4 , gpt-5-4-mini , gpt-5-4-nano , deepseek-v3 , mistral-large-3 , llama-4-maverick-17b-instruct , llama-4-scout-17b-instruct . This list is the Code gateway catalog. The Elysia API chat catalog ( gpt-4o , AWS Claude IDs, DeepSeek-V3-0324, …) is a different surface. Do not mix the two when setting name of model on /v1/ai/chat/stream/completion . This machine Path Role --- --- ~/.local/bin/elysia-code Installed CLI /Users/william.kitchin/Dev/elysia-code-latest/ Source / latest zip extract ~/.claude/commands/elysia-config.md (and model, compression) Slash-command prompts Applications Elysia.app and Elysia — Salesforce Assistant.app Desktop wrappers; not documented further here Next Nine automation candidates, prioritised, with estimated hours.","path":"/operations/ai/elysia-code"},{"title":"Elysia","sidebarTitle":"Platform overview","description":"Informa's internal AI platform: the API, Elysia Code gateway for Claude Code, and how Connect uses it locally.","headings":["How Connect uses it","Two API products","Auth (two layers)","Local inventory (this machine)","What not to do","Next"],"body":"Elysia is Informa's managed AI platform . It sits between our tools and LLM providers. It routes models, keeps traffic on Informa infrastructure, and applies governance, tracing, and billing. Connect uses it in two ways: as an HTTP API for chat, agents, and content, and as Elysia Code , a CLI that points Claude Code at the Informa AI gateway. Captured from local files and Notion, 12 August 2026. Sources: elysia-console , Elysia-Web-App , ~/Elysia API Docs , Dev/elysia-code-latest (CLI 0.3.0), Notion Elysia Code 0.3.0 (verified) and Elysia API Guidebook . Do not put API keys, client secrets, workspace IDs, or bearer tokens on this site. Credentials live in ~/Elysia API Docs/Elysia Credentials.txt and the Notion credentials block — retrieve them there, never copy them here. This is not Agentforce. Agentforce agents run in connect-prod . Elysia is the Informa-wide AI estate. They can meet: Elysia chat can call local MCP tools (Salesforce, Visit Create, Power BI) through the console bridge. How Connect uses it Surface Local path Role --- --- --- Elysia API Stage: https://api.stage.ai.informa.com . Dev also appears in the complete API notes: https://api.dev.ai.informa.com . Prod host is unconfirmed in the console handoff. Chat, agents, content, assets, apps Elysia Code CLI elysia-code 0.3.0 at ~/.local/bin/elysia-code . Download: pilots.ai.informa.com/elysia-code Configures Claude Code to use the Informa AI gateway Elysia Console /Users/william.kitchin/repos/elysia-console Local API tester + chat UI + MCP bridge. python3 elysia console.py → http://localhost:8765/ Elysia Web App /Users/william.kitchin/repos/Elysia-Web-App Next.js Salesforce assistant. Read-only SOQL via MCP, PDF export Two API products The Elysia API Guidebook (Company OS, status Done) distinguishes: Community API Professional API --- --- --- For Internal use cases and developer testing (performance, pen-test, UAT) Customer-facing solutions after the checklist Hosting Informa infrastructure only Same, after review SLA / security Not guaranteed for customer-facing use Requires SLAs, GDPR, AI Governance, cost code, security review, usage metrics Monthly cap (guidebook) 5,000 calls per instance After re-engagement with the Elysia product team Support Integration is not supported by the Elysia product team Via the product team Contact elysia@informa.com Professional access form (product team) Auth (two layers) From the console handoff and the elysia-api skill. Do not treat Cloudflare Access as the API bearer token. 1. OAuth2 client credentials → Cognito JWT. Token URL used locally: https://idp.dev.ai.informa.com/oauth2/token . Send as Authorization: Bearer … . 2. Cloudflare Access / basic auth protects the docs and some console flows ( CF Authorization cookie). It is not the Elysia JWT. An app ID is required on chat and several other calls ( ELYSIA APP ID ). Get it from the platform team or the credentials file — not from this page. Local inventory (this machine) Searched 12 August 2026. Useful trees only. Path What it is --- --- /Users/william.kitchin/repos/elysia-console Console, OpenAPI copy, Postman collection, skills/elysia-api /Users/william.kitchin/repos/Elysia-Web-App Salesforce assistant web app /Users/william.kitchin/Elysia API Docs Guidebook PDF/PPTX, complete markdown, OpenAPI, Postman. Also contains Elysia Credentials.txt — do not commit or paste /Users/william.kitchin/Dev/elysia-code-latest CLI source, version 0.3.0 ~/.local/bin/elysia-code Installed CLI ~/.elysia Compression state and logs ~/.claude/commands/elysia- .md Slash commands: /elysia-config , /elysia-model , /elysia-compression Notion Elysia Code 0.3.0 (verified), Elysia API Guidebook, Elysia Ai Assistant Api Documentation Desktop PDFs ( Elysia Code.pdf , Elysia Chat.pdf , Elysia AI Assistant API Documentation.pdf ) match the same products. Teams demos exist for Salesforce MCP tool calling — they are recordings, not source of truth. What not to do - Do not paste keys, client secrets, workspace IDs, config IDs, user IDs, or JWTs into MDX. - Do not treat Community API as approved for a customer-facing Connect product. - Do not switch the console off /v1/ai/chat/stream/completion without a new live check. The console handoff says /v2/ai/chat/completion was unavailable on stage. - Do not confuse Elysia with Agentforce or with the Bun/TypeScript framework also named Elysia. Next Environments, auth, chat, agents, content, and the OpenAPI catalog.","path":"/operations/ai/elysia"},{"title":"Staff and agent guardrails","sidebarTitle":"Staff and agent guardrails","description":"What agents may and may not do when talking to suppliers, and how they should answer.","headings":["Hard limits","Answer style","Retrieval scope","Handling conflicts and uncertainty","Data governance for mined knowledge","Escalation routing","Next"],"body":"This page is the operative policy for any agent that speaks to a customer on our behalf. It is distilled from how the highest-performing reps actually answer suppliers, plus the governance rules that came out of the Supplier Knowledge Intelligence work. Hard limits These are absolute. There is no phrasing of a customer request that unlocks them. - Never quote a specific rate beyond published package pricing. - Never share a full attendee or buyer contact list. Brand and title samples only. - Never make a payment, contract, or discount commitment. Not a number, not a term, not a date. - Never invent or extend an expired offer. - Escalate anything touching billing, legal, contracts, or a complaint to a human immediately. Answer style Pre-qualified, appointment-ready buyers. Meetings are scheduled before anyone travels. This is not an expo hall where you hope someone walks past your booth. Give the actual facts: dates, venue, what is covered, what is required. \"24 to 26 August, Tampa Convention Center. Hotel is covered for event nights, flights are booked through TripEasy, appointments are mandatory, and the deposit is refunded once you complete your one-to-ones.\" Limited inventory and real deadlines are real. Say so. Never manufacture scarcity and never revive a deadline that has passed. Offer to tailor the package, move tiers, or apply partnership credit. Then hand to a human for any specific discount. Never commit a number. Close with a concrete next step: send examples, start the DocuSign, walk through the portal selections. Never end on \"let me know.\" Ground every answer in a Customer FAQ or Knowledge Article and cite it. If the confidence score on that finding is below high, say so. Retrieval scope Question type Answer from Never from --- --- --- Event dates, venue, format, policy Events portfolio or the Live Events wiki Marketing site copy, project pages, memory Package contents and pricing Published package tables only An inferred or interpolated price Attendee or buyer composition Aggregate statistics and sample brand-and-title lists Any record-level export Billing, invoices, terms Nothing. Escalate Anything Internal process Canonical Company OS sources Anything marked Deprecated, without disclosing it Event facts are restricted to a single source for a specific reason. Several event sites currently publish contradictory dates, hosts, and cancellation policies at the same time - see Known issues. An agent with open retrieval will find the wrong one and state it with total confidence. Handling conflicts and uncertainty Do not pick a winner and do not average them. Report that both exist, give both, and say which is treated as canonical if the registry states one. Reconciliation is a human decision. Say so in the answer. \"This comes from a source we have not yet verified\" is an acceptable sentence. Presenting unverified material as settled is not. Single-source items are never treated as policy. Minimum corroboration for anything stated as policy is two independent sources. Say you do not know and route to the owner. Who owns what is the routing table. A confident wrong answer to a supplier costs a renewal, and renewals carry 79 percent of supplier revenue. Data governance for mined knowledge Rules applied to anything derived from the supplier email corpus: - Raw email bodies are never stored - only generalised, PII-redacted knowledge. - Pricing, legal, contract, payment, complaint, and PII content is flagged High or Critical and routed to the Knowledge Candidates review queue rather than auto-published. - Every finding retains its source Salesforce record IDs for auditability. - Every finding carries a confidence score and an occurrence signal. Escalation routing Topic Goes to --- --- Billing, invoices, payment terms Human, Stephen Han (Finance) Contracts, legal, T&Cs Human, via Legal routing Complaints Human, named account owner Specific discounts The account's sales owner Event operations and logistics Events team, Tinsley Conway's area Hosted buyer eligibility and experience Rachel Piper's area Data or CRM questions Mac Kitchin Next The six Agentforce agents in connect-prod and what each one is for.","path":"/operations/ai/guardrails"},{"title":"AI and automation","sidebarTitle":"AI and automation overview","description":"Internal map of guardrails, agents, retrieval rules, MCP tooling, Elysia, and the automation roadmap.","headings":[],"body":"AI work in Connect spans production Agentforce agents, MCP-connected internal assistants, the Informa Elysia platform, and workflow automation. Non-negotiable communication, data, pricing, and escalation boundaries. The documented Agentforce agents, plus the Company Brain ingest job on this Mac. Drop-folder and Mac-only AgentMail compile-at-ingest on this machine. Connected tools, platforms, registered assets, and undocumented territory. How an agent selects sources, handles conflicts, cites answers, and stops. Informa's managed AI platform, HTTP API, and Elysia Code gateway. Prioritised opportunities, prerequisites, and implementation patterns. An agent being listed as Active does not prove that every production channel is deployed or safe for unrestricted use. Verify deployment, grounding, permissions, and evaluation evidence before launch.","path":"/operations/ai/overview"},{"title":"MCP and tooling","sidebarTitle":"","description":"The MCP servers, integrations, and platforms the AI stack runs on.","headings":["MCP servers in use","Registered assets","Sales-side AI tooling in the field","Companion skill","Undocumented territory","Next"],"body":"The AI work runs across two surfaces: Agentforce inside Salesforce for production agents, and MCP-connected assistants (Cursor, Claude) for build, analysis, and knowledge work. MCP servers in use Server Purpose --- --- salesforce / salesforce-mcp-server / salesforce-dx Query and administer connect-prod , run SOQL, deploy metadata notion-workspace Read and write the Company OS Notion workspace informa-connect Informa Connect internal tooling visit-create Visit Create API v2. See Visit Create API Elysia (HTTP + Code CLI) Informa AI platform. See Elysia HubSpotDev HubSpot CLI for projects, apps, and CMS development Playwright Browser automation for testing and site crawling vercel Deployment supabase Database huggingface-skills ML tooling mainframe Platform plugin The Playwright server is not incidental. The Live Events wiki pages are built from full site crawls of informaconnect.com - the Connect Marketplace page alone was refreshed from 24 pages on 3 August 2026. That crawl is how most of the source conflicts on Known issues were found. Registered assets Everything is recorded in the AI & Agent Library with an owner, platform, status, and docs link. Asset Type Platform --- --- --- Salesforce MCP Servers (Cursor) MCP Tool Cursor Notion MCP Server (Cursor) MCP Tool Cursor Playwright MCP MCP Tool Cursor Visit Create MCP MCP Tool Other Informa Connect MCP MCP Tool Other HubSpot Dev MCP MCP Tool HubSpot Supabase MCP MCP Tool Other Vercel MCP MCP Tool Other Prompt Library (Salesforce KB) Prompt Salesforce Agent Prompts & Examples Prompt Notion Connect (SF Agent) Agent Salesforce Data Steward Agent Agent Salesforce Flow Automation Agent Agent Salesforce Notebook AI Agent Agent Salesforce Opportunity Agent Agent Salesforce Agentforce Sales Coach Agent Salesforce Company Brain ingest agent Agent Other Sales-side AI tooling in the field Observed in the supplier email corpus rather than centrally registered, but worth knowing about because it affects how you read activity records. Tool Used for --- --- Outreach Sales sequencing Artisan AI AI SDR - autonomous outbound ZoomInfo Enrichment and intent, also integrated into Salesforce as DOZISF Chorus Conversation intelligence, in the org as affectlayer DocuSign Contract execution Not every logged email in Task was written by a human. Outreach sequences and Artisan AI both log activity. When analysing rep behaviour or response times, account for automated sends or you will draw the wrong conclusion about who is doing what. Companion skill The reusable analytical context from the data consolidation project was packaged as an informa-data-analyst skill: entity definitions, metric calculations, standard filters, and common query patterns for connect-prod . It is the machine-readable version of the data model and metrics pages on this site. Undocumented territory Notion and Zapier automations are not yet documented. They exist and they run. The standing instruction is to record each new workflow in the AI & Agent Library before launch. Until the backlog is captured, treat any assumption about what is automated in Notion or Zapier as unverified. Next Informa's AI platform: API, Elysia Code, and the local console.","path":"/operations/ai/tooling"},{"title":"Dashboard index","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Which Salesforce dashboards exist in connect-prod, which folders they live in, and which metric definition they should use. Working, not Canonical.","headings":["Folders worth opening first","Recently maintained (last modified 2025)","How to pick a dashboard","Next"],"body":"Reporting dashboards are Working in the source-of-truth registry: live in connect-prod , not indexed in Notion. This page is a Company Brain index of names and purpose. It is not Canonical. It does not hold live figures. Queried Dashboard in connect-prod on 12 August 2026 . There are 200+ dashboard records. Most are year-stamped personal or team copies from 2019–2022. Use a recently modified company, public, hosted-buyer, travel, or ops dashboard — not a 2020 personal activity clone. If a dashboard number disagrees with a query on Metrics, the query wins until a human changes the definition. Do not paste dashboard totals into MDX. Folders worth opening first Folder What it holds --- --- Public Dashboards Cross-brand media, product-category, renewals, hunting, event TV walls Company Dashboards All-sales activity, company-wide sales, Q4 event sales, accounting exceptions Hosted Buyer Team dashboards Approvals, HBE goals, registrations by rep, command centre Connect Travel Dashboards Annual Connect Travel sales (2024, 2025, 2026) Ops Workflow Dashboards Sold-product status, TSNN fulfilment, content activation 2024 Sales Dashboards / 2025 AKA 2 Centuries + 1/4 Century Leadership copies (P and L, rebook). Treat as Working Older folders ( North/South/West Sales Team Activity Dashboards , 2020 Sales Dashboards , 2020 PDT Dashboards , PDT Activity Dashboards ) are historical. Do not use them for current pipeline. Recently maintained (last modified 2025) Last-modified dates from the org query. Descriptions are inferred from the title only where the Salesforce Description field is blank. Dashboard Folder Last modified Use for --- --- --- --- 2026 Media Dashboard for All Brands Public Dashboards 17 Dec 2025, Wei Zheng Media revenue by Organization c . Metric: revenue by business unit 2025 Media Dashboard for All Brands Public Dashboards 11 Dec 2025, Wei Zheng Prior-year media. Same definition, filter product year 2025 2025 Centralized Product Category Dashboard for Connect - SS Team Public Dashboards 10 Dec 2025, Wei Zheng Connect product mix. Metric: revenue by Product Category c 2026 DR Rebook 2025 AKA 2 Centuries + 1/4 Century 4 Dec 2025, Derek Rodriguez Rebooking / Existing Business. Metric: Type , product year 2026 Connect Travel Sales Connect Travel Dashboards 24 Nov 2025, Wei Zheng Travel / eTourism. Filter Organization c = 'Travel' 2026 All Sales Activity Tracker Company Dashboards 12 Nov 2025, Rebecca Rosengren Activity volume. Automated Outreach/Artisan rows sit in Task — do not treat every row as a human send Account Renewals Dashboard Public Dashboards 5 Nov 2025, MacKenzie Kitchin Existing Business coverage Sales Renewals by Rep Y/Y Public Dashboards 4 Nov 2025, MacKenzie Kitchin Rep-level renewal Sold Product Status for This Year Ops Workflow Dashboards 10 Oct 2025, Wei Zheng Fulfilment of won lines ( Product Status c ) Centralized Product Category Dashboard for All Event Sales Public Dashboards 2 Oct 2025, Wei Zheng Event-line mix 2025 Show Approvals Hosted Buyer Team dashboards 11 Sep 2025, Wei Zheng Qualification pipeline. Count Fully Registered, not Registered 2025 Q4 Event Sales (Winter, West, South) Company Dashboards 2 Sep 2025, Wei Zheng Regional event bookings 2025 Hosted Buyer Registrations by Reps Hosted Buyer Team dashboards 8 Jul 2025, Wei Zheng Recruiter activity 2025 Hosted Buyer Command Center Hosted Buyer Team dashboards 23 Jan 2025, Wei Zheng HBX operating view Sales Hunting Board Public Dashboards 21 Mar 2025, Wei Zheng New Business Exception Dashboard for Accounting Company Dashboards 19 Nov 2024, Wei Zheng Billing exceptions. Escalate; do not answer invoice questions from it Copies (\"Copy of…\", \"for suzi\", \"TV Dash\", named-rep media dashboards) exist alongside these. Prefer the untitled-as-copy original. How to pick a dashboard 1. Match the question to a metric definition. 2. Prefer Organization c for brand, Product Year c for season, Type for new vs existing. 3. Exclude barter and Expired unless the question needs them. 4. If the dashboard is year-stamped 2024 or earlier and nothing newer exists, say so. Owner of the dashboard index (Working): Mac Kitchin. Most current objects were last edited by Wei Zheng . Next AdBook, Visit Create, and the rest of the managed-package estate.","path":"/operations/data/dashboards"},{"title":"Data quality and governance","sidebarTitle":"Data quality and governance","description":"Internal controls for source ownership, record data, metric definitions, privacy, conflicts, and data-quality findings.","headings":["Governing principles","Salesforce controls","Data-quality workflow"],"body":"These controls decide which source wins, what may be stored here, and how data-quality issues are handled. Governing principles 1. The owning system wins when this site disagrees with it. 2. Company Brain pages document structure and meaning, not record-level customer data. 3. Metric definitions and SOQL have one canonical home: Metrics and definitions. 4. Known source conflicts remain visible until the responsible owner resolves them. 5. Working or Deprecated material must be disclosed as such. 6. Customer, subscriber, and hosted-buyer PII must not be added to MDX. Salesforce controls - Describe unfamiliar objects and fields before querying them. - Use the normal data API for records and Tooling API for metadata or tooling objects. - Apply the documented default filters unless the question requires otherwise. - Use OpportunityLineItem.Organization c for the brand split. - Exclude barter from cash revenue and distinguish product year from close date. - Treat Fully Registered as confirmed attendance; other registration stages are not equivalent. Data-quality workflow Reproduce it against the live owning system or an owned source. Capture the field, population, source date, and affected workflow without copying customer records into this site. Do not create a second issue or silently reconcile an existing conflict. Use Who owns what and update the source system, not merely the mirror.","path":"/operations/data/data-governance"},{"title":"Data model","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Core objects, relationships, and the fields that actually matter for analysis.","headings":["Relationship map","Disambiguating everyday words","Account","Opportunity","Stage ladder","OpportunityLineItem","Contact","Registration and Marketplace","The five gotchas that cause most wrong answers","Next"],"body":"Use this object map before writing a query or interpreting a field. It documents structure, not record contents. Relationship map Disambiguating everyday words When someone says one of these, they mean a specific object. They say They mean --- --- Customer, client Account . Type separates Client, Prospect, 3rd Party Planner, Competitor, Agency. Account Status c is Active, Dormant, Inactive, Nurturing, or For Connect Contact Contact , an individual at an Account via AccountId . Contact Role c is Decision Maker, Influencer, Marketing, Sales, Operations Deal, opportunity Opportunity , linked to an Account via AccountId , with product detail on OpportunityLineItem Registration, attendee Registration c , linked to Contact via Registrant c and Account via Planner Account c Event, marketplace Marketplace c , or Campaign where referenced by Registration c.Meeting Event c Prospect Lead . The object is labelled \"Prospect\" in this org Account The central entity for both sides of the business. Field Meaning --- --- Type Client, Prospect, 3rd Party Planner, Agency, Competitor Account Status c Active, Dormant, Inactive, Nurturing, For Connect Primary Category c Association, Corporation, Agency, Venue- , Supplier- . The supplier cohort filter is LIKE 'Supplier-%' Market Segment c Association, Connect, Corporate, Sports, Third Party Sales Territory c Midwest, Northeast, South, West, International. Derived from BillingState Industry Highly detailed, includes Supplier- and Venue- categories Credit Worthiness Score c Green, Yellow, Red, Approval required Red Flagged Status c Blacklisted, Unpaid Debt ParentId Parent account, for hierarchies Spend rollups are denormalised onto Account and maintained by the dlrs package. Use them rather than aggregating opportunities. Total Spend This Product Year c , Total Spend Last Product Year c , Total Spend 2 Product Years Ago c through ... 5 Product Years Ago c , Total Historical Account Spend c , plus events-versus-media splits: Total Spend Events This Product Year c and Total Spend Media This Product Year c . Per-event participation is also denormalised, as booleans and counts such as Num Spring MP Products This Year c and Has Connect Spring 2026 Purchase c . Opportunity Field Meaning --- --- StageName See the stage ladder below Amount Deal total. Note this is deal-level, not line-level CloseDate Expected or actual close. Required Type New Business or Existing Business. The single most predictive field in the dataset ForecastCategoryName Omitted, Pipeline, Best Case, Commit, Closed IO Number c Insertion order number. Required, unique Opportunity Product Type c Registration, Sponsorship, Digital, Print, CAP, Bundle, Marketplace, Services. Currently 100% null Sales Territory c Eastern, Midwest, Northeast, South, West, International Barter c Non-cash trade. Always exclude for cash revenue Reason Lost c Loss reason. Blank on 421 of 424 lost deals Event Market Segment c Association, Corporate, Sports, Faith Lead Channel c Inbound or Outbound. Inbound wins at 77.9% vs 71.5% Initial Close Won Date c First time marked Closed Won, for historical accuracy Change Made After Closed Won c By Production, By Accounting Billing Terms c One Time, Recurring, Compound, Cancelled, Billed in Full Stage ladder Stage IsClosed IsWon --- --- --- Made Contact & Set Meeting false false Prospecting/RFP false false Previous Year Purchase false false Proposal Creation (BANT Known) false false Initial Call · Interested · Proposal · Proposal Sent false false Presentation · Proposal Review · Negotiation false false Verbal · MSA Signed · SOW Signed false false Closed Won true true Expired true false Closed Lost true false Expired is not lost. It is a deal that timed out without a decision. Exclude it from win-rate denominators or you understate performance by roughly nine percentage points - and you hide \\$660K of un-followed-up pipeline. OpportunityLineItem Where the product taxonomy lives, and where revenue is actually attributable. Field Values --- --- TotalPrice Line item total. Reconciles to Opportunity.Amount but not always Product Category c Digital, Event, Event Amplification, Special Projects Product Type c 50\\+ types: Association, BizBash, Corporate, Marketplace, Sports, Webinar Event Name c Spring Marketplace, MEGA, Winter Marketplace Organization c Meetings, Travel, BizBash, TSNN - the business unit split Product Year c The calendar year the product is for Product Status c Pending, In Process, Completed Barter Value c / Monetary Value c Non-cash and cash portions AB2 ABPayType c PaidFor, ValueAdd, NonBillable, Barter, Promo Boolean helpers exist per event, such as IsSpringMarketplaceProduct c , IsMEGAProduct c , Is Show Booth c , and IsRegistrationProduct c . Contact Field Meaning --- --- Contact Role c Decision Maker, Influencer, Marketing, Sales, Operations Inactive c / No Longer with Company c Filter both out for live outreach Decision Maker c Boolean Lead Type c Advertiser, Agency, Event Pro, Non-customer Connect No Fly List c Banned from events. Always exclude from event queries Quality Score c , National Meetings Score c Planner qualification scoring HasOptedOutOfEmail Email opt-out Registration and Marketplace Registration c links a hosted buyer to an event. Field Meaning --- --- Registrant c The Contact Planner Account c The Account Meeting Event c The Campaign Marketplace c The specific Marketplace c instance Registration Stage c Registering, Pending Confirmation, Registered, Fully Registered , Cancelled, Declined, Verbal, Stopped Hosted Buyer Type c Anchor Planner, End User, Third Party, Non-Hosted Buyer Qualification Status c Qualification outcome Visit Registration Key c , Visit Visitor Id c , Visit Order Id c , Visit Expo Id c Visit Create platform sync keys Marketplace c carries Event Type c , Event Track c , Start Date c , End Date c , Event Status c , Visit Expo ID c , a multi-picklist of available appointment tracks, and registration rollup counts by stage. The five gotch","path":"/operations/data/data-model"},{"title":"Metrics and definitions","sidebarTitle":"Metrics and definitions","description":"Exact definitions and SOQL for revenue, pipeline, win rate, registrations, and account spend.","headings":["Revenue","Revenue booked (Closed Won)","Revenue by product category","Revenue by business unit","Pipeline","Active pipeline value","Pipeline by stage","Weighted pipeline by forecast category","Win and loss","Win rate","Loss reasons","Events","Registrations by event and stage","Confirmed attendees","Event quick stats, using rollups","Registration conversion rate","No-show rate","Accounts"],"body":"Every metric below is defined once, here. If a number in a deck disagrees with one of these queries, the query wins until someone changes the definition on this page. Revenue Revenue booked (Closed Won) Total value of won deals, cash only. Segment by Sales Territory c , Media Rep c , Type (New versus Existing), or Event Market Segment c . Amount can be edited after Closed Won. For a historically accurate figure, check Change Made After Closed Won c and Initial Close Won Date c . Revenue by product category Revenue by business unit Returns the Meetings, Travel, BizBash, and TSNN split. Note this filters on product year , not close date. Pipeline Active pipeline value Pipeline by stage Weighted pipeline by forecast category Win and loss Win rate Percentage of closed deals that were won, excluding Expired from the denominator . SOQL has no CASE , so run two queries and divide client-side. Report both the excluding-Expired and including-Expired figures. On the supplier cohort they are 78% and 69%, and the nine-point gap is the follow-up problem - 79 deals worth \\$660K that simply timed out. Loss reasons This query currently returns almost nothing useful. Reason Lost c is blank on 421 of 424 lost deals. Loss intelligence today comes from mined email, not from CRM fields. Enforcing this field at Closed Lost is an open action on the data consolidation project. Events Registrations by event and stage Confirmed attendees Event quick stats, using rollups Registration conversion rate Compare Total Registrations c against Total Fully Registered c on Marketplace c and divide client-side. No-show rate No-shows are commercially material, not administrative: the hosted buyer deposit is forfeited on missed appointments, and supplier renewal depends on appointments actually happening. Accounts Top accounts by spend Events versus media split Year-over-year change Pull Total Spend This Product Year c and Total Spend Last Product Year c together and compute the delta client-side. Historical years go back five product years via Total Spend 2 Product Years Ago c through ... 5 Product Years Ago c . Reference benchmarks Current figures on the supplier cohort, for sanity-checking any new analysis. Benchmark Value --- --- Average won deal ~\\$16K Average renewal deal ~\\$18.8K Average new-business deal ~\\$10.1K Renewal win rate 84.6% New-business win rate 67.2% Inbound win rate 77.9% Outbound win rate 71.5% Overall win rate excluding expired ~78% Overall win rate including expired ~69% Next Which Salesforce dashboards exist, which folders to open, and which metric they should use.","path":"/operations/data/metrics"},{"title":"API catalogue","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Documented APIs used by Connect systems and agents, with their authoritative contract and disclosure boundary.","headings":[],"body":"API Role Authoritative contract Company Brain reference --- --- --- --- Salesforce data APIs Query and update standard and custom records in connect-prod Live Salesforce describe and API metadata Salesforce org Salesforce Tooling and Metadata APIs Inspect or deploy metadata and tooling objects Live org metadata Salesforce org Visit Create v2 Read expos, registrations, visitors, types, and related event-registration resources Vendor OpenAPI create-v2.json Visit Create API Elysia HTTP API Internal AI chat, agents, content, and applications Live stage OpenAPI when available Elysia API This catalogue documents interfaces, not credentials. Retrieve secrets through the approved credential store or connection and never paste them into MDX, examples, logs, or prompts.","path":"/operations/integrations/api-catalogue"},{"title":"Automation catalogue","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Index of documented production automation, agent-assisted workflows, roadmap candidates, and known documentation gaps.","headings":["Documented automation","Planned or candidate automation","Documentation gaps"],"body":"Use this index to find documented production automations, agent-assisted workflows, and known gaps. Documented automation Area Automation Status source --- --- --- Registration Visit Create webhook pipelines into Salesforce Visit Create workflows Salesforce rollups dlrs -maintained account spend rollups Salesforce integrations AI agents Six Agentforce agents recorded as Active in the AI & Agent Library Agent roster Email activity Outreach and Artisan AI write automated activity to Salesforce Task Data model Planned or candidate automation The Automation roadmap contains prioritised candidates and estimated time savings. A roadmap entry is not proof that a production automation exists. Documentation gaps Notion and Zapier automations are not yet documented. Treat claims about them as unverified and record new agents, prompts, MCP tools, and workflows in the AI & Agent Library before launch.","path":"/operations/integrations/automation-catalogue"},{"title":"Integration map","sidebarTitle":"Integration map","description":"Task-led map of the documented integration boundaries between Salesforce, Visit Create, media ordering, finance, marketing, and internal AI.","headings":[],"body":"Use this section to identify the owning system, interface, and detailed reference before diagnosing or changing an integration. Managed packages, field prefixes, billing boundaries, and technical debt. The documented APIs and where their exact contracts live. The five Visit Create inbound pipelines and their recovery path. Documented automations, candidates, owners, and verification status. Do not place credentials, signing tokens, bearer tokens, visitor records, or webhook URLs containing secrets on this site.","path":"/operations/integrations/overview"},{"title":"Salesforce integrations","sidebarTitle":"Salesforce integration contracts","description":"AdBook, Visit Create, ZoomInfo, DocuSign, Marketing Cloud, FinancialForce, and the managed-package estate in connect-prod.","headings":["The critical two","Working with AdBook fields","The rest of the estate","Billing and payment fields","Technical debt worth knowing about","Next"],"body":"connect-prod is a heavily integrated org. Thirteen managed packages sit on top of it, several with triggers on core objects. Understanding which package owns which field prefix is the difference between a correct query and a confusing one. The critical two Full ad order management: line items, rate cards, pricing, and the IO lifecycle. 25\\+ objects, 200\\+ Apex classes, triggers on Account, Contact, Opportunity, OpportunityLineItem, and Product2. Every AB2 field comes from here. Registration platform. Five webhook pipelines into Registration c and Supplier Registration c . Keys: Visit Registration Key c , Visit Visitor Id c , Visit Order Id c , Visit Expo Id c . Working with AdBook fields On Opportunity Meaning --- --- AB2 ABOrderID c AdBook order ID AB2 AB IO Number c AdBook IO number AB2 ABTotalGrossCost c Total gross cost AB2 ABBillTo c Bill to Client or Agency AB2 ABBillType c Net or Gross AB2 ABAgencyCommission c Agency commission percentage AB2 Order Created c Whether an order exists in AdBook AB2 ABDoNotSync c Exclude from sync On OpportunityLineItem Meaning --- --- AB2 ABDropID c Ad drop or placement ID AB2 ABDropCost c / AB2 ABDropPrice c Cost and price AB2 ABPayType c PaidFor, ValueAdd, NonBillable, Barter, Promo AB2 ABPriceType c CPM, CPC, Flat Rate, CPZ AB2 ABImpressions c Contracted impressions AB2 ABDropCancelled c Whether the drop was cancelled Not every opportunity uses AdBook. Check AB2 Order Created c = true before relying on any AB2 field, or your averages will be computed over nulls. And for cash revenue at line level, filter AB2 ABPayType c = 'PaidFor' - ValueAdd, NonBillable, Barter, and Promo are all non-cash. The rest of the estate Package Purpose Notes --- --- --- DOZISF (ZoomInfo) Contact and account enrichment ZoomInfo c , ZoomInfo Intent c , ZoomInfo Scoop c , plus enrich-status fields on Account and Contact dsfs (DocuSign) Contract and IO e-signature Triggers on DocuSign Status c and DocuSign Recipient Status c et4ae5 (Marketing Cloud Connect) Salesforce to Marketing Cloud email sync SendDefinition c , IndividualEmailResult c , 8\\+ triggers silverpop Legacy email marketing Being phased out in favour of Marketing Cloud. Sync dates and lead scores still on Contact VisualAntidote (Formstack) Forms, submissions, document generation 15\\+ objects affectlayer (Chorus / ZoomInfo) Call recording and conversation analytics Engagement c , Moment c , ChorusEngagementContact c dlrs Declarative lookup rollup summaries Critical. Triggers on Account, Opportunity, and OpportunityLineItem. Maintains every Total Spend rollup sf\\ devops Change management and deployment pipeline 15\\+ objects, 500\\+ Apex classes FinancialForce Invoicing and financial documents Invoice c , CreditNote c , Transaction c . Field visibility is limited - prefer Opportunity billing fields pnova (Cuneiform) Data quality profiling 100\\+ Apex classes Conga Composer Document generation and merge 50\\+ Composer classes Billing and payment fields Revenue flows through three systems, and the boundaries matter. Billing Terms c (One Time, Recurring, Compound, Cancelled, Billed in Full), Billing Start Date c , Invoice Number c , Invoice Date c , Opportunity Paid In Full c , PO Number c , Opportunity Currency c . The IO lifecycle lives in AdBook and reflects into AB2 fields on the opportunity and its line items. Invoice objects exist but with limited field access. For detailed invoice analysis, use Opportunity-level billing fields unless additional FinancialForce field access has been granted. Line-level payment fields include AR Payment Status c , Paid In Full Date c , Do Not Invoice c , and Delayed Invoice Date c . Currency. Most deals are USD but some are CAD, flagged on Opportunity Currency c . Do not sum across currencies without converting. Technical debt worth knowing about - Two agency-contact objects. Agency Contact c and Agency Contacts c both exist and appear to overlap. - ProposalDetailsTrigger on Proposal Details c runs on API version 29, dating from 2014. - Order Details c is likely legacy or supplementary to OpportunityLineItem . - Silverpop and Marketing Cloud coexist , with Silverpop notionally being retired. Next The registration platform, live expos, and how it maps into Salesforce.","path":"/operations/integrations/salesforce-integrations"},{"title":"Webhooks","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Internal index of the documented inbound Visit Create webhook pipelines and their Salesforce processing path.","headings":[],"body":"The documented webhook estate currently centres on Visit Create notifications entering Salesforce Sites and being processed by isolated Apex pipelines. Pipeline Registration audience Canonical detail --- --- --- Hosted Buyer Connect hosted buyers Visit Create workflows Connect Supplier Marketplace suppliers Visit Create workflows eTourism Destination Marketer eTourism audience Visit Create workflows Brand Experience Pass BizBash-related pass audience Visit Create workflows Industry Supplier Pass Supplier-pass audience Visit Create workflows The canonical implementation is the sf-visit-webhook repository. This page is only an index; the workflow page records matching, deduplication, logging, email, and recovery. Store path patterns without secret query strings. Never copy a URL containing sig= or another signing value into this site.","path":"/operations/integrations/webhooks"},{"title":"Agentforce Agent Rollout","sidebarTitle":"Agentforce rollout","description":"Standing up and operationalising the Agentforce agent roster in connect-prod.","headings":["Overview","Goals and success criteria","The roster","Next steps","Risks and blockers","How the three P1 workflows work","Evaluation and guardrails","Dependencies","Next"],"body":"Stage In progress · Priority High · Type Integration · Area Technology & AI · Lead Mac Kitchin Overview Stand up and operationalise the Agentforce agent roster in connect-prod - Connect (SF Agent), Data Steward, Flow Automation, Notebook AI, Opportunity Agent, and Agentforce Sales Coach - prioritised by the automation roadmap, which is worth roughly 1,405 hours a year. Goals and success criteria - P1 workflows live : FAQ deflection (250 hrs/yr), campaign analytics and reporting (300 hrs/yr), attendee-details collection and follow-up (200 hrs/yr). - Every agent documented in the AI & Agent Library with an owner and a status. - Customer-facing answers grounded in the Customer FAQs library or the Live Events wiki, with citations. The roster See Agent roster for what each one does. All six are recorded as Active in the AI & Agent Library, all on the Salesforce platform, all owned by Mac Kitchin. Next steps - [ ] Confirm deployment status per agent against the Agentforce Agents inventory - [ ] Wire FAQ deflection to the Customer FAQs library - [ ] Define evaluation and guardrail checks - no rates, no contact lists, escalate billing and legal Risks and blockers Risk Impact Mitigation Status --- --- --- --- Agents answering from stale event facts Wrong customer answers, at scale Chatbots restricted to the Live Events wiki; refresh SOP in place Controlled The stale-event-facts risk is not theoretical. Multiple event sites currently publish contradictory dates, hosts, venues, and cancellation policies - see Known issues. An agent with unrestricted retrieval would confidently give a supplier the wrong date for Connect North. Restricting customer-facing retrieval to the Live Events wiki is the entire mitigation. How the three P1 workflows work The two highest-frequency supplier questions are \"what attendee details do I submit after registering?\" and \"when and where is Connect Marketplace 2026?\", both with very high frequency signal and confidence of 0.86 and 0.90. Both are pure lookups. The agent answers from Customer FAQs and the Live Events wiki, cites the source, and never improvises a date. The largest single time sink. Suppliers ask for campaign performance and reps assemble it manually. Split into self-service reporting plus an agent that assembles and explains the numbers. Depends on reporting dashboards being indexed, which is currently marked Working rather than canonical. After a supplier registers, someone has to chase attendee details. This is the number-one question by frequency and it is entirely mechanical - collect, validate, remind, escalate on non-response. Evaluation and guardrails The answer-style guide distilled from the highest-performing reps, and the hard limits, are documented in full on Guardrails. In short: lead with the value frame, be concrete about logistics, use honest urgency, never invent an offer, never quote a rate, never share a contact list, always propose a concrete next step, always cite. Dependencies Depends on Why --- --- Supplier Knowledge Intelligence Supplies the grounded FAQ and article library agents cite Company OS rebuild Supplies the canonical sources and truth levels Data consolidation Without clean account ownership, routing and escalation break Next Open data-quality problems, content conflicts, and documentation gaps. Do not reconcile them.","path":"/operations/projects/agentforce-rollout"},{"title":"Completed and archived projects","sidebarTitle":"Completed and archived projects","description":"Governed destination for completed, cancelled, superseded, or archived project documentation.","headings":["Archive rules"],"body":"No completed project is currently listed in the Company Brain navigation. Master Projects in Company OS remains the canonical register. Archive rules - Move a project here only after its canonical Master Projects row has a terminal status. - Preserve the final outcome, owner, dates, decisions, unresolved follow-up, and replacement link. - Do not delete a superseded project page when it explains a material decision or data lineage. - Mark any continuing operational area separately; an area does not become a completed project. Active work remains on Active projects. Deprecated databases and knowledge sources remain recorded in the Source-of-truth registry.","path":"/operations/projects/archive"},{"title":"Notion Workspace Rebuild","sidebarTitle":"Company OS and Notion workspace","description":"Consolidating three front doors and ~35 databases into Company OS.","headings":["Overview","Goals and success criteria","Key decisions","What Company OS now contains","Next steps","Risks and blockers","Conventions the rebuild established","Why this matters beyond Notion","Next"],"body":"Stage In progress · Priority High · Type Knowledge · Area Technology & AI · Lead Mac Kitchin · Timeline 26 June 2026 to 31 July 2026 Overview Consolidate three competing front doors and roughly 35 scattered databases into Company OS : PARA navigation over a master-database engine, with a Knowledge Base, department pages, an AI & Automation Hub, and a Source of Truth Registry. The design principle is a clean separation between two layers. Projects, Areas, Resources, Archive. Front doors that show linked views. Optimised for a human finding something. Master databases - People, Projects, Docs, Tasks, Areas, Resources. Each fact has exactly one home. Optimised for correctness. Goals and success criteria - One front door. Legacy hubs archived with redirect banners. - Every domain has exactly one canonical home, recorded in the source-of-truth registry. - A new hire can self-orient in under fifteen minutes. Key decisions Date Decision Why, and the trade-off Decided by --- --- --- --- 2026-06-27 PARA navigation plus hub-and-spoke master databases Separates navigation from data and kills duplication Mac Kitchin 2026-07-06 Keep the original Salesforce Knowledge Base as canonical; archive the duplicate The original is linked from every hub, and row counts were identical Mac Kitchin What Company OS now contains Layer Contents --- --- Front doors Home, Team HQ, Projects & Initiatives, Areas & Departments, Resources, AI & Automation Hub, Source of Truth Registry, Archive, Databases (Admin) Master databases People, Projects, Docs, Tasks, Areas, Resources, Registry, AI & Agent Library Domain hubs Salesforce Knowledge Base for connect-prod ; Supplier Knowledge Intelligence Wikis Live Events (2026-2027), the canonical event source Next steps - [ ] Create the Company OS teamspace in the Notion UI and move the page tree into it - [ ] Restore or confirm the Salesforce teamspace flagged in trash - [ ] Migrate legacy Team HQ starter-database rows into the master databases - [ ] Merge the two prompt libraries into one Risks and blockers Risk Impact Mitigation Status --- --- --- --- Salesforce teamspace flagged in trash Loss of the Salesforce knowledge engine Key pages already moved into Company OS; verify the teamspace in the UI Open Old links pointing at archived hubs User confusion Redirect banners on every legacy front door Mitigated Conventions the rebuild established These now apply to anything added to the workspace, and by extension to this site. If it has an end date and a definition of done, it is a project and belongs in Master Projects. If it is ongoing ownership, it is an area. Do not create area pages for time-bound work. Both projects and areas link to their canonical docs and sources rather than duplicating them. Duplication is what created the problem this project is solving. Every major information type gets exactly one Canonical registry entry, with an owner and a refresh cadence. When a source is replaced, the old row is marked Deprecated and linked to its replacement. Owner, stage, priority, area, and timeline live in database properties and stay authoritative. Page bodies hold context and decisions, not status. Why this matters beyond Notion This project is the precondition for everything in the AI stack. An agent grounded on a workspace with three front doors, duplicate databases, and no truth levels will confidently cite the wrong thing. The registry is what lets an agent say \"this source is marked Working, treat it with caution\" instead of guessing. Next Merge Connect and BizBash account, contact, lead, and opportunity data.","path":"/operations/projects/company-os"},{"title":"Connect and BizBash Data Consolidation","sidebarTitle":"Data consolidation","description":"Merging Connect and BizBash account, contact, lead, and opportunity data into one clean dataset.","headings":["Overview","Goals and success criteria","The headline problem","The second problem: loss data does not exist","What the analysis established","Next steps","Risks and blockers","The seven-part analysis","Why this blocks other work","Next"],"body":"Stage In progress · Priority High · Type Ops · Area Operations · Lead Mac Kitchin Overview Consolidate Salesforce Accounts, Contacts, Leads, Prospects, Opportunities, Campaigns, Campaign Members, and Account Team data across the Connect and BizBash businesses so that routing, reporting, and sales coverage all work from one clean dataset . Connect and BizBash grew up separately. They now share a Salesforce org but not a coherent data model, and the seams show most clearly in account ownership. Goals and success criteria - Account team coverage ready for BizBash routing. - A deduplicated account and contact universe with categories preserved. - The data-quality plan executed against the source analysis. The headline problem 26 account-team rep assignments against 20,876 advertiser accounts. On the BizBash side, effectively no advertiser account has a recorded owner. Any routing rule, coverage report, or agent escalation path that assumes account ownership is currently operating on nothing. The second problem: loss data does not exist Reason Lost c is blank on 421 of 424 lost deals , and Opportunity Product Type c is 100 percent null . Structured win/loss and objection analytics are therefore impossible from CRM fields today. This is why the objection and loss intelligence in the Supplier Knowledge Intelligence project had to be mined from email rather than queried. What the analysis established Real numbers from supplier-account opportunities closing since 1 January 2025, filtered on Account.Primary Category c LIKE 'Supplier-%' . Metric Value What it means --- --- --- Closed Won 492 deals, \\$7.85M Average won deal around \\$16K Closed Lost 139 deals, \\$1.93M Roughly 78% win rate, won over won plus lost Expired 79 deals, \\$0.66M Roughly 69% win rate including expired - expiries are a follow-up gap, not a loss Existing / renewal business 84.6% win, \\$6.22M, ~\\$18.8K average Renewals are the revenue engine New business 67.2% win, \\$1.64M, ~\\$10.1K average New logos convert lower and smaller Inbound vs outbound 77.9% vs 71.5% win rate Inbound converts better - prioritise inbound response time The gap between the 78 percent and 69 percent win rates is 79 expired deals worth \\$660K. Those are not deals we lost on merit; they are deals nobody followed up on. That is the cheapest revenue in the analysis. Next steps - [ ] Review the seven-part analysis and confirm the deployment map - [ ] Enforce Reason Lost c and product type at Closed Lost Risks and blockers Risk Impact Mitigation Status --- --- --- --- Team-role routing falls through at cutover Advertiser accounts left unowned Build the deployment map before cutover Open The seven-part analysis The full working sits in Notion Company OS as a seven-document series. The findings and the data behind them. What lives where across the two businesses. How accounts and contacts should be categorised post-merge. Decision rules for inbound records. The rules that replace the missing account team coverage. The remediation programme. The reusable analytical context, which became the informa-data-analyst skill. Why this blocks other work Clean account ownership is a precondition for agent escalation (who does the agent hand off to?), for coverage reporting, and for any renewal-outreach automation. Given that renewals carry 79 percent of supplier revenue, an unowned account is an unrenewed account. Next Mine the supplier email corpus into a governed, agent-ready knowledge base.","path":"/operations/projects/data-consolidation"},{"title":"Active projects","sidebarTitle":"Active projects","description":"The four initiatives currently in flight, their stage, owner, and how they connect.","headings":["How they fit together","Project page shell","The through-line","Next"],"body":"Master Projects in Notion Company OS is the canonical register - one row per initiative with owner, stage, priority, area, and timeline. Four initiatives are currently open. All four are led by Mac Kitchin . Project Stage Priority Type Area --- --- --- --- --- Notion Workspace Rebuild - Company OS In progress High Knowledge Technology & AI Connect and BizBash Data Consolidation In progress High Ops Operations Supplier Knowledge Intelligence Planned Medium Knowledge Technology & AI Agentforce Agent Rollout In progress High Integration Technology & AI How they fit together These are not four independent efforts. They form a single chain, and the order matters. You cannot ground an agent on a workspace with three competing front doors and 35 scattered databases. The Notion rebuild establishes one canonical home per domain, recorded in a source-of-truth registry. Everything downstream depends on it. Connect and BizBash account, contact, lead, and opportunity data has never been merged. Routing, reporting, and coverage all break on it. Until this lands, any agent answering a question about account ownership is answering from a broken dataset. The real answers live in reps' inboxes, not in documentation. Mining 26,600 supplier emails turns tribal knowledge into a governed, citable FAQ and article library that agents can ground on. With a clean container, clean data, and a grounded knowledge base, the agent roster goes live against a prioritised roadmap worth roughly 1,405 hours a year. Project page shell Every project page in Company OS uses the same six sections, and the database properties stay authoritative over the page body. What the project is and why it exists. What done looks like, stated measurably. Date, decision, why and trade-off, decided by. Open checklist items. Risk, impact, mitigation, status. Supporting material, related projects. Starting something new? Add a row to Master Projects first, set Owner, Stage, Priority, Area, and Timeline, then copy the six-section shell into the page body. The database is the record; the page is context. The through-line Read the four projects together and one theme emerges: this business runs on knowledge that has never been written down . Loss reasons live in nobody's head because Reason Lost c is blank on 421 of 424 lost deals. Account ownership is unrecorded for 20,876 advertiser accounts. Event facts contradict themselves across a dozen websites. The projects are all, in different ways, an attempt to move institutional knowledge out of inboxes and individual memory and into systems that a person or an agent can query. Next Fix the container first: one canonical home per domain, recorded in the registry.","path":"/operations/projects/overview"},{"title":"Supplier Knowledge Intelligence","sidebarTitle":"Supplier knowledge intelligence","description":"Mining the supplier email corpus into a governed, agent-ready knowledge base.","headings":["Overview","Scope and method","Governance","What v1 produced","The top supplier questions","Next steps","Risks and blockers","Scaling to the full corpus","Tooling observed in the corpus","Next"],"body":"Stage Planned (v1 complete, full-corpus run pending) · Priority Medium · Type Knowledge · Area Technology & AI · Lead Mac Kitchin Overview An institutional-knowledge base mined from historical Salesforce correspondence with supplier accounts - exhibitors, sponsors, advertisers, DMOs and CVBs, venues, AV, production, and technology vendors. It turns tribal knowledge that lived only in reps' inboxes into a searchable, retrieval-ready knowledge base for both employees and Agentforce agents. The next phase scales the pipeline from the v1 sample to the full 2025-2026 corpus of roughly 26,600 logged supplier emails , to produce a true ranked top-100 question list and quantified deal correlations. Scope and method --- --- Supplier cohort Accounts where Primary Category c LIKE 'Supplier-%' - roughly 13,800 accounts across 29 supplier categories Why that filter Account Sub Type c = 'Supplier' returns only 30 records; category was the far better signal Activity profiled 80,612 EmailMessage records (67k outbound, 13k inbound) and 416,578 Task records, of which 385k are logged emails v1 sample The most recent ~200 days of supplier email threads, to validate the pipeline end to end and seed each knowledge base with grounded findings Confidence Every finding carries a confidence score and an occurrence signal; minimum corroboration for anything treated as policy is two independent sources Governance This is the most sensitive project in the portfolio, and its governance rules are non-negotiable. - Raw email bodies are never stored. Only generalised, PII-redacted knowledge. - Pricing, legal, contract, payment, complaint, and PII content is flagged High or Critical sensitivity and routed to the Knowledge Candidates review queue rather than auto-published. Six sensitive items are currently held there awaiting human review. - Single-source items are never treated as policy. - Source Salesforce record IDs are retained on every finding for auditability. What v1 produced Knowledge base Contents --- --- Customer FAQs ~31 grounded question-and-answer pairs with confidence scores, ranked by frequency Knowledge Articles 10 retrieval-optimised articles Tribal Knowledge 12 undocumented know-how items Automation Opportunities 9 candidates worth roughly 1,405 hrs/yr Knowledge Gaps 8 documentation gaps Communication Patterns 7 lifecycle patterns Agentforce Recommendations 6 agent topics Knowledge Candidates Governed review queue, 6 sensitive items held The top supplier questions Ranked by observed frequency in the sample. Frequency is a relative signal, not a full-corpus count. Question Category Frequency Confidence --- --- --- --- --- 1 What attendee details do I submit after registering? Registration Very high 0.86 2 When and where is Connect Marketplace 2026? Event logistics Very high 0.90 3 What is included in a supplier package? Connect Marketplace High 0.82 4 What media or advertising can I buy to reach attendees? Media & Advertising High 0.85 5 When is payment due, and what are the terms? Billing & Contracts High 0.75 6 Can I get the buyer list or attendee breakdown? Attendee Data High 0.78 7 Can I get campaign analytics or reporting? Campaign Management High 0.78 8 How do I book my hotel room block? Hotel & Logistics Medium 0.80 9 Do you have creative examples before I sign? Media & Advertising Medium 0.75 10 How do I sign, and what is the contract process? Billing & Contracts Medium 0.88 11 Am I eligible for the Hosted Buyer or VIP programme? Hosted Buyer Medium 0.83 12 How are buyer appointments scheduled? Connect Marketplace Medium 0.80 13 What editorial and print ads exist, and what are the deadlines? Media & Advertising Medium 0.80 14 Can I transfer my invite to a colleague? Hosted Buyer Low-medium 0.70 15 What incentives apply if I sign now? Promotions Low-medium 0.70 Questions 1, 2, 6, and 7 together account for the three P1 automations on the roadmap. They are high-frequency, low-judgement, and entirely answerable from existing sources - which is exactly the profile of good automation. Next steps - [ ] Define batch windows and a thread-level dedupe strategy - [ ] Re-run scoring and update Last Observed on all findings - [ ] Human review pass on the Knowledge Candidates queue - 6 sensitive items waiting Risks and blockers Risk Impact Mitigation Status --- --- --- --- PII or pricing leakage into published knowledge Governance breach Knowledge Candidates review gate plus redaction rules Controlled Scaling to the full corpus 1. Iterate the supplier cohort across the full 2025-2026 history in windowed batches, deduplicating by thread. 2. Join activity to opportunity stage and outcome to quantify which questions and objections correlate with won deals. 3. Add numeric Frequency and Rank properties to Customer FAQs and build a sorted top-100 view. 4. Re-run scoring, promote review-queue items after human approval, update Last Observed. Tooling observed in the corpus Outreach, Artisan AI (AI SDR), DocuSign, and ZoomInfo appear throughout the supplier email sample - useful context when interpreting activity records, since not every logged email was hand-written by a rep. Next Deploy the agent roster once the container, data, and knowledge base are in place.","path":"/operations/projects/supplier-knowledge"},{"title":"Notion and Company OS","sidebarTitle":"Notion and Company OS","description":"How Company OS organises projects, people, SOPs, event facts, and source governance.","headings":["What it owns","Relationship to this site"],"body":"Company OS is the internal Notion workspace that organises governed company knowledge using a shared master-database model and PARA-style navigation. What it owns Information Canonical Notion surface --- --- People and team records Master People, with Team HQ as the front door Projects and initiatives Master Projects Tasks Master Tasks Event facts Live Events wiki SOPs and internal documents Master Docs and relevant knowledge hubs Source status and ownership Source-of-truth registry AI agents, prompts, and tools AI & Automation Hub and AI & Agent Library Relationship to this site The Company Brain is a retrieval-friendly mirror. It may reorganise and explain Company OS material, but it does not supersede the owned Notion record. Rebuild goals, decisions, blockers, and remaining work. Owners, statuses, and refresh cadences for each information type. Notion credentials, workspace IDs, integration tokens, and private credential tables must not be copied into this site.","path":"/operations/systems/notion-company-os"},{"title":"Other documented platforms","sidebarTitle":"Other approved platforms","description":"Verified supporting platforms and managed packages that appear in Connect workflows.","headings":[],"body":"Only platforms with a verified role in the current source set appear here. This is not a procurement catalogue or permission list. Platform or package Documented role Detailed reference --- --- --- AdBook Media order management, rate cards, pricing, and IO lifecycle Salesforce integrations DocuSign Agreement and attendee-detail workflows connected to Salesforce Sales process Marketing Cloud Connect Salesforce-to-Marketing-Cloud email sync Salesforce integrations FinancialForce Invoicing and financial documents with limited field visibility Salesforce integrations ZoomInfo Account and contact enrichment plus conversation tooling Salesforce integrations Formstack Forms, submissions, and document generation Salesforce integrations Elysia Informa-managed AI platform and gateway Elysia Shopify and Stripe appeared in an earlier placeholder architecture but are not sufficiently documented in the current source set. They remain out of the approved navigation until their role and owner are verified.","path":"/operations/systems/other-platforms"},{"title":"Systems and data","sidebarTitle":"Systems overview","description":"Task-led map of the systems, datasets, integrations, and reporting references used by Connect Meetings & Events.","headings":["Choose the right reference"],"body":"Start with the system that owns the fact or workflow. This site explains structure and operating boundaries; it does not replace live record data. Accounts, contacts, opportunities, registrations, products, revenue, and operational automation. Event registration, visitor types, expo IDs, and webhook-driven Salesforce workflows. Projects, people, SOPs, source registry, event facts, and governed knowledge. Data model, metric definitions, dashboards, and data-governance controls. Integration map, managed packages, APIs, webhooks, and automation catalogue. Approved systems with a verified role in the current source set. Choose the right reference Need Page --- --- Meaning of a Salesforce field or object Data model or Salesforce glossary Current CRM contents Query connect-prod ; do not use a static page Metric definition or query Metrics and definitions Existing report or dashboard Dashboard index Visit registration or webhook path Visit Create workflows System ownership Who owns what Known defect or discrepancy Known issues Never place customer records, credentials, tokens, signed webhook URLs, or named deal values in this knowledge base.","path":"/operations/systems/overview"},{"title":"Registration platforms","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Documented registration path for hosted buyers and the limits of what this site can say about attendee portals.","headings":["Staff guidance","Customer-safe information","Internal-only context","Limits and escalation","Related pages"],"body":"- Purpose: Find the documented registration system without inventing an attendee-portal vendor. - Audience: Operations, Hosted Buyer Experience, and approved internal agents. - Canonical source: Visit Create integration and hosted-buyer registration playbook. - Owner: Hosted buyer data — Bill Mathis; Visit Create workflows — as named on those pages. - Source status: Canonical for Visit Create; Working or TODO for Map Your Show / MatchPoint / custom portals. - Last reviewed: 27 August 2026. Staff guidance Hosted-buyer applications enter through Visit Create and sync into connect-prod . Use: - Visit Create platform overview - Create API - Salesforce workflows - Hosted-buyer registration Customer-safe information None from the integration internals. Do not describe webhook URLs, registration-type IDs, or processor behaviour to a customer. Internal-only context Visit webhook routing, Registration Stage c , and known marketplace lookup defects. Limits and escalation Which appointment portal (Map Your Show, MatchPoint, or custom) applies to each event is still unconfirmed. Leave that TODO. Do not invent a public application URL. Related pages Documented registration platform. Supporting packages with a verified role.","path":"/operations/systems/registration-platforms"},{"title":"Salesforce glossary","sidebarTitle":"","description":"API names, objects, and how everyday words map onto connect-prod.","headings":["Everyday word → object","Business terms with API names","Systems","Next"],"body":"Everyday business terms live on the Glossary. This page is the field-level companion for anyone querying connect-prod . Everyday word → object They say They mean --- --- Customer, client Account Deal, opportunity, IO Opportunity (plus OpportunityLineItem ) Hosted buyer registration Registration c Event / marketplace Marketplace c (and Registration c.Meeting Event c ) Prospect The object labelled Prospect in the org Supplier accounts: Primary Category c LIKE 'Supplier-%' . Business terms with API names Term Field or object --- --- Marketplace Also the Salesforce object Marketplace c Hosted buyer Tracked on Registration c.Hosted Buyer Type c IO / Insertion Order Every Opportunity carries IO Number c Product year OpportunityLineItem.Product Year c Closed Won StageName = 'Closed Won' , IsWon = true Barter Barter c = true . Always exclude from cash revenue PSL Contains PSL c on Opportunity Sales territory Derived from BillingState , not a territory object Expo Visit event instance. ID on Marketplace c.Visit Expo Id c Visitor Hosted buyers and most pass holders become Registration c ; Connect suppliers become Supplier Registration c Systems Term Definition --- --- connect-prod The production Salesforce org, ID 00D30000001H2naEAC . AdBook (AB2) Ad order management. All AB2 prefixed fields come from it. Check AB2 Order Created c before relying on them. Visit Create Event-registration platform (Create API v2). See Visit Create. Agentforce Salesforce agent platform. Roster runs in connect-prod . Elysia Informa's managed AI platform. Not Agentforce. See Elysia. Elysia Code CLI that points Claude Code at the Informa AI gateway. Current version 0.3.0. Metric definitions and SOQL live on Metrics. Object relationships live on the data model. Next What connect-prod holds and how to work with it.","path":"/operations/systems/salesforce-glossary"},{"title":"The Salesforce org","sidebarTitle":"Salesforce org overview","description":"connect-prod: what it holds, how it is structured, and how to work with it.","headings":["What this documentation does not cover","What the org supports","Querying it","SOQL is not SQL","Standard filters","Object landscape","Junction and bridge objects","Performance notes","Next"],"body":"Org : connect-prod · Org ID : 00D30000001H2naEAC · Truth level : Canonical for all client and account data · Refresh : live Salesforce is the record of business truth for Connect Meetings & Events. Notion documents its structure - objects, fields, relationships, automation - and never its record data. If you want to know what a field means, read the data model. If you want to know what is in it, query the org. What this documentation does not cover This site documents the Salesforce org so you can understand and query it. It does not document how to operate the Salesforce UI. For task-based, in-app how-tos, go to the source: - Creating or editing records (Accounts, Contacts, Opportunities, Registrations): use Salesforce's own Help documentation or ask your Salesforce admin. Standard record creation is done from the object tab in Lightning ( App Launcher → Accounts → New ), not through a custom process. - Permissions, sharing, licences : contact the Salesforce admin. See People & org for who owns Salesforce administration. - Reports and dashboards inside Salesforce : use the Reports tab in the org. For metric definitions and the SOQL behind them, see metrics. If a record-creation workflow is Connect-specific (for example, hosted buyer registrations flowing in from Visit Create), it will be documented under Visit Create workflows or the relevant object in the data model. What the org supports A B2B events business running marketplace-style appointment events plus media and digital advertising products. The sales model is supplier-side: CVBs, DMOs, hotels, venues, and other hospitality suppliers purchase booth, appointment, sponsorship, and digital products in order to meet hosted-buyer planners. Querying it SOQL. Use salesforce query records for records and salesforce aggregate query for COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX, and GROUP BY. sf sobject describe and sf data query . The Salesforce Knowledge Base in Notion is regenerated from these. SOQL is not SQL SQL habit What to do in SOQL --- --- JOIN Not supported. Use dot notation for parents ( Account.Name on Opportunity) and subqueries for children UNION , HAVING , complex subqueries Not supported CASE Not supported. Run two queries and compute client-side Date arithmetic Use literals: TODAY , LAST N DAYS:30 , THIS YEAR , LAST YEAR , THIS QUARTER Case-sensitive string comparison SOQL string comparison is case-insensitive by default ORDER BY supports NULLS FIRST and NULLS LAST . LIMIT and OFFSET are supported. Standard filters Apply these unless you have a specific reason not to. When to override: include Closed Lost for win/loss and loss-reason analysis; include Inactive accounts for historical spend analysis; include Expired when analysing stale or ageing pipeline. Object landscape Object Role Volume --- --- --- Account Clients, suppliers, prospects, agencies, and planners. Type and Primary Category c separate them. Territory derives from BillingState . High Opportunity A sales deal, effectively an insertion order. IO Number c and Opportunity No c are required. High Contact People at accounts. 30\\+ newsletter subscription booleans, quality scoring, Marketing Cloud sync fields. Large OpportunityLineItem The revenue line items. Carries the full product taxonomy. Very high Campaign Lightly customised. Serves as the event reference for Registration c . Medium Object Role Volume --- --- --- Registration c Hosted buyer registrations. 40\\+ fields sourced from Visit hosted-buyer registration forms. High Marketplace c A specific event instance, e.g. Spring Marketplace 2026. Holds dates, status, appointment tracks, and registration rollups. Low, ~10-15/year Visit Webhook Log c Audit log for the Visit Create webhook integration. Medium Attendee Assignment Detail c Attendee assignment tracking. Medium Categorized Sales Total c Pre-aggregated sales totals by category, for reporting performance. Medium Quota c , Sales Goal c , Sales Team Split c , SalesTeam c Quota and revenue-split management. Low to medium Digital Work Order c , Production c Fulfilment workflow for digital and print deliverables. Medium External Lead c , External Lead Map c External lead intake and mapping. Medium 1. OpportunityLineItem - multiple per opportunity 2. Contact - large database with extensive marketing fields 3. Account 4. Opportunity 5. Registration c - one per planner per event, across years 6. AB2 AdBookIndependentDrop c - ad line items synced from AdBook 7. et4ae5 IndividualEmailResult c - email tracking results Junction and bridge objects Object Bridges --- --- OpportunityLineItem Opportunity to Product2 Sales Team Split c Opportunity to sales team members AB2 OpportunityABRep c Opportunity to AdBook reps AB2 ClientAgencyRelation c Client to agency, inside AdBook Agency Contact c / Agency Contacts c Agency accounts to contacts - two similar objects exist, likely technical debt Performance notes - Filter on dates ( CloseDate , CreatedDate ) to limit the scan. - Use LIMIT while exploring. - For large result sets use the aggregate query tool rather than pulling records. - Prefer Account rollup fields ( Total Spend ) over aggregating opportunities. The dlrs package maintains them. Next Core objects, relationships, and the fields that matter for analysis.","path":"/operations/systems/salesforce-org"},{"title":"Shopify and Stripe","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Placeholder architecture slot. Shopify and Stripe are not verified in the current source set.","headings":["Staff guidance","Customer-safe information","Internal-only context","Limits and escalation","Related pages"],"body":"- Purpose: Prevent staff and agents from treating an unverified payments stack as approved. - Audience: Information systems, finance, and approved internal agents. - Canonical source: None imported. - Owner: Unverified. - Source status: Working. Not an approved platform until a source and owner are imported. - Last reviewed: 27 August 2026. Shopify and Stripe appeared in an earlier placeholder architecture. They are not sufficiently documented in the current Salesforce, Company OS, or Live Events source set. Do not describe them as Connect payment or storefront systems. Staff guidance Use documented billing and agreement paths instead: - Invoicing: FinancialForce, via Salesforce integration contracts - Agreements: DocuSign, via Sales process - Invoice exceptions: Stephen Han (Finance) Customer-safe information None from this page. Internal-only context That the slot exists only so the architecture gap is explicit. Limits and escalation Do not invent a Shopify storefront, Stripe account, or payment-link process. Billing questions escalate to Finance. Related pages Platforms that do have a verified role. Finance and systems routing.","path":"/operations/systems/shopify-and-stripe"},{"title":"Visit Create API","sidebarTitle":"Create API","description":"Create v2: resources, authentication, paging, webhooks, rate limits, and the MCP plus plugin skill we use against it.","headings":["Basics","How to sync","Resources","Agent tooling","visit-create MCP","visit-create-api plugin skill","What the Salesforce org uses","HTTP errors (from the spec)","Next"],"body":"Official spec: create-v2.json (OpenAPI 3.0.3, API version 2.0 ). Recaptured 12 August 2026 . Interactive console: api.visitcloud.com/create/docs. Vendor PDF (March 2023, source-dated): JSON API V2.pdf. Login and the full resource list live on Visit Create. This page is a working map for agents. For exact request bodies, open the spec or the bundled copy in sf-visit-webhook/plugins/visit-create-api/assets/openapi-spec.json . Basics --- --- Base URL https://api.visitcloud.com/create/v2 Auth HTTP Basic. Username = API key. Password blank. Keys are managed by the event organiser. A key may be all-events or expo-scoped, optionally IP-allowlisted, optionally write-enabled. Write permission Without it, only GET — except webhooks , which can be managed with a read-only key. Scope Almost every path includes expoId . GET /expos shows what the current key can see. Revision Incremental long. Advances on create or update. Lists return oldest revision first. Paging Default/max 100 on most lists; 1,000 on licenses and touchpoints. Next page: fromRevision= lastSeen+1 . Rate limit Two parallel requests. HTTP 429 if exceeded. Timeout Spec and our MCP client use 30 seconds . Salesforce talks to the same base URL through Named Credential Visit API . Do not put the key, the named-credential password, or a Authorization: Basic … header in MDX. How to sync Configure a webhook type ( visitor , partner , participant , action , connection , content , payment , order , activity ). Visit notifies https:// ?expoId=&webhookId= . There is no payload body . The consumer then GETs the matching resource with webhookId . The consumer must return 2xx within 30s of the notification, but only after it has received the pulled data. Failed deliveries retry at 10s, 1m, 5m, 15m, 1h, then 1 day. After the last failure, state becomes error until a PUT resets it to wait . Persist the highest revision seen + 1 and request only newer rows. This is how backfills and the nightly reconciliation batch work. Webhook notifications originate from Visit IPs 52.30.181.174 , 52.31.69.196 , 52.50.125.171 (from the spec). Our Salesforce Site is https://connectmeetings.my.salesforce-sites.com/VisitWebhook/services/apexrest/visit/… . Resources Tag Role Typical verbs --- --- --- Expos Event instances the key can access GET list / GET one Visitors Pre-event registrations. Only registrationState=registered may then participate GET, POST, PUT, DELETE Partners Exhibiting companies (child of a Contact) GET, POST, PUT, DELETE RegistrationTypes Visitor and partner categories, org-level or expo-level GET Orders Shop transactions. States: paying, paymentPending, completed, timeout, cancelled GET Payments PSP instructions. States: pending, paid, denied, refunded, cancelled. Methods: card, invoice, manual, free GET, PUT (state only) Participants Who actually attended, with activity times GET Activities Sessions, workshops, presentations GET, POST, PUT, DELETE Actions Raw scans / prints / interactions GET Connections Visitor–partner / content interactions. Key must have the endpoint enabled GET Contents Digital profiles and products in the apps GET, POST, PUT, DELETE Labels Content categories GET, POST, PUT, DELETE Licenses Systems and partner licences at the event GET, POST, PUT, DELETE Touchpoints NFC devices GET Webhooks Change notifications GET, POST, PUT, DELETE SendEmail Currently type=confirmation only; force=true to resend POST Upload Visitor ProfilePhoto, IdCard, IdPhoto POST Visitor list filters used in our receivers: registrationStates ( registered , incomplete , denied , cancelled ), registrationTypes (CSV of type IDs), webhookId , fromRevision , showDeleted (defaults true ). Agent tooling There is no standalone visit CLI on this machine. Agents use: visit-create MCP Repo: Visit-Create-MCP (50+ tools wrapping Create v2). Connected in this workspace as server visit-create . Resource Tools --- --- Expos visit list expos , visit get expo Visitors visit list visitors , visit get visitor , visit create visitor , visit update visitor , visit delete visitor Partners visit list partners , visit get partner , create / update / delete Activities / contents / labels / licenses / webhooks list, get, create, update, delete Connections, actions, participants, orders, payments, touchpoints, registration types list / get (payments also update) Email / upload visit send email , visit upload file Bulk visit list all — sequential pages, max 2 parallel, default cap 1,000 visit list all is the right tool for a complete expo extract. Do not paste the extract into this site. visit-create-api plugin skill Lives in sf-visit-webhook/plugins/visit-create-api/ . The skill points at a bundled OpenAPI file and the same request conventions as the spec. Use it when writing or reviewing API calls. What the Salesforce org uses Apex callout:Visit API/... from the five receivers and processors. Not the MCP. The MCP is for agents and backfill scripts. Default to read tools ( list / get ). Creating or deleting visitors, partners, or webhooks changes production registration data. Only do that when a human named the expo and the write. HTTP errors (from the spec) Status Meaning --- --- 400 Bad request / expo not found 401 Invalid API key 403 Key lacks write permission 404 Resource not found 422 Validation failed ( errors[] with field and description ) 429 Rate limit — wait and retry Next The five Salesforce pipelines, webhook endpoints, and what happens after Visit notifies us.","path":"/operations/systems/visit-create-api"},{"title":"Visit Create workflows","sidebarTitle":"Salesforce workflows","description":"How Visit Create webhooks land in connect-prod: five isolated pipelines, Marketplace lookup, logging, email, and recovery.","headings":["Shared pattern","The five pipelines","Contact and registration match","Active wiring (from sf-visit-webhook README)","Hosted Buyer","eTourism Summit","Brand Experience + Industry Supplier","Marketplace 2026 webhook list (live)","After insert","Recovery","Next"],"body":"Canonical code: sf-visit-webhook . The older sanitised template is visit-to-salesforce-webhooks-automation-workflows . This page is the Connect wiring. The planner-facing path after a hosted-buyer row exists is Hosted buyer operations. Five pipelines share a pattern and are kept in separate Apex classes so a change for Marketplace buyers cannot regress eTourism or BizBash. Last reviewed against the repo README and the live Marketplace 2026 webhook list on 12 August 2026 . Shared pattern Visit has no body on the notify. The receiver pulls . It always returns HTTP 200 so Visit does not escalate; failures are logged instead. Processors run as the VisitWebhook Site Guest User . VisitRecordSync queries in system mode and must not filter on the guest user's FLS. Receivers also support a legacy POST that accepts a JSON array and skips the pre-filter. The five pipelines Pipeline Site path Type filter Processor / mapper Object --- --- --- --- --- Hosted Buyer /visit/webhook/ 1ze3uz619 VisitRegistrationProcessor / VisitFieldMapper (139 question IDs) Registration c Connect Supplier /visit/supplier/ 1ze7qthhh , 1zenu5xxe SupplierRegistrationProcessor / SupplierFieldMapper (65 IDs) Supplier Registration c eTourism (Destination Marketer) /visit/etourism/ 1zhfq3ufw eTourismRegistrationProcessor / eTourismFieldMapper (13 IDs) Registration c Brand Experience Pass /visit/brand-experience/ 1zhfq3ug0 BrandExperienceRegistrationProcessor / BrandExperienceFieldMapper Registration c ( Registration Category c = Brand Experience ) Industry Supplier Pass /visit/industry-supplier/ 1zhfq3ug1 IndustrySupplierRegistrationProcessor / IndustrySupplierFieldMapper Registration c Hosted-buyer stage mapping from Visit registrationState : cancelled / unregistered → Cancelled; pendingConfirmation → Pending Confirmation; otherwise Registered. Fully Registered is not set by the webhook. Supplier pipeline calls Visit twice (once per type), merges IDs, and will not create a new row on a cancellation event. eTourism hard-codes Appointment Track c = 'eTourism Summit' and uses the Meeting/Sales Contact record type for new Contacts. Brand Experience dispatches form fields by Visit form.id and picks Contact record type by expo family (Sports vs BizBash). Visa and passport questions are intentionally unmapped (PII policy). Contact and registration match Hosted buyer / eTourism / pass pipelines: 1. Contact: email → first+last → first+last+company → create. 2. Registration, marketplace-scoped: Visit Visitor Id c + Visit Expo Id c → Registrant c + Marketplace c → email+ Marketplace c . Supplier uses Visit Registration Key c as the stable key and marks the Contact as a Connect Meetings Supplier. If Marketplace c.Visit Expo Id c does not match the incoming expo, the processor skips with an activity-log error. Active wiring (from sf-visit-webhook README) Marketplace Salesforce IDs below are from that README, not from a live SOQL of Visit Expo Id c (this session's Salesforce user saw that field as null — treat as FLS). Hosted Buyer Event Visit expo Webhook README status --- --- --- --- Spring Marketplace 2026 05iui498hhbpn 1ool7nylzf9ap Active MEGA / Connect Marketplace 2026 3512dko89j55x 2hbdc022kyqqu Active — confirmed live 12 Aug 2026, state=wait , last send the same day Connect North 2026 3w3prdavo639i 3mdm2a1zigrdf Active South + Faith 2026 21v0o8zgtegpq 3ibni3fc35mm5 Active West 2026 3lc7u93zax9al 23w8y61j2ishn Active Texas 2026 1g6fkh9uc4bhd 2m2pa54iut0vu Active Winter Marketplace 2026 14d0ea7t27s15 (live API) not in README README still said pending Spring Marketplace 2027 0d1ppa08k8lhu 2zrnq9pukhr1d Active eTourism Summit Event Visit expo Webhook --- --- --- 2026 31p4239o58hz9 280xwgim6kpie 2027 0t5po900ozhkm 3unla0tbcnnu7 eTourism Marketplace 2026 ( 3ifgqc5icj4qz ) is live in Visit and not in this table. Brand Experience + Industry Supplier Each Sports / BizBash expo has two visitor webhooks, both pointing at the same two Apex endpoints. Event Visit expo --- --- Sports Innovation Forum 2026 0e2lrg6h41qxa Sports Leadership Summit 2026 0w5jkrrwco7yj BizBash Innovation Forum 2026 2a5gwo7scw0ci Experiential Leadership Summit 2026 0irj7vuovwdwg Marketplace 2026 webhook list (live) visit list webhooks on 3512dko89j55x , 12 August 2026: ID Type Enabled State Target --- --- --- --- --- 2hbdc022kyqqu visitor yes wait Salesforce Site /visit/webhook 3lythtvqv0sqv visitor yes wait Salesforce Site /visit/supplier 1kjz88r0jp8nf visitor no error placeholder URL 3rmmr473mnlwv visitor no wait Power Automate (secret in URL — do not copy) 3s8ebi8p3b5en order yes error Zapier (7 errors; last send 20 Nov 2025) After insert Registration Created Email Notification (after-insert Flow, system context) emails once when CreatedById is the VisitWebhook guest user. From: attendeeinfo@informa.com . Notification Sent At c is the send-once stamp. Processors must not also call RegistrationEmailNotifier on insert — that caused duplicate mail on 4 August 2026 for eTourism and Industry Supplier. Supplier Registration c has no notification Flow. That is intentional. Recovery The repo also ships: - VisitIngestionRecovery / VisitRecoveryDrainBatch / Visit Recovery Queue c — drain overflow when a notify would exceed 50 Queueables - VisitReconciliationBatch / VisitReconciliationScheduler — poll Visit by revision and backfill missed visitors - Visit Ingestion Ops Lightning app, dashboard, and reports Monitor Visit Webhook Log c , Visit Webhook Activity c , Setup → Apex Jobs, and webhook state=error in Visit. Activity logs store field names , not values. Do not \"improve\" them by writing form answers into MDX or into Change Summary c . Next After the row exists: qualification, TripEasy, appointments, deposit.","path":"/operations/systems/visit-create-workflows"},{"title":"Visit Create","sidebarTitle":"Platform overview","description":"Visit Create is the registration platform behind Connect marketplaces, eTourism, and Sports/BizBash passes. How it maps to Salesforce, and which expos are live.","headings":["Official resources","Login and product","API and developer","Help centre (operators)","Connect inbound (ours)","What it is for us","Registration types we actually sync","Live expos (12 August 2026)","Salesforce Marketplace c lookup","What not to do","Next"],"body":"Visit Create is the event-registration and visitor-data platform Connect uses for hosted buyers, suppliers, eTourism destination marketers, and Sports/BizBash pass holders. The admin UI is create.visitcloud.com. The product API is Create v2 . This site documents our usage , not the vendor's marketing site. - Captured: 12 August 2026 - API: Create v2 OpenAPI - Base: https://api.visitcloud.com/create/v2 - Salesforce named credential: Visit API - Code: sf-visit-webhook - Agent tooling: visit-create MCP and the visit-create-api plugin skill Do not put API keys, webhook signing tokens, or visitor records on this site. Official resources Checked live 12 August 2026 . Prefer the interactive Create v2 docs over the dated PDFs when they disagree. API keys and webhooks are created in the Create UI under Organisation → API . Login and product Resource URL What it is --- --- --- Visit Create login create.visitcloud.com/login Admin UI for expos, forms, registration types, API keys, and webhooks. Vendor PDF names this as Visit Create. Forgot password create.visitcloud.com/forgotpassword Create account reset Visit Connect login connect.visitcloud.com/login Exhibitor / partner portal and app. Separate product from Create. Visit (vendor) visitcloud.com GES Visit platform home Integrations overview visitcloud.com/event-software-integrations Vendor page that points at the Create API console www.visitcreate.com did not resolve from this environment. Use create.visitcloud.com for login. API and developer Resource URL What it is --- --- --- Interactive Create v2 docs api.visitcloud.com/create/docs Current browser console. Try endpoints with an expo ID and API key. OpenAPI spec create-v2.json Machine-readable v2. Base URL https://api.visitcloud.com/create/v2 . JSON API v2 PDF JSON API V2.pdf Vendor write-up (March 2023). Names Create and Connect login URLs. Treat as source-dated against the live spec. Create API PDF create API-4.pdf Second vendor PDF on the Create API. Also source-dated March 2023. Older API PDF Create-Visits-API.pdf September 2022. Describes XML and JSON as interchangeable. Do not use for new work. Legacy API console api.visitcloud.com/legacy Old console. The v2 docs page links here as \"View old API console\". Create keys and Advanced API Organisation → API How to add keys, IP allowlists, event scope, background tasks, endpoint functions, call statistics. Technical integrations Integrations PSP and other technical setup. Connections API Connect knowledgebase Partner lead-retrieval API. Not the Create webhook we use for Salesforce. Our Company Brain map of the same API: Visit Create API. Help centre (operators) Resource URL What it is --- --- --- Create knowledgebase help.visitcloud.com/create User guide, videos, what's new, on-site tools Connect knowledgebase help.visitcloud.com/connect Visit Connect help Accounts Accounts User access in Create Registration types Add and manage registration types Badge / form categories ( 1ze3uz619 and the rest) Visitors Manage visitors Service Centre visitor records Visit Connect from Create Configure Visit Connect Event-level Connect settings Video tutorials Video tutorials Feature walkthroughs What's new What's new Release notes Connect inbound (ours) Resource URL What it is --- --- --- Hosted Buyer webhook https://connectmeetings.my.salesforce-sites.com/VisitWebhook/services/apexrest/visit/webhook Salesforce Site. Visit notifies this; we pull visitors. Supplier webhook same host, /visit/supplier Connect supplier pipeline eTourism / Brand Experience / Industry Supplier /visit/etourism , /visit/brand-experience , /visit/industry-supplier The other three pipelines. See workflows. Do not paste webhook URLs that contain sig= or other secrets. The Site paths above have none. What it is for us An expo in Visit is one event instance. A visitor is a person registered against that expo. A partner is an exhibiting company. A registration type is the badge / form category. Almost every API call is scoped by expoId . Connect uses Visit as the system of capture. Salesforce connect-prod is the system of record after the webhook lands. The human path after that is Hosted buyer operations. The five Apex pipelines are on Visit Create workflows. The HTTP surface is on Visit Create API. Visit concept Salesforce --- --- Expo Marketplace c , matched on Visit Expo Id c Visitor (hosted buyer, eTourism, brand/industry pass) Registration c + Contact (+ Account) Visitor (Connect supplier) Supplier Registration c + Contact + Account Visitor / order / webhook receipt Visit Webhook Log c , Visit Webhook Activity c Registration type Hard-coded filter per pipeline (see below) Registration types we actually sync IDs are organisation-level in Visit and shared across expos. Listed from the live GET /registrationTypes/ expoId on Connect Marketplace 2026 ( 3512dko89j55x ) and the sf-visit-webhook README. Pipeline Visit type ID Visit name Writes to --- --- --- --- Hosted Buyer 1ze3uz619 Hosted Buyer Registration c Connect Supplier 1ze7qthhh Supplier Supplier Registration c Connect Supplier (no appointments) 1zenu5xxe Supplier Non-Appointment Takers Supplier Registration c eTourism Summit 1zhfq3ufw Destination Marketer (README name). Live list also has eTourism - Destination Marketer as 1zigwlgvs Registration c Brand Experience Pass 1zhfq3ug0 Brand Experience Registration c Industry Supplier Pass 1zhfq3ug1 Industry Supplier Pass Registration c The organisation also has dozens of unused or other-division types (XO\\ \\ Informa events, demo, press, speaker). Do not invent a pipeline for a type that is not in the table above. The eTourism Summit pipeline filters on 1zhfq3ufw . The Marketplace 2026 type list also returns 1zigwlgvs / 1zigwlgvt as eTourism Destination Marketer / Travel Industry Supplier. Do not assume those IDs are interchangeable with 1zhfq3ufw . Confirm before changing a filter. Live expos (12 August 2026) visit list expos against the connected API key returned 29 expos. Production n","path":"/operations/systems/visit-create"},{"title":"Departments","sidebarTitle":"","description":"The eight areas of the business, who owns each one, and what they are accountable for.","headings":["What each area actually does","Domain hubs","Next"],"body":"Company OS distinguishes areas from projects : areas are ongoing ownership, projects end. These are the areas. Area Owner Remit --- --- --- Leadership Matt Johnson Strategy, priorities, decisions, company-wide communication Sales Derek Rodriguez Supplier packages, media and advertising sales across Connect Marketplace, BizBash, TSNN, and Connect Travel Marketing Jordan Bouldin Campaigns, media and advertising products, event promotion, audience growth Operations Rachel Piper (see note) Registration, attendee data, account routing, data quality, reporting Events Tinsley Conway Event delivery and logistics for Connect Marketplace and all live events Technology & AI Mac Kitchin Salesforce org, integrations, AI agents, automation, and the Notion workspace Customer Success Unassigned Supplier and client services: onboarding, support, retention Finance Stephen Han Billing, contracts, payment terms, revenue operations One open ownership gap. Customer Success has no recorded area owner, even though three managers work in it. Finance is owned by Stephen Han in this site; Master People has no Stephen Han row and Master Areas still has an empty Finance owner. There is also a conflict on Operations: Team HQ names Rachel Piper as area owner while the Areas database names Andrew Dysart. Both are logged on Known issues. What each area actually does The commercial engine, and the largest team. Three sub-organisations: Connect marketplace sales under Suzi Schnell, BizBash under Michela Giovannotto, media and travel under Andrew Dysart. Sells appointment packages, sponsorships, event amplification, and media insertion orders. Territories are Midwest, Northeast, South, West, and International, derived from account billing state rather than a separate territory object. The key commercial fact: renewals win at 84.6 percent and average around \\$18.8K, while new business wins at 67.2 percent and averages around \\$10.1K. Rebooking is where the money is. Owns everything between a registration and a clean dataset: hosted buyer registration and qualification, attendee data, account routing, data quality, and reporting. Four Hosted Buyer Experience Managers handle the registration lifecycle. Bill Mathis holds hosted buyer data. Operations owns the Connect and BizBash data consolidation project. Delivery and logistics for the live Connect marketplaces, sports events, and Connect Travel events: venues, agendas, room blocks, receptions, onsite operations. A Meetings & Events Director, two Meetings Managers, and a Coordinator under Tinsley Conway. Campaigns, event promotion, audience growth, and the media and advertising products themselves. Digital marketing, design, and coordination in-house; editorial is largely freelance and reports separately. The Salesforce org, its integrations, the Agentforce agent roster, automation, and the Notion Company OS workspace. Two people - Mac Kitchin as AI & Innovation Lead, Wei Zheng as Information Systems Manager - and all four active company projects sit here or partner with here. Supplier and client onboarding, support, and retention. Functionally real but structurally distributed: its three managers each report into a different sales leader, and no area owner is recorded. Billing, contracts, payment terms, revenue operations. Runs through FinancialForce invoicing and the AdBook order lifecycle rather than a dedicated in-unit team. Area owner: Stephen Han ( stephen.han@informa.com ). He is not yet in Master People; Master Areas still has no Finance owner. Strategy, prioritisation, and company-wide communication. Matt Johnson as Managing Director, Derek Rodriguez as EVP Connect Meetings, Kimberly Collie as Strategic Director. Domain hubs Two areas maintain their own deep knowledge bases rather than living entirely in the department page. Objects, fields, relationships, automation, and integrations for connect-prod . Email-mined supplier FAQs, tribal knowledge, and automation opportunities. Next Which person to go to for which system, dataset, or decision.","path":"/people/departments"},{"title":"Directory","sidebarTitle":"Staff directory","description":"Full people directory for Connect Meetings & Events with role, email, team, and location.","headings":["Leadership","Sales","Operations","Events","Marketing and content","Technology & AI","Finance","Customer Success","External - contractors and freelancers","Offices","Next"],"body":"Mirrored from Master People in Notion Company OS, the canonical people record, with emails captured 27 August 2026. All recorded addresses on this page are @informa.com . Leadership Name Role Email Location --- --- --- --- Andrew Mullins Informa Executive (external to Connect) — - Matt Johnson Managing Director matt.johnson@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Derek Rodriguez EVP, Connect Meetings derek.rodriguez@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Kimberly Collie Strategic Director kimberly.collie@informa.com Chicago, 300 S. Riverside Plaza Sales Name Role Email Location --- --- --- --- Andrew Dysart VP, Media & Travel andrew.dysart@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Suzi Schnell VP, Connect suzi.schnell@informa.com Remote Michela Giovannotto VP, BizBash michela.giovannotto@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Matthew Lee Sr. Director of Sales matthew.lee@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Keenan Medlock SVP, North Region keenan.medlock@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Chris Conwell VP, Group Media chris.conwell@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Ashleigh Trout VP, Travel ashleigh.trout@informa.com Remote Alex Conwell VP, Sales alex.conwell@informa.com Remote Monica Kelser VP, Sales monica.kelser@informa.com Remote Bennett Carpenter VP, Event Experience Sales bennett.carpenter@informa.com Norcross, 15 Technology Parkway South Chelsea Justice Brand Sales Director chelsea.justice@informa.com Remote Taylor Snow Sports Director taylor.snow@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Chela Nicholson National Account Director chela.nicholson@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Jacob Sylak Sales Manager jacob.sylak@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Jean Nelson Regional Sales Manager jean.nelson@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Emma Wray Regional Sales Manager emma.wray@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Daniel Schulz Sales Account Manager daniel.schulz@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Ashley Chambers Brand Sales Executive ashley.chambers@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Clinton Perez Sales Development Representative clinton.perez@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Claudia Curry Sales claudia.curry@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Operations Name Role Email Location --- --- --- --- Rachel Piper VP, Hosted Buyer & Industry Relations rachel.piper@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Bill Mathis Hosted Buyer Data Manager bill.mathis@informa.com Remote Caroline Brass Hosted Buyer Experience Manager caroline.brass@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Alexa Dye Hosted Buyer Experience Manager alexa.dye@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Brittni Clark Hosted Buyer Experience Manager brittni.clark@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Madi Groulx Hosted Buyer Experience Manager madi.groulx@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Events Name Role Email Location --- --- --- --- Tinsley Conway VP, Meetings & Events tinsley.conway@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Erica Miller Meetings & Events Director erica.miller@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Hannah Miller Meetings Manager hannah.miller@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Madison Kobb Meetings Manager madison.kobb@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Madeline Wingfield Meetings Coordinator madeline.wingfield@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Marketing and content Name Role Email Location --- --- --- --- Jordan Bouldin VP, Marketing & Content jordan.bouldin@informa.com Remote Danica Tormohlen VP, Content danica.tormohlen@informa.com Remote Sarah Kloepple Editorial Director sarah.kloepple@informa.com Remote Sarah Whiting Digital Marketing Manager sarah.whiting@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Amanda Dennihy Marketing Specialist amanda.dennihy@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Abby Ramsey Marketing Coordinator abby.ramsey@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Grace Richey Marketing Coordinator grace.richey@informa.com Remote Clint Poy Graphic Designer clint.poy@informa.com Remote Technology & AI Name Role Email Location --- --- --- --- Mac Kitchin AI & Innovation Lead mac.kitchin@informa.com Norcross, 3740 Davinci Court Wei Zheng Information Systems Manager wei.zheng@informa.com Remote Finance Name Role Email Location --- --- --- --- Stephen Han Finance area owner stephen.han@informa.com — Stephen Han is the Finance area owner as of 27 August 2026. He is not in Master People. Email is from the active connect-prod User record. Location and reporting line are unconfirmed. Master Areas still has no Finance owner. Customer Success Name Role Email Reports to --- --- --- --- Orlon Daniels Customer Success Manager orlon.daniels@informa.com Andrew Dysart Kimberly Dorf Customer Success Manager kimberly.dorf@informa.com Suzi Schnell Monica Garcia Customer Success Manager monica.garcia@informa.com Michela Giovannotto External - contractors and freelancers Name Role Email Attached to --- --- --- --- Adam Manno Contractor adam.manno@informa.com Matt Johnson Matthew Swenson Contractor matthew.swenson@informa.com Matt Johnson Claire Hoffman Freelance Writer claire.hoffman@informa.com Matt Johnson Michele Laufik Freelance Writer michele.laufik@informa.com Matt Johnson Shannon Thaler Freelance Writer shannon.thaler@informa.com Matt Johnson John Rice Contractor john.rice@informa.com Derek Rodriguez Cole Dysart Contractor cole.dysart@informa.com Derek Rodriguez Parker Bakarich Contractor parker.bakarich@informa.com Michela Giovannotto Jordan Bullard Contractor jordan.bullard@informa.com Rachel Piper Karissa Autry Contractor karissa.autry@informa.com Rachel Piper Offices Office Who is based there --- --- US Norcross - 3740 Davinci Court Leadership, most of Sales, Events, Operations, part of Marketing US Norcross - 15 Technology Parkway South Bennett Carpenter, Event Experience Sales US Chicago - 300 S. Riverside Plaza Kimberly Collie, Strategic Director US Home (remote) Sales, Marketing, Customer Success, contractors","path":"/people/directory"},{"title":"Org chart","sidebarTitle":"Organisation chart","description":"Reporting lines for Connect Meetings & Events, from the Informa group down to individual contributors.","headings":["Top of house","Sales, under Derek Rodriguez","Operations, under Rachel Piper","Events, under Tinsley Conway","Marketing, under Jordan Bouldin","Reading the structure","Next"],"body":"Sourced from Master People in Notion Company OS, which is the canonical people record. Roughly 50 people plus contractors and freelancers. Top of house Sales, under Derek Rodriguez Operations, under Rachel Piper Events, under Tinsley Conway Marketing, under Jordan Bouldin Known discrepancy. Master People records Alexa Dye as reporting to Rachel Piper, while Matt Johnson's direct-reports rollup also lists her. The Rachel Piper line is treated as correct here. Logged on Known issues. Reading the structure A few things that are not obvious from the boxes. - Sales is the largest function by far and is split three ways: Suzi Schnell runs Connect marketplace sales, Michela Giovannotto runs BizBash, Andrew Dysart runs media and travel. Matthew Lee sits alongside them as Sr. Director of Sales. - Technology & AI reports into Sales , not into a separate technology function. Mac Kitchin and Wei Zheng both sit under Andrew Dysart. This is why the AI roadmap is framed in commercial hours-saved terms. - Customer Success is distributed , not a department. Its three managers report into different sales leaders: Orlon Daniels to Andrew Dysart, Kimberly Dorf to Suzi Schnell, Monica Garcia to Michela Giovannotto. - Editorial sits partly outside Marketing. Sarah Kloepple reports directly to Matt Johnson, and Danica Tormohlen reports to Kimberly Collie, while the marketing team reports to Jordan Bouldin. - A meaningful share of content is contract , with five freelance writers and contractors attached mostly to editorial and BizBash. - Finance is an area, not a reporting box in Master People. Stephen Han is the area owner. He has no Master People row, so this chart does not invent a manager. Next Role, email, team, and location for everyone in Master People.","path":"/people/org-chart"},{"title":"People and ownership","sidebarTitle":"Overview","description":"Choose the right people reference for responsibilities, decisions, reporting lines, or contact details.","headings":[],"body":"The people section answers four different questions. Use the page that matches the task. What each ongoing business area is accountable for and where ownership remains unresolved. The authoritative routing table for systems, datasets, commercial decisions, and escalations. Reporting lines from Informa leadership through Connect teams. Staff roles, locations, and approved @informa.com addresses. A manager is not automatically the owner of a system or policy. Use Who owns what for decisions and exceptions. Known ownership conflicts remain on Known issues.","path":"/people/overview"},{"title":"Who owns what","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Ownership index - which person to go to for which system, dataset, or decision.","headings":["Systems and data","AI and automation","Events and commercial","Unowned","The escalation rule","Next"],"body":"If you need a decision, a permission, or an authoritative answer, this is the routing table. Systems and data Thing Owner Notes --- --- --- Salesforce org connect-prod - schema and metadata Mac Kitchin Refreshed via sf sobject describe and sf data query Client and account data in Salesforce Andrew Dysart Notion documents structure only, never record data Sales process and business process catalogue Andrew Dysart Business Processes catalogue in the Salesforce KB Reporting dashboards Mac Kitchin Live in connect-prod , not indexed in Notion. Company Brain dashboard index is Working Hosted buyer data Bill Mathis Registration and attendee datasets Information systems Wei Zheng Notion Company OS structure and conventions Mac Kitchin PARA navigation, master databases, naming and archive rules Projects and initiatives register Mac Kitchin Master Projects, one row per initiative Tasks Mac Kitchin Master Tasks. Legacy Tasks, My Tasks, and Claude Tasks databases are deprecated People directory Mac Kitchin Master People is the data source; Team HQ is the front door Company terminology Mac Kitchin Master Docs glossary, plus a Salesforce-specific glossary SOPs and process documentation Mac Kitchin Company-wide SOPs in Master Docs; Salesforce SOPs in the SF KB AI and automation Thing Owner Notes --- --- --- AI agent documentation Mac Kitchin AI & Automation Hub plus the AI & Agent Library Prompt library Mac Kitchin Canonical merged index of prompts Supplier knowledge, email-mined Mac Kitchin FAQs, articles, tribal knowledge, gaps, automation opportunities Customer FAQ answers Mac Kitchin ~31 grounded Q&A pairs with confidence scores; agents cite these Events and commercial Thing Owner Notes --- --- --- Event information, calendar, operational resources Mac Kitchin Live Events is the single authoritative event source, one page per event Event delivery and logistics Tinsley Conway Hosted buyer experience and industry relations Rachel Piper Connect marketplace sales Suzi Schnell BizBash sales Michela Giovannotto Media and travel sales Andrew Dysart Regional event sponsorship inquiries sales@connectmeetings.com BizBash and Sports Innovation Forum sponsorship bizbash.sales@informa.com Event amplification, regional Andrew Dysart Billing, invoices, payment terms Stephen Han Finance area owner. Email stephen.han@informa.com from connect-prod . Not yet in Master People; Master Areas Finance owner is still empty Unowned These have no recorded owner and are the clearest gaps in the ownership map. - Customer Success as an area, despite having three managers working in it - Company policies - marked Working, HR and legal policies not yet imported - Training materials and onboarding - marked Working, needs role-specific steps per department - Notion and Zapier automations - not yet documented; record each new workflow in the AI & Agent Library before launch The escalation rule For anything customer-facing that touches billing, legal, contract terms, a specific rate, or a complaint , an agent must not answer. Escalate to a human. That rule is absolute and is reproduced on Guardrails. Next Reporting lines from the Informa group down to individual contributors.","path":"/people/who-owns-what"},{"title":"Event sponsorships","sidebarTitle":"Event sponsorships","description":"Connect Marketplace 2026 sponsorship inventory across booths, signage, activations, hospitality, direct-to-buyer, digital, and add-on products.","headings":["Connect Marketplace 2026","Trade show booths","High-visibility branding and signage","Branded attendee touchpoints","Speaking, content, and visibility activations","Custom on-floor activations","Networking and special-event sponsorships","Regiception","5K Run & Walk","WIST Hot Girl Walk","50 Over 50 Happy Hour","Closing Party sponsorships","Direct-to-buyer products","Sports-specific sponsorships","Digital event sponsorships","Booth add-ons","Event amplification products in the sponsorship deck","Next"],"body":"Sponsorship inventory for Connect Marketplace 2026, taken from the Tampa deck. Availability counts in that deck are not live. Connect Marketplace 2026 The 2026 sponsorship deck covers Connect Marketplace in Tampa Bay, August 24–26, 2026 , and is built around access to 3,000\\+ event professionals . Public Turtl copy: informa.turtl.co/story/connect-marketplace-2026. Event homepage: informaconnect.com/connect-marketplace. Why Sponsor: why-sponsor. Inventory spans traditional exhibit space, high-visibility branding, experiential activations, direct-to-buyer programs, closing-party sponsorships, digital products, and booth add-ons. Prices and availability below reproduce the 2026 deck. Sold status and remaining inventory may have changed. Trade show booths Product Price Source status --- --: --- 10' × 10' booth \\$10,000 Listed 10' × 20' booth \\$15,000 Listed 20' × 20' booth \\$25,000 Listed 30' × 30' booth \\$40,000 Sold out 40' × 40' booth \\$50,000 Sold out High-visibility branding and signage Product Price What it includes --- --: --- Interior hanging signage \\$5,000 Large-format branding in specified convention-center locations Registration windows \\$5,000 per set of 4 Branding across registration windows Escalator side clings \\$5,000 per side Large side clings on escalators Escalator center clings \\$4,000 per center Center escalator branding Exterior doors \\$1,500 per door Branding on exterior entrance doors Exterior double-sided lamp-post banners \\$1,500 per banner Outdoor arrival branding Hanging banners From \\$2,000 per banner 12' × 4', double-sided Marketplace aisle signage \\$15,000 Branded directional signs in appointment areas; exclusive by track Trade-show floor decals \\$15,000 Four branded floor decals featuring floor-map information The deck also lists a digital LED wall near escalators in the Ballroom B and C foyers, with dimensions and pricing available on request. Branded attendee touchpoints Product Price Key inclusion --- --: --- Conference Wi-Fi \\$35,000 Branded username and password; sold in source deck Employee shirts \\$25,000 per day Sponsor logo on Connect staff shirts; all three days sold in source deck Coffee Break \\$30,000 Four marketplace coffee breaks with branded sleeves, napkins, and signage Hydration Station \\$40,000 Water stations across all three days plus reusable branded bottles Connect Cafeteria \\$60,000 Sponsorship of the trade-show-floor cafeteria, hanging banner, signage, seating Bag Check \\$20,000 Branded luggage tags, signage, website and event listing Welcome Gift \\$15,000 Track-specific welcome gift, branded bag, onsite table, optional insert Conference Swag \\$15,000 Choice of three branded items for planner gift bags Schedule at a Glance \\$15,000 Exclusive branding on the digital schedule card Planner Table Tents \\$7,500 Track-exclusive logo placement on planner table tents Conference Bags \\$10,000 Logo on planner bags across tracks Lanyards \\$30,000 Sponsor of attendee lanyards plus website logo Conference Name Badges \\$30,000 Logo on all attendee badges; sold in source deck Passport Listing \\$3,000 Listing in event passport to drive booth traffic Marketplace Countdown Clock \\$15,000 Sponsor logo/image on countdown clock; track-based availability Speaking, content, and visibility activations Product Price Key inclusion --- --: --- Speaking Opportunity \\$15,000 Five-minute brand representative speaking slot before an education session; subject to content-team approval Speaker Showcase \\$60,000 Brand visibility around Speaker Showcase; sold in source deck Full-page Marketplace Guide ad \\$7,500 Full-page ad in official conference program distributed to 3,000\\+ attendees Safari Selfies \\$15,000 Branded photo-op with exotic animals, branded photo frame, pre-event email Afternoon Amp-Up \\$15,000 per day Energy drinks delivered to hosted buyers, branded napkins Vibe Sponsor From \\$5,000 per day DJ/live performance, activation, or interactive installation support Custom on-floor activations The deck explicitly encourages custom concepts and lists example starting prices: Activation Starting price Source status --- --: --- Puppy Lounge \\$40,000 Sold Speaker Showcase \\$60,000 Sold Wellness Lounge \\$40,000 Sold Sports Bar \\$40,000 Available in source deck Pickleball Tournament \\$40,000 Available in source deck Gifting Lounge \\$50,000 Sold Headshot Suite \\$25,000 Available in source deck Networking and special-event sponsorships Regiception \\$75,000 — sold in source deck. Includes a \\$10,000 F&B credit, registration-area branding, website placement, and official agenda listing. 5K Run & Walk \\$20,000 — sold in source deck. Includes official sponsorship, finish-line shirts, post-run refreshments, and entertainment. WIST Hot Girl Walk \\$20,000. Day-two women's sports networking walk with coffee/drinks, branded napkins and signage, and official agenda placement. 50 Over 50 Happy Hour TSNN's 50 Over 50 happy hour offers modular sponsorship inventory: Product Price --- --: Title Sponsor \\$20,000 Specialty Cocktail \\$5,000 Gifting Sponsor \\$12,000 No Small Talk table cards \\$2,000 Legacy Wall \\$5,000 Held in Hand branded glassware \\$7,500 Awards \\$15,000 Closing Party sponsorships Product Price Source status / notes --- --: --- Specialty Cocktail Bar \\$15,000 7 available in source deck Sponsored Hats \\$20,000 Sold Meet & Greet Presenting Sponsor \\$25,000 Sold; 25 tickets plus logo on tickets/photos LED Foam Sticks \\$15,000 1 available in source deck VIP Seating Area \\$15,000 Sold out Meet & Greet Ticket Package \\$5,000 Tickets for sponsor distribution Friendship Bracelet Wall \\$12,500 Branded wall plus 3,000 bracelets Networking Cards \\$12,500 Branded conversation cards plus agenda placement Direct-to-buyer products Product Price What it does --- --: --- Hosted Buyer Gifting Lounge \\$50,000 Exclusive hosted-buyer gifting experience; sold in source deck Pre-FAM \\$15,000 Sponsor hosts 10–15 planners in a nearby city before or after Connect Planner Checkout \\$7,500 per t","path":"/products-services/event-sponsorships"},{"title":"Media and amplification","sidebarTitle":"Media and amplification","description":"Starting prices from the 2026 Events | Travel | Sports media kit.","headings":["Media network","Owned media and awareness","Paid media and retargeting","Sports extras","Email newsletters","Dedicated eblasts","Thought leadership","Awards partnerships","Print solutions","Event amplification","Overlap with Connect Marketplace sponsorships","Compared with the 2025 kit","Related pages"],"body":"- Purpose: Source-dated rate card for owned, paid, email, print, thought leadership, and event-amplification products. - Audience: Sales, media, and approved internal agents. - Canonical source: 2026 Events Travel Sports media kit PDF ( BizBash MediaKit 2026-5 1.pdf ), captured 27 August 2026, published in Master Docs. - Owner: Media and travel sales — Andrew Dysart; BizBash — Michela Giovannotto. - Source status: Working. Notion verification is still Unverified. - Last reviewed: 27 August 2026. Audience figures and prices on this page are source-dated from the 2026 kit PDF. Do not treat them as live inventory. Print copy inside that PDF still describes 2024/2025 issues. Do not apply these rates to a 2026 Connect Marketplace sponsorship proposal without current sales confirmation. Media network The kit presents BizBash, Trade Show News Network (TSNN), and Corporate Event News as one events-industry media network. Figures below are only those clearly labelled in the extracted 2026 PDF. The audience/social spread is layout-ambiguous. Do not total or average the split lines. Measure as labelled in the 2026 PDF Figure --- --- Digital users (2024) 1.8M combined line; BizBash 1.3M, TSNN 430,000, CEN 55,000 Newsletter subscribers 60,000+ combined line; BizBash 40,000+, BizBash Sports 1,500+, TSNN 9,500+, CEN 9,000+ Active subscribers (approx.) 215,000 combined line; Connect 85,000, BizBash 190,000, TSNN/CEN 40,000 Social headlines Facebook 66,800; LinkedIn 64,462; Instagram 76,959; Event Pros Gather LinkedIn Group 80,704 The three “active subscribers” parts do not add to 215,000. Brand-level social splits on that spread are not reliable from text extraction. The 2025 kit used a different metric set (combined social 378K, monthly uniques 164K, owned-channel views 411K, newsletter subscribers 93.8K). That is not a rounding of the 2026 lines. Both are on Known issues. Public Turtl copy of the 2025 kit: Events \\ Travel \\ Sports 2025 media kit. Brand sites: bizbash.com, tsnn.com. Owned media and awareness Product Starting price Delivery in the 2026 kit --- --: --- Onsite Display Ads \\$3,000+ 100K impressions alongside daily editorial content. Footnote: TSNN starting at \\$5,000 per week Sponsored Social Media \\$3,000+ Curated posts on Instagram and LinkedIn Bookends \\$7,500+ Website-wide for 1 week, linked to the client site Big Sky \\$3,000+ per day Large ad experience using static text, imagery, and video In-Article Video \\$5,000+ Video integrated within articles Wallpapers (\\$5,500, 5 days) and Interstitials (\\$5,500, 4 days) appear in the 2025 kit and are not named in the 2026 extract. Paid media and retargeting Starting prices match the 2025 page. Product Starting price Delivery --- --: --- Connected TV \\$20,000 125,000 impressions across connected TV, tablet, and phone inventory such as YouTube TV, Prime, Hulu, MLB.tv, ESPN, Roku, Fire TV, and more Online Radio \\$10,000 100,000 plays across audio environments such as Pandora, Spotify, iHeart Media, Audacy, and more Social Media Retargeting \\$10,000+ 1,000 guaranteed clicks from Facebook, Instagram, and web placements Banner Retargeting \\$10,000+ 930,233 impressions across the web Video Pre-Roll \\$10,000+ 100,000 completed views of a 15- or 30-second video Content Activation \\$10,000+ 1,000 clicks from native retargeting of sponsored content Sports extras Product Starting price Delivery in the 2026 kit --- --: --- Connect Sports Dedicated Email \\$5,000 Email to sports planners Content Activation—Sports \\$7,500+ 500 clicks; native retargeting of sponsored content to the sports audience BizBash Sports Newsletter Sponsorship \\$4,000 More than 1,600 sports planners Sports Facilities Guide \\$7,000+ Full-page ad Email newsletters Newsletter advertising sits alongside editorial content. Placement is a leaderboard or client-provided advertorial, subject to availability. Newsletter Cadence 2026 kit --- --- --- BizBash Buzz Three times weekly \\$4,000 to 40K event professionals BizBash Sports Sports events coverage \\$4,000; more than 1,600 sports planners Association Show News Twice monthly \\$4,000; more than 8,000 subscribers Corporate Event News Wednesdays \\$4,000 to the corporate database The Industry This Week is on the 2025 page (weekly, \\$4,000 to 13K) and is not named in the 2026 extract. Coverage called out in the 2026 kit: - BizBash Sports — sports-event news - Association Show News — association-sector shows and industry associations - Corporate Event News — corporate event planning, marketing, destinations and venues, people and development, technology, and industry news Dedicated eblasts Dedicated eblasts can carry a brand message, event invitation, or other exclusive marketing initiative to the selected audience. Audience/database Reach Price --- --: --: BizBash database 92K event professionals \\$10,000 Connect database 28K event professionals \\$7,500 TSNN database 13K event professionals \\$5,000 Corporate Event News database 7.2K event professionals \\$5,000 Thought leadership Product Starting price What it is --- --: --- Sponsored Content \\$6,500+ Client-written article on a media platform, with optional photo or video Editorial Sponsorship Not published Brand aligned with expert editorial content Surveys \\$25,000+ Custom survey of industry trends and audience needs White Papers \\$25,000+ Data-driven content produced by media brands E-Books \\$25,000 Informative e-books produced by media brands Webinars \\$25,000+ Media-brand-produced webinar Fast Chat Video \\$10,000 5–10 minute editorially driven video interview Custom Video \\$3,000+ Video produced with the content team around live-event participation Awards partnerships The 2025 kit identified two awards-oriented partnership opportunities without published pricing. They are not named in the 2026 extract. Partnership Focus Source --- --- --- TSNN Awards Trade-show industry innovation, leadership, and event achievement 2025 kit BizBash Event Experience Awards Creative and innovative event design","path":"/products-services/media-amplification"},{"title":"Content activation specs","sidebarTitle":"Content activation","description":"Copy, image, and logo limits for BizBash content activation and native offsite promotion.","headings":["What to submit","Copy and image limits","Related pages"],"body":"- Source: BizBash Content Activation Specs (1).pdf , captured 27 August 2026. - Production inbox on the sheet: bizbashprod@informa.com . The programme promotes a supplier story to the BizBash audience through native placements and allows the audience to be pixelled for later campaigns. Original PDF What to submit - Story URL on the supplier's own site. UTM codes are allowed. Destination redirects are not permitted ; the final URL must match the display URL. - Headlines and description. - Images and logos. - Social handles and branded-content partner approvals. Copy and image limits Element Spec --- --- Headline (short) 30 characters Headline (long) 90 characters Description 90 characters Landscape image 1200 x 628 px, JPG or PNG, 1.91:1, maximum 5120KB Square image 1200 x 1200 px, JPG or PNG, maximum 5120KB Landscape logo 1200 x 300 px, JPG or PNG, 4:1, maximum 5120KB Square logo 1200 x 1200 px, JPG or PNG, maximum 5120KB Avoid filters unless needed to sharpen a photograph. The index sheet also lists a Connect Content Activation product. There is no separate Connect spec in this folder; do not invent one. Related pages Media specs overview · Native ads · Media and amplification","path":"/products-services/media-specs/content-activation"},{"title":"Customer acquisition programmes","sidebarTitle":"Customer acquisition","description":"Source-dated FAM trip recruiting and destination focus-group programmes from the Connect and BizBash sell sheet.","headings":["FAM trip recruiting","Destination focus groups","Related pages"],"body":"- Source: CustomerAcquisitionPrograms.pdf , captured 27 August 2026. - Status: Working sell sheet. Contact the account's sales owner for current availability. Prices below are as printed, not live inventory. Original PDF Connect and BizBash recruit planners for a host destination. The sheet promises a minimum of 10 high-qualified planners and event marketers, with recruitment, coordination, and travel expenses handled by Connect. Never quote these starting prices as a live offer without the sales owner. The sheet says “starting at” and “for an additional cost” for air and rooming lists. FAM trip recruiting Starting at \\$25,000 on the sheet. - Audit of meeting / exhibit space (hotels) or overall space (venue). - Target planners by compatibility with the destination or hotel. - Collect interest for host review before securing attendees. - Air travel and a rooming list are additional cost . Destination focus groups Starting at \\$30,000 on the sheet. - Same space audit and planner review as a FAM. - Pre-arrival survey with the host. - Agenda designed to highlight the host. - Two-hour facilitated session at the end of the programme. - Post-arrival survey after planners return home. - Detailed report within 14 days : participants, pre-arrival results, session discussion, post-event survey. Related pages Media specs overview · Media and amplification · Who owns what","path":"/products-services/media-specs/customer-acquisition"},{"title":"Dedicated email specs","sidebarTitle":"Dedicated email","description":"HTML, subject-line, and plaintext rules for BizBash sponsored eblasts, from the 2025 sheet and a 2024 sheet that still sits in the folder.","headings":["HTML rules that both sheets share","Where the sheets diverge","Subject line","Plaintext version","Best practices printed on the sheets","Related pages"],"body":"- Sources: BizBash Dedicated email specs 2025.pdf and BizBash Dedicated email specs 2024 (2) (1).pdf , captured 27 August 2026. - Production inboxes on the sheets: bizbashprod@informa.com (2025); bizbashprod@informa.com or connectprod@informa.com (2024). A sponsored email has three parts: subject line , HTML body , and a plaintext .txt version . Materials are due five business days before the scheduled send. Production tests the message and sends a test for supplier approval before deployment. The 2025 sheet caps HTML width at 600 pixels . The 2024 sheet says 600–728 pixels and adds a minimum height of 820 pixels . Do not treat one as a silent replacement for the other until production confirms which sheet applies to the booking. HTML rules that both sheets share 2025 PDF · 2024 PDF - Submit a complete HTML file. Images, links, bold, and italics are allowed. - Layers, background images, rollovers, forms, JavaScript, Flash, and CSS are prohibited. - Personalisation is available (first and/or last name, with a default if the name is missing). - Each image about 50KB, JPG or GIF, referenced by absolute URL. BizBash can host images if they are sent with the file. - Do not submit Constant Contact or other copyrighted third-party templates; they are returned. - Put the offer, call to action, dates, location, and links as real text in the top third. Many clients block images. - HTML may be supplied as a link to a dedicated web page of the message. Images to be hosted go in a zip. Where the sheets diverge Rule 2025 sheet 2024 sheet --- --- --- Maximum HTML width 600 pixels 600–728 pixels Minimum HTML height Not stated 820 pixels Inbox bizbashprod@informa.com bizbashprod@informa.com or connectprod@informa.com Subject line Keep it short. Recipients may see only the first few words. Avoid “spammy” terms such as “Free!”, all caps, and heavy use of red or exclamation marks. Plaintext version A .txt file with no images, hyperlinks, or styling. Type every URL in full, including http:// . Best practices printed on the sheets 1. Call to action and click-through at the top (preview pane). 2. One offer. 3. Be succinct. 4. Assume mobile; more than half of email is opened on a phone. Responsive design if possible. Limit the subject line to about 30 characters. 5. Readable type and colour. 6. Production tests the code; the 2025 sheet names http://www.contactology.com/check mqs.php as a spam-score example. Related pages Media specs overview · Newsletters · Media and amplification","path":"/products-services/media-specs/dedicated-email"},{"title":"2025 BizBash editorial calendar","sidebarTitle":"Editorial calendar","description":"Source-dated 2025 BizBash digital, print, and events-on-radar calendar. Not a 2026 calendar.","headings":["Standing digital columns","Print issues on the sheet","Monthly digital features named on the sheet","Events on our radar (as printed)","Related pages"],"body":"- Source: 2025 BizBash Editorial Calendar.pdf , captured 27 August 2026. - Pitch inbox on the sheet: editorial@bizbash.com . This is a 2025 calendar. Some rows on the sheet itself still say 2024 (VidCon, MPI WEC, Accor GME). Do not treat it as the live 2026 plan. The editor's note says events and print content are subject to change. Original PDF Standing digital columns Every month: event strategies, experiential activations, local coverage, Q&amp;As, profiles, and BizBash Sports takeaways, plus: - Event Tech Check - What's New in Event Venues - What's New in Meetings and Trade Shows - Cool Event Ideas You May Have Missed This Month - Top Trending Stories of the Month Print issues on the sheet Issue Timing on the sheet Editorial features named --- --- --- Spring issue March 2025 Event Experience Awards, 40 Under 40, Connect Spring Marketplace Guide; new and renovated U.S./Canada venues Fall issue + Marketplace Guide August 2025 Industry Innovators, Connect Marketplace Guide; event technology and top events Monthly digital features named on the sheet Month Digital coverage / BizBash features printed --- --- January 2025 venue openings; Super Bowl watch-party ideas; Industry Innovators: Catering February Valentine's activations; Industry Innovators: Vendors and suppliers March St Patrick's activations; Industry Innovators: Planners, designers, and suppliers; new/renovated venues for spring April 2025 40 Under 40; Industry Innovators: Event technology May Industry Innovators: Experiential agencies June Spring 2025 galas; Pride activations; Industry Innovators: Brands July Fourth of July activations; Most Influential People from past 25 years; new/renovated summer venues August (print Fall issue) September Summer galas; back-to-school activations; BizBash Sports Power Players October Halloween activations; corporate holiday party ideas November Holiday party ideas from brands; 2025 Most Innovative Meetings; new/renovated fall venues December Fall galas; 2025 trend predictions; Event Experience Awards Events on our radar (as printed) The sheet lists CES, GRAMMYs, Super Bowl, SXSW, NCAA Final Four, Met Gala, Connect Spring Marketplace, Connect Marketplace, Sports Innovation Forum, IMEX America, and others. Several radar rows still carry 2024 event names. Do not update them here. Pitch the editors at editorial@bizbash.com . Related pages Media specs overview · Media and amplification · BizBash","path":"/products-services/media-specs/editorial-calendar"},{"title":"Native ad specs","sidebarTitle":"Native ads","description":"2019 native ad image and headline rules. Dated relative to the rest of the Media Specs folder.","headings":["Specs on this sheet","Related pages"],"body":"- Source: NativeAdSpecs2019.5d83e5c1ea762.pdf , captured 27 August 2026. Filename includes 2019 . - Production inbox on the sheet: production@bizbash.com . This sheet is older than the content-activation spec in the same folder. If a booking is labelled content activation, use Content activation. If it is labelled native ad, use this sheet until production confirms otherwise. Original PDF The ad sits adjacent to relevant editorial. The sheet recommends two sets of creative. The ad must still be clearly distinguishable as advertising. Specs on this sheet Element Rule --- --- Image Static JPEG or GIF File size Maximum 100KB Aspect ratio 16:9 Imagery Keep core messaging centred Headline Up to 75 characters Click-through Required; UTM allowed Third-party tags Not supported Materials due five business days before campaign start. New creative may be submitted at any time; the five-day clock still applies. For long campaigns, update about every three months. Related pages Media specs overview · Content activation","path":"/products-services/media-specs/native-ads"},{"title":"Newsletter specs","sidebarTitle":"Newsletters","description":"Positions and file limits for the BizBash daily e-newsletter, from the Today in Events spec sheet.","headings":["Positions","Title sponsor copy","Display boxes","Advertorial","Submission","Related pages"],"body":"- Source: bizbash daily specs.5c9b88ee7fa47.pdf , titled “Today in Events: Our New Daily eNewsletter”. Captured 27 August 2026. - Production inbox on the sheet: production@bizbash.com . The index sheet in the same folder labels this product BizBash Buzz Newsletter . The spec sheet itself uses Today in Events . Do not collapse the names. Original PDF Positions An Issue Sponsor buys all positions and must supply two different display-box ads. Position Name Dimensions Format --- --- --- --- A Title sponsor See copy rules Text and link B Advertorial See copy rules JPEG plus text C First display box 300 x 250 JPEG, GIF D Second display box 300 x 250 JPEG, GIF A click-through link is required with all creative. Some third-party tracking tags interfere with BizBash link tracking; production will agree which tracker takes priority. Title sponsor copy Maximum 100 characters including spaces . Company name is spelled out as text; logos are not accepted . The click-through is embedded in the company name. Display boxes - File size maximum 80KB - 300 x 250 pixels - Hairline border on light-colour ads - Animated GIF is allowed; the first frame must stand alone because some email clients do not animate - Flash is not accepted Advertorial - Image maximum 80KB , 400 x 267 pixels - Headline maximum 10 words - Body maximum 500 characters (about 100 words), single paragraph only - No logos - Copy may be edited to fit and to match editorial style Submission Email production@bizbash.com . Materials due five business days before the newsletter campaign. Related pages Media specs overview · Dedicated email · Editorial calendar","path":"/products-services/media-specs/newsletters"},{"title":"Offsite banner retargeting specs","sidebarTitle":"Offsite banners","description":"Creative units and file limits for the BizBash + Connect targeted display programme beyond BizBash.com.","headings":["Units","File limits","Submission","Related pages"],"body":"- Source: Offsite Banner Targeted Display (1) (1).pdf , captured 27 August 2026. - Programme name on the sheet: BizBash + Connect Targeted Display Program. - Production inbox on the sheet: bizbashprod@informa.com . The programme places banners on sites the BizBash and Connect audience uses beyond BizBash.com . Existing BizBash or Connect banner creative may be reused. This sheet does not accept most third-party script-based tags, Flash, or link shorteners. That conflicts with the onsite banner sheets, which do accept many third-party tags. Use this sheet only for offsite / retargeting. Units Original PDF Annual Banner Program sales: submit at least three of each unit in the table. Not every unit is required, but the sheet recommends submitting as many as possible. Update creative at least every 90 days on long campaigns, otherwise monthly. Ad unit Dimensions --- --- Tablet leaderboard 728 x 90 Mobile leaderboard Not printed on this sheet Desktop leaderboard 970 x 90 Half page 300 x 600 Billboard 970 x 250 Display box 300 x 250 Skyscraper 160 x 600 A click-through link is required. UTM links are supported. File limits Rule This sheet --- --- Formats Static JPEG or PNG File size 150KB . There is no flexibility; anything over 150KB is rejected Border Clearly defined border or a non-white background Third-party tags Generally not accepted. Confirm any tag with bizbashprod@informa.com before sending The sheet states that the environment blocks adult, hate-speech, torrent, disaster, and other sensitive categories, and that whitelist or blacklist targeting can be set campaign by campaign. Submission Email files to bizbashprod@informa.com . Materials due five business days before campaign start. Related pages Media specs overview · Onsite banners · Media and amplification","path":"/products-services/media-specs/offsite-banners"},{"title":"Onsite banner specs","sidebarTitle":"Onsite banners","description":"BizBash.com banner units, file limits, and submission rules from three overlapping onsite spec sheets.","headings":["Units on Onsite Display AdSpecs1.pdf","Units on Onsite Display Ads (Rev 20220902)","Units on Banner Program Specs","Shared submission rules","Related pages"],"body":"- Source: Onsite Display AdSpecs1.pdf , Onsite Display Ads.60136bb579025.pdf (Rev 20220902), and Banner Program Specs.5ca4c25f3ff31.pdf . Captured 27 August 2026 from Documents/Media Specs . - Production inboxes on the sheets: bizbashprod@informa.com and production@bizbash.com . The three sheets disagree on maximum file size, whether the desktop leaderboard may animate, and the name of the 300 x 50 mobile unit. Report both versions. Do not average them. All three sheets require a click-through link with every creative. Flash is not accepted. Ads must have a clearly defined border so they are not confused with page content. Materials are due five business days before campaign start; rich media and non-standard units need ten additional days . Expected time from complete materials to posting is five business days. Units on Onsite Display AdSpecs1.pdf Original PDF Ad unit Dimensions File types --- --- --- Tablet leaderboard 728 x 90 JPG, GIF, animation accepted Mobile leaderboard 300 x 50 JPG, GIF, static only Desktop leaderboard 970 x 90 JPG, GIF, animation accepted Half page 300 x 600 JPG, GIF, animation accepted Billboard 970 x 250 JPG, GIF, animation accepted Display box 300 x 250 JPG, GIF, animation accepted Individual/CPM sales: follow the unit purchased; multiple files for the same unit are allowed. A Leaderboard campaign on this sheet requires all three leaderboard units . Annual Banner Program: submit at least one of each unit in the table. Campaigns are optimised to the most successful unit. File limits on this sheet: JPEG, GIF, or animated GIF; maximum 100KB ; animation maximum 15 seconds, repeat 3 times. HTML5 is accepted as a zip of all supporting files; compressed size must not exceed 250KB . Third-party tags such as DoubleClick are accepted after confirmation. 1x1 impression pixels and UTM click tracking may be supplied instead of third-party tags. Units on Onsite Display Ads (Rev 20220902) Original PDF Ad unit Dimensions File types --- --- --- Tablet leaderboard 728 x 90 JPG, GIF, animation accepted Desktop leaderboard 970 x 90 JPG, GIF, static only Medium rectangle 300 x 250 JPG, GIF, animation accepted Half page 300 x 600 JPG, GIF, animation accepted Billboard 970 x 250 JPG, GIF, animation accepted Mobile billboard 300 x 50 JPG, GIF, static only This sheet names the 300 x 250 unit Medium rectangle and the 300 x 50 unit Mobile billboard . Banner Program and Display Roadblock both ask for one of each unit in the table. Multiple creatives per position, with separate tracking links, are allowed. File limits on this sheet: JPEG, static or animated GIF, and HTML5; maximum 250KB ; animation maximum 15 seconds, repeat 3 times, maximum 24 fps. Google Ad Manager will reject any ad over 1MB. Third-party tags and impression tags are accepted after confirmation with production@bizbash.com . Units on Banner Program Specs Original PDF Same unit table as Onsite Display AdSpecs1.pdf (tablet 728 x 90, mobile 300 x 50, desktop 970 x 90, half page 300 x 600, billboard 970 x 250, display box 300 x 250). Desktop leaderboard animation accepted . Annual Banner Program: at least one of each unit. Individual/CPM: follow the purchased unit; mobile versions of the leaderboard and billboard are required . File limits on this sheet: JPEG, GIF, or animated GIF; maximum 100KB ; animation maximum 15 seconds, repeat 3 times. Third-party tags such as DoubleClick accepted after confirmation. Custom HTML must be vetted; allow 14 extra days. Third-party and custom HTML tags are not accepted for interstitial programmes. Shared submission rules - Send complete, final materials. Incomplete files restart the five-day clock. - For long campaigns, the sheets recommend new creative about every three months. - Freelance design support may be available at an hourly rate if the supplier cannot meet spec. - Topic and category sponsorships are generated from Premium Listing content on the Banner Program sheet; listing specs are separate and are not in this folder. Related pages Media specs overview · Offsite banners · Media and amplification","path":"/products-services/media-specs/onsite-banners"},{"title":"Media specs","sidebarTitle":"Media specs overview","description":"Internal index of BizBash and Connect creative specifications, production contacts, and lead times captured from the Media Specs folder.","headings":["Standing rules across the folder","Which spec to open","Production inboxes on the sheets","Original files"],"body":"- Audience: Sales, production, and approved internal agents. - Source: OneDrive folder Documents/Media Specs , captured 27 August 2026. - Owner: Media and travel — Andrew Dysart; BizBash — Michela Giovannotto. - Production: bizbashprod@informa.com , production@bizbash.com , and connectprod@informa.com all appear on the sheets. - Status: Working. Sheets disagree with each other; do not pick a winner. Use this section when a supplier has bought media and needs to submit creative. For prices and product definitions start at Media and amplification. For appointment packages start at Packages and published pricing. Several spec sheets cover the same product with different file-size, unit, inbox, and animation rules. Preserve both versions and send the supplier the sheet that matches the product they bought. Conflicts are listed on Known issues. Standing rules across the folder - Materials are due five business days before campaign start on every sheet that states a lead time. - Rich media, HTML5, and non-standard units need ten additional business days on the onsite banner sheets. - A click-through URL is required with all banner and display creative. UTM links are accepted on the offsite and native sheets. - Flash is not accepted on any sheet. - BizBash may reject creative that it considers illegal or otherwise inappropriate. Which spec to open Product Page Source file in the folder --- --- --- Index of products and submission forms This page Media Specs & Creative Links.pdf Onsite web banners Onsite banners Three overlapping sheets Offsite / retargeting banners Offsite banners Offsite Banner Targeted Display Dedicated eblast Dedicated email 2025 sheet and a 2024 sheet Newsletter positions Newsletters bizbash daily specs Content activation Content activation BizBash Content Activation Specs Native ads Native ads NativeAdSpecs2019 2025 editorial calendar Editorial calendar 2025 BizBash Editorial Calendar.pdf FAM / focus-group programme Customer acquisition CustomerAcquisitionPrograms.pdf The same folder also holds media-kit-2025.pdf . Product prices from that kit live on Media and amplification, not here. Production inboxes on the sheets Do not collapse these to a single address. Use the inbox printed on the sheet for the product in hand, or ask the account's sales owner. Inbox Appears on --- --- bizbashprod@informa.com Onsite Display AdSpecs1, offsite banners, dedicated email 2025, content activation production@bizbash.com Banner program specs, onsite display rev 20220902, native ads 2019, newsletter daily specs connectprod@informa.com Dedicated email 2024 sheet (alongside the BizBash Informa inbox) Original files Staff copies of the captured PDFs: - Index of creative links - Onsite display AdSpecs - Onsite display ads, rev 20220902 - Banner program specs - Offsite banner retargeting - Dedicated email 2025 - Dedicated email 2024 - Newsletter daily specs - Content activation - Native ads 2019 - Editorial calendar 2025 - Customer acquisition programmes","path":"/products-services/media-specs/overview"},{"title":"Products & services","sidebarTitle":"Products and services overview","description":"Commercial products across Connect Marketplace sponsorships, media, thought leadership, print, and event amplification.","headings":["Commercial offer at a glance","Audience and network context","How the offer fits together","Next"],"body":"This page maps the commercial offer across sponsorships, media, and event amplification. Prices here are source-dated, not live inventory. Public kits and decks: 2025 Events \\ Travel \\ Sports media kit and Connect Marketplace 2026 sponsorship deck. Event URLs: Public event sites. Commercial offer at a glance Connect Meetings & Events sells a mix of live-event sponsorships , digital and paid media , email , thought leadership , print , and event amplification products. The two source documents used for this section are the Connect Marketplace 2026 sponsorship deck and the Events Travel Sports 2025 Media Kit . Prices and availability in this section are source-dated. The Connect Marketplace sponsorship deck is for Tampa Bay, August 24–26, 2026; the media kit is dated 2025. “Sold,” “sold out,” and availability counts reflect the documents at the time they were produced, not current inventory. Booths, signage, onsite activations, hospitality, hosted-buyer access, closing-party placements, digital event sponsorships, and booth add-ons. Onsite display ads, wallpapers, interstitials, sponsored social, newsletters, and dedicated eblasts across BizBash, TSNN, Corporate Event News, and Connect audiences. Connected TV, online radio, social retargeting, banner retargeting, video pre-roll, and native content activation. Sponsored content, editorial sponsorship, surveys, white papers, e-books, webinars, and awards partnerships. BizBash seasonal issues, Sports Facilities Guide, Texas Meeting Planners Guide, and Marketplace Guides. Pre/post-show email, event targeting, lead insights, custom video, and broader multi-channel event promotion. Audience and network context The 2026 Events Travel Sports media kit still positions BizBash, TSNN, and Corporate Event News as one network. Its labelled audience lines (2024 users 1.8M, newsletter 60,000+, active subscribers 215,000 approx.) are a different metric set from the 2025 kit (combined social 378K, monthly uniques 164K, owned-channel views 411K, newsletter 93.8K). The 2025 kit also cites a \\$1.1M average event budget . Do not blend those models. Source-dated products and both metric sets live on Media and amplification. Brand-level figures in the kit include: Brand Audience highlights --- --- BizBash 107K monthly uniques, 231K monthly views, 46.7K newsletter subscribers TSNN 53K monthly users, 175K monthly views, 42.9K total newsletter subscribers Corporate Event News Positioned around corporate event and meeting planners; no comparable traffic totals are published in the kit How the offer fits together The product portfolio can be sold as standalone placements or combined around an event lifecycle: 1. Before an event — sponsored social, eblasts, newsletters, pre-show email, content activation, retargeting, and event targeting. 2. At the event — trade-show booths, signage, hospitality, activations, branded attendee touchpoints, hosted-buyer programs, app sponsorships, push notifications, and custom video. 3. After the event — post-show email, event targeting, lead insights, retargeting, sponsored content, and other media that extends campaign reach. See Event sponsorships for the Connect Marketplace 2026 inventory, Media and amplification for the cross-brand media portfolio, and Media specs for creative sizes and submission rules. Supplier marketplace packages and pricing live on Packages and pricing in Exhibitor Support. Next Connect Marketplace 2026 inventory: booths, signage, hospitality, and add-ons.","path":"/products-services/overview"},{"title":"Knowledge-base change log","sidebarTitle":"Change log and archive","description":"Material architecture, governance, and publication-boundary changes to Connect Company Brain.","headings":[],"body":"- Company Brain ingest now creates Master Docs and Live Events drafts with the Notion REST API. Claude Agent SDK remains only for engagement replies and for mirroring MDX after Published and Verified. See Company Brain ingest agent. - Kept the task-led Welcome page as the front door and moved the longer orientation to How to use this brain. - Collapsed nested navigation groups by default, restored their disclosure controls, and kept top-level icons as sparse visual landmarks. - Removed routing stubs and the unverified Shopify and Stripe placeholder from primary navigation while keeping their source pages available by direct link and search. - Restored the system, light, and dark appearance choices. - Replaced Maple's display title, large lead, and em-based headings with a hardcoded rem scale so page title, lead, section headings, and body keep one hierarchy on every viewport. - Added a launchd Claude Agent SDK job that watches source/inbox/incoming/ and company-brain@agentmail.to (Mac Kitchin only), writes Notion drafts, and mirrors Company Brain MDX only after Master Docs is Published and Verified. See Company Brain ingest agent. - Media and amplification now source-dates starting prices to the 2026 Events Travel Sports kit PDF, after the Master Docs extract was marked Published. 2025 figures stay on the page as comparison. Conflicts are logged on Known issues. Notion verification of that row is still Unverified. - Restored Maple sidenav groups (no top tab bar), section eyebrows, and default Maple typography. - Removed custom CSS that hid sidebar icons and uppercased group labels. Informa navy remains on the internal banner only. - Rebuilt the sidebar around the ten-section Company Brain architecture, without section numbers. - Welcome Find-an-answer routes, exhibitor and hosted-buyer response-library children, systems registration and Shopify/Stripe gap pages, and split policy/template pages were added. Shopify and Stripe remain unverified. - Customer FAQs stay at /welcome/customer-faqs as the canonical extract and are linked from both response libraries rather than duplicated. - Directory now uses connect-prod User emails where they disagreed with Master People. - Recorded Stephen Han as Finance area owner. He is not in Master People; Master Areas still has no Finance owner. - Added @informa.com addresses to the staff directory from Master People, and logged Master People vs connect-prod email conflicts on Known issues. - Reframed Exhibitor Support and Hosted Buyer Support as internal staff playbooks. - Replaced the eight numbered sections with ten task-led, unnumbered sections. - Split Systems & Data, AI & Automation, and Projects into peer navigation sections. - Restored systems, AI, integration, and project pages that were deleted during the initial cleanup. - Added task-led section landing pages, redirects, source/governance shortcuts, and a reusable page contract. - Moved the detailed event calendar into its canonical calendar path. - Added Connect Travel, BizBash, TSNN, Customer FAQs, sales-process, dashboard, Visit Create, and Elysia documentation. - Recorded live event-site conflicts without reconciling them. Git history remains the authoritative file-level record. This page records only material reader-facing changes.","path":"/reference/change-log"},{"title":"Content ownership and review","sidebarTitle":"","description":"The page contract every substantive Company Brain page should make discoverable.","headings":["Staff guidance","Customer-safe information","Internal-only context","Limits and escalation","Related pages"],"body":"- Purpose: Make source, owner, status, and escalation visible on every substantive page. - Audience: Page authors and reviewers. - Canonical source: This page and the knowledge-article template. - Owner: Mac Kitchin. - Source status: Canonical for this knowledge base. - Last reviewed: 27 August 2026. Staff guidance Every substantive page should identify: 1. Purpose — why an employee would use the page. 2. Audience — the staff roles for whom it is relevant. 3. Canonical source — the system or document that owns the facts. 4. Owner — the person or team responsible for maintenance. 5. Source status — Canonical, Working, or Deprecated. 6. Last reviewed — including the expected review cadence. 7. Staff guidance — what employees should do. 8. Customer-safe information — what may be communicated externally. 9. Internal-only context — operational details that must remain internal. 10. Limits and escalation — what staff must not promise, disclose, or decide. 11. Related pages — links to the next likely staff task. Use the knowledge article template when creating a page. Customer-safe information Customer-safe wording within a page does not make the complete page suitable for public distribution or direct customer access. Internal-only context Working and Deprecated labels, ownership gaps, and review cadences. Limits and escalation Do not present Working material as policy. Reconciliation of conflicts is a human decision by the source owner. Related pages Starter frontmatter and headings. Who owns each source system.","path":"/reference/content-ownership"},{"title":"Data governance standards","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Company Brain rules for record data, metric definitions, and unresolved conflicts.","headings":["Staff guidance","Customer-safe information","Internal-only context","Limits and escalation","Related pages"],"body":"- Purpose: Keep record data out of this site and preserve source ownership. - Audience: Anyone querying systems or editing data pages. - Canonical source: Data quality and governance for operating controls. - Owner: Mac Kitchin for this site's data pages; hosted buyer datasets — Bill Mathis. - Source status: Canonical for the Company Brain rules; dashboards remain Working. - Last reviewed: 27 August 2026. Staff guidance - Respect the owning system. This site is not a system of record. - Keep record-level customer, visitor, and payment data out of MDX. - Use canonical metric definitions on Metrics and definitions. - Preserve unresolved conflicts on Known issues. Do not pick a winner. Customer-safe information Aggregate statistics and published package tables only. Never a full attendee or buyer contact list. Internal-only context SOQL, object API names, known Salesforce defects, and unowned-area notes. Limits and escalation Do not copy webhook URLs that contain secrets. Hosted-buyer dataset questions go to Bill Mathis. Schema questions go to Mac Kitchin. Related pages Operating controls in the systems section. Open data-quality problems.","path":"/reference/data-governance-standards"},{"title":"Glossary","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Everyday company terminology used across Connect Meetings & Events.","headings":["Business and event terms","Commercial terms","Brands and programmes","Governance vocabulary","Next"],"body":"Business language only. Salesforce API names and object mappings live on the Salesforce glossary. Business and event terms Term Definition --- --- MICE Meetings, Incentives, Conferences, Exhibitions — the industry we serve. Marketplace A Connect in-person event where planners meet suppliers in scheduled one-to-one appointments. Reverse tradeshow Our event format: buyers sit at tables and suppliers rotate to them, rather than buyers walking an expo floor. Hosted buyer An event planner whose attendance is sponsored — flights and hotel covered — in exchange for completing an assigned appointment schedule. Anchor planner A hosted buyer category signalling unusually high sourcing volume. Power Pass A non-hosted badge. Full event access and education, but no pre-scheduled appointments. Regiception Combined registration and opening reception that starts most Connect events. Track A qualification-gated segment of a marketplace: Association, Corporate, Sports, Specialty, International, Informa. Club Connect Points-based hosted buyer rewards programme, redeemable for priority seating, hospitality suite access, and transportation. CMP credits Certified Meeting Professional continuing-education credits earned in our education sessions. Event amplification Advertising add-ons sold alongside an event package: custom video, lead insights, digital media, event targeting. Commercial terms Term Definition --- --- IO / Insertion Order A signed advertising order. Every Salesforce Opportunity carries one. Product year The calendar year a product is sold for . A deal closed in December 2026 can carry product year 2027. Closed Won The booked stage. Expired A stale deal that timed out. Closed but not lost — exclude from win-rate denominators. Barter A non-cash trade deal. Always exclude from cash revenue. PSL Prospect / Sponsorship Lead. Total spend Historical account spend rolled up by product year. Sales territory Midwest, Northeast, South, West, or International. Taken from the account billing state. Brands and programmes Term Definition --- --- BizBash Events media and awards brand within Informa Connect. See BizBash. Event Experience Awards BizBash's annual awards for event design, strategy, and execution. 14th edition open as of August 2026. BizBash Innovation Forum Three-day BizBash conference, 23–25 August 2026, Tampa Convention Center. Not an appointment marketplace. TSNN Trade Show News Network. See TSNN. TSNN Awards Annual exhibition-industry awards. 16th edition in 2026; nominations close 30 September; ceremony 16 November in Milwaukee with IAEE. TSNN Top 250 Annual ranking of US trade shows by net square footage, attendance, exhibitors, and location. TripEasy Travel partner that sends hosted-buyer flight-booking invites. BizBashPayNow Square checkout link used for supplier invoices, per the Customer FAQ library. Connect Travel The travel-sector arm. Live events are eTourism Marketplace and eTourism Summit. Hosted DMO The hosted-buyer equivalent on Connect Travel events. Company OS The Notion workspace that holds projects, people, docs, and the source-of-truth registry. PARA Projects, Areas, Resources, Archive — the navigation model Company OS uses. Governance vocabulary Term Definition --- --- Canonical Authoritative. Exactly one canonical entry exists per information type. Working Useful but unverified. Must be checked before it is relied on. Deprecated Retained for history. Must not be used for new work. Knowledge Candidates The human review queue for mined knowledge flagged as sensitive — pricing, legal, PII. Next The 29 grounded supplier and hosted-buyer answers agents should cite.","path":"/reference/glossary"},{"title":"Knowledge article template","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Starter frontmatter and headings for a substantive Company Brain page.","headings":["Staff guidance","Customer-safe information","Internal-only context","Limits and escalation","Template","Staff guidance","Customer-safe information","Internal-only context","Limits and escalation","Related pages","Related pages"],"body":"- Purpose: Start a new factual page with the required contract. - Audience: Page authors. - Canonical source: This template. - Owner: Mac Kitchin. - Source status: Canonical for this knowledge base. - Last reviewed: 27 August 2026. Staff guidance Copy the block below. Replace bracketed text. Do not invent facts to close a TODO. Customer-safe information Not applicable. Internal-only context Template only. Limits and escalation Unverified facts stay as . Template Exhibitor and hosted-buyer support pages must still distinguish what staff should do, what may be communicated, what remains internal, what must be verified per event or record, and where exceptions escalate. Related pages SOP and project templates.","path":"/reference/knowledge-article-template"},{"title":"Knowledge-base standards","sidebarTitle":"","description":"How Company Brain pages should be written, linked, titled, and added to navigation.","headings":["Staff guidance","Customer-safe information","Internal-only context","Limits and escalation","Related pages"],"body":"- Purpose: Apply a consistent writing and navigation standard. - Audience: Anyone editing this site. - Canonical source: This page and AGENTS.md. - Owner: Mac Kitchin. - Source status: Canonical for this knowledge base. - Last reviewed: 27 August 2026. Staff guidance - Write for Connect staff and approved internal agents. - Use British spelling, active voice, sentence-case headings, and concrete language. - Give each factual page one canonical navigation home; link to it from related sections. - Use root-relative internal links without file extensions. - Preserve TODO markers for unverified facts rather than filling gaps. - Use unique page titles for search, with shorter sidebarTitle values where useful. - Add new pages to docs.json , then validate navigation and links. Customer-safe information Not applicable. These are internal editorial rules. Internal-only context The Company Brain is a derived mirror, not a public handbook. Limits and escalation Do not invent facts to close a TODO. Schema and site-structure questions go to Mac Kitchin. Related pages Hub for all governance rules on this site. Required page contract.","path":"/reference/knowledge-base-standards"},{"title":"Known issues","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Open data-quality problems, unresolved content conflicts, and documentation gaps.","headings":["Data quality in Salesforce","Ownership gaps","Media spec conflicts","Event content conflicts","Contact page conflicts","Cancellation policy conflicts","Documentation gaps","Capture gaps"],"body":"Everything on this page is a known, unresolved problem. It is here so that nobody - person or agent - has to rediscover it, and so that nobody quietly papers over a conflict by picking a side. The standing rule: preserve both versions, flag the conflict, do not reconcile. Reconciliation is a decision for the owner of the source, not for a reader or a retrieval system. Data quality in Salesforce Issue Detail Impact Owner --- --- --- --- Reason Lost c is blank on 421 of 424 lost deals Loss reason is effectively unrecorded Structured win/loss and objection analytics are impossible from CRM fields. Loss intelligence has to be mined from email instead Mac Kitchin Opportunity Product Type c is 100% null Product type never captured at opportunity level Product-level win/loss analysis unavailable Mac Kitchin 26 account-team rep assignments against 20,876 BizBash advertiser accounts Account ownership effectively absent on the BizBash side Routing, coverage reporting, and agent escalation all break Mac Kitchin Two agency-contact objects Agency Contact c and Agency Contacts c overlap Ambiguous joins Mac Kitchin ProposalDetailsTrigger on API v29 Dates from 2014 Technical debt Mac Kitchin Silverpop and Marketing Cloud coexist Silverpop notionally being retired Duplicate email tracking Mac Kitchin Ownership gaps Gap Detail --- --- Customer Success has no area owner Three Customer Success Managers work in it, each reporting to a different sales leader Stephen Han is Finance owner here but not in Company OS Staff confirmation 27 August 2026. Active connect-prod User stephen.han@informa.com . Master People has no Stephen Han row. Master Areas Finance Owner is empty Staff email conflicts between Master People and connect-prod User Directory uses the Salesforce User address where the two sources disagree Operations has two recorded owners Team HQ names Rachel Piper; the Areas database names Andrew Dysart Alexa Dye has two recorded managers Master People records Rachel Piper; Matt Johnson's direct-reports rollup also lists her. Rachel Piper is treated as correct An unnamed placeholder area exists Created without a name or owner, retained for review rather than deleted. Merge or archive once ownership is confirmed Media spec conflicts Captured 27 August 2026 from OneDrive Documents/Media Specs . Full tables live on Media specs. Onsite Display AdSpecs1.pdf and Banner Program Specs cap image files at 100KB and allow desktop-leaderboard animation. Onsite Display Ads Rev 20220902 caps files at 250KB and marks the desktop leaderboard static only. The 300 x 50 unit is Mobile leaderboard on two sheets and Mobile billboard on the 20220902 sheet. The 300 x 250 unit is Display box or Medium rectangle depending on the sheet. The 2025 dedicated-email sheet caps HTML at 600 pixels wide and states no minimum height. The 2024 sheet still in the folder allows 600–728 pixels and requires a minimum height of 820 pixels. bizbashprod@informa.com , production@bizbash.com , and connectprod@informa.com all appear. Offsite banners reject most third-party tags; onsite sheets accept DoubleClick-class tags after confirmation. The index PDF labels the newsletter BizBash Buzz . The spec PDF is titled Today in Events: Our New Daily eNewsletter . Event content conflicts Found during the August 2026 site crawl. All are live on informaconnect.com as of capture. The agenda pages are headed \"Tuesday 27 January 2026\", \"Wednesday 28 January 2026\", and \"Thursday 29 January 2026\". Every other page - Home, How to Attend, Who Attends, Hosted Buyers, Contact, Terms - states 22-24 February 2027. The sponsors page meta description references \"Connect North 2026\", a third variant. The sponsors page lists Visit Pittsburgh as host, while the homepage hero reads \"Thank you, Pittsburgh - Cincinnati, you're up next!\" The sponsor list appears to be carried over from the prior Pittsburgh edition. The sponsors page lists Visit Lake Tahoe as host with Margaritaville Resort Lake Tahoe, Alterra Mountain Company and others, despite the 2026 event being at The Westin Kierland Resort and Spa, Scottsdale. Carried over from a prior South Lake Tahoe edition. The Plan Your Visit page describes Frisco, TX - hotels, meeting space, airports, attractions - while the venue everywhere else is The Woodlands, TX. Leftover page template from a different Connect event. The Plan Your Visit page describes Fort Worth, TX including the Stockyards District, despite the venue being Branson Convention Center, Branson, MO. The Faith Track page heading states 9-11 November 2026 while every other page and the actual two-day agenda state 9-10 November. Nov 9-10 is treated as canonical. The contact-us page displays \"22-24 April, 2025, Wynn and Encore Las Vegas\", conflicting with the 21-23 April 2027 at The Wynn used consistently everywhere else. Supplier badge appointments: \"up to 25\" on the homepage, \"up to 40\" on How to Attend. Hosted buyer appointments: \"up to 45\" on two pages, \"up to 40 curated 9-minute meetings\" on the Hosted Buyer Experience page. \"900+ actively sourcing planners\" on Why Attend versus \"1,100+ actively sourcing hosted buyers\" on Why Sponsor - unclear whether these are different populations or different snapshot dates. CMP credit hours cited as both 14 and 15. Homepage body copy refers to \"Connect Winter Marketplace 2027\" despite dates of 18-20 November 2026. This may be a season-year branding convention rather than an error. Captured 27 August 2026. The Hosted Buyer heading is followed by supplier package copy, and the Suppliers heading is followed by hosted-buyer copy. Button labels are Hosted Buyer Registration ( 3d5r46gen3aq5 ) and Supplier Registration ( 25ss34wgg94rd ). This site records the button labels and the swapped copy. Do not silently correct either. Captured 27 August 2026. Destination Marketer and Travel Industry Supplier \"Register Now\" buttons on How to Attend both pointed at https://register.visitcloud.com/survey/0vuudv97bjcrg . The homepage also expose","path":"/reference/known-issues"},{"title":"Reference and governance","sidebarTitle":"Overview","description":"Entry point for terminology, source ownership, standards, templates, known issues, and change history.","headings":[],"body":"Use this section to determine what a term means, which source owns a fact, whether a conflict is known, and how Company Brain content should be maintained. Task-led lookup routes live in Welcome; this section holds the governing references behind them. Task-led routes to events, products, owners, systems, and customer FAQs. Approved supplier and hosted-buyer answers with confidence scores. Business terminology and links to the Salesforce field-level companion. Canonical, Working, and Deprecated sources with owners and refresh cadences. Knowledge, content, data, customer-support, and AI governance rules. Required page contract plus knowledge, SOP, and project templates. Open conflicts, gaps, data-quality problems, and ownership ambiguity. Material architecture and governance changes to this knowledge base.","path":"/reference/overview"},{"title":"Policies and standards","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Verified standards for Company Brain content, ownership, customer support, data governance, and internal agents.","headings":["Staff guidance","Customer-support standards","Customer-safe information","Internal-only context","Limits and escalation"],"body":"- Purpose: Find the right governance rule before editing or answering. - Audience: Staff and page authors. - Canonical source: This section. - Owner: Mac Kitchin. - Source status: Canonical for this knowledge base. Company HR, Legal, and training policies remain Working and are not imported. - Last reviewed: 27 August 2026. These standards govern this knowledge base. They do not replace HR, Legal, Finance, privacy, or information-security policies that have not been imported. Staff guidance Writing, linking, titles, and navigation rules. The eleven-part page contract. Record data, metrics, and conflict handling. Hard limits for internal agents. Customer-support standards - Exhibitor Support and Hosted Buyer Support are internal staff playbooks. - Customer-facing agents may ground on approved FAQs, knowledge articles, and event sources. - Staff must not share internal IDs, defects, ownership gaps, private source locations, or named customer records. - Billing, invoices, legal, contracts, complaints, specific discounts, and unpublished rates require human escalation. - Never share a full attendee or buyer contact list. Customer-safe information Not applicable. These are internal rules. Internal-only context Working labels on company policies, HR, legal, and training materials. Limits and escalation Do not present this page as a complete corporate policy library. Company policies, HR and legal materials, and role-specific training are marked Working in the source registry.","path":"/reference/policies-standards"},{"title":"Project template","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Required sections for an active or archived Company Brain project page.","headings":["Staff guidance","Customer-safe information","Internal-only context","Limits and escalation","Related pages"],"body":"- Purpose: Keep project pages consistent with Company OS. - Audience: Project leads and page authors. - Canonical source: Master Projects in Company OS. - Owner: Mac Kitchin for the register. - Source status: Canonical for page shape; Master Projects remains authoritative for owner, stage, priority, area, and timeline. - Last reviewed: 27 August 2026. Staff guidance Project pages follow this structure: 1. Overview 2. Goals and success criteria 3. Key decisions 4. Next steps 5. Risks and blockers 6. Related documentation Do not treat a project page as current-state policy. Customer-safe information Not applicable. Project pages are internal. Internal-only context Stage, priority, and decision logs. Limits and escalation The Master Projects row wins on owner, stage, priority, area, and timeline. Related pages Current change work. Other page templates.","path":"/reference/project-template"},{"title":"SOP template","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Starter structure for an internal procedure page.","headings":["Staff guidance","Customer-safe information","Internal-only context","Limits and escalation","Template","Before starting","Staff guidance","Exceptions and recovery","Customer-safe information","Internal-only context","Limits and escalation","Related pages","Related pages"],"body":"- Purpose: Start a procedure page that staff can follow. - Audience: Page authors. - Canonical source: This template. - Owner: Mac Kitchin. - Source status: Canonical for this knowledge base. - Last reviewed: 27 August 2026. Staff guidance Copy the block below. Every step should name the evidence of completion. Customer-safe information Not applicable unless the SOP itself is customer-facing — most are not. Internal-only context Template only. Limits and escalation Do not publish an SOP that implies a customer commitment. Template Related pages Knowledge article and project templates.","path":"/reference/sop-template"},{"title":"Source of truth","sidebarTitle":"Source-of-truth registry","description":"Which system owns which fact, who maintains it, and how often it refreshes.","headings":["Truth levels","Registry rules","The registry","Canonical","Working - verify before relying on","Deprecated","How this site relates to the registry","Refresh cadences at a glance","Next"],"body":"The Source of Truth Registry is the decision layer for information ownership. Check it before editing a page, and before answering when sources disagree. Truth levels Authoritative. Exactly one canonical entry exists per information type. Useful but unverified. Must be checked before it is relied on, and disclosed as unverified when cited. Retained for history. Must not be used for new work. Registry rules 1. Every major information type gets exactly one Canonical entry. 2. Every entry needs an Owner and a Refresh Cadence . 3. When a source is replaced, mark the old row Deprecated and link the replacement. 4. AI agents should prefer Canonical rows and disclose uncertainty when a row is Working or Deprecated. The registry Canonical Information type System Owner Refresh Notes --- --- --- --- --- Client and account data Salesforce Andrew Dysart Live connect-prod Org 00D30000001H2naEAC . Notion documents structure only, never record data Salesforce org metadata and schema Salesforce Mac Kitchin As needed Refresh via sf sobject describe and sf data query Sales process and business processes Salesforce Andrew Dysart Quarterly Business Processes catalogue in the Salesforce KB, linked to Customer FAQs Event information, calendar, operational resources Notion Mac Kitchin Monthly Live Events is the single authoritative event source , one page per event. Customer-service chatbots answer only from these Projects and initiatives Notion Mac Kitchin Weekly Master Projects, one row per initiative Tasks Notion Mac Kitchin Live Master Tasks. Legacy Tasks, My Tasks, and Claude Tasks are deprecated - do not add rows there People directory Notion Mac Kitchin Quarterly Master People is the data source; Team HQ is the front door Workspace structure and conventions Notion Mac Kitchin Quarterly PARA navigation, master databases, naming and archive rules SOPs and process documentation Notion Mac Kitchin As needed Company-wide in Master Docs; Salesforce-specific in the SF KB Company terminology Notion Mac Kitchin As needed Company glossary in Master Docs; Salesforce terms in the SF KB glossary AI agent documentation Notion Mac Kitchin Monthly AI & Automation Hub plus the AI & Agent Library. Agentforce detail in the SF KB Prompt library Notion Mac Kitchin As needed Canonical merged index of Prompt Library plus Agent Prompts & Examples Customer FAQ answers Notion Mac Kitchin Monthly ~31 grounded Q&A pairs with confidence scores, ranked by frequency. Agents cite these Supplier knowledge, email-mined Email-mined Mac Kitchin Quarterly FAQs, articles, tribal knowledge, gaps, automation opportunities. Sensitive items gated Connect and BizBash consolidation analysis Notion Mac Kitchin Quarterly The seven-part analysis Working - verify before relying on Information type System Owner Notes --- --- --- --- Reporting dashboards Salesforce Mac Kitchin Live in connect-prod but not indexed in Notion . Company Brain now has a dashboard index (Working) Company policies Notion Mac Kitchin Policy docs in Master Docs. HR and legal policies not yet imported Training materials and onboarding Notion Mac Kitchin Training docs in Master Docs. Needs role-specific onboarding steps per department Media creative specifications OneDrive Documents/Media Specs Andrew Dysart / Michela Giovannotto Mirrored on Media specs. Sheets disagree on file size, units, and inboxes Public event marketing sites, registration, decks, and kits informaconnect.com , bizbash.com , tsnn.com , VisitCloud, Turtl, idem.events Event operations / brand owners Indexed on Public event sites. Recaptured 27 August 2026. Not Canonical for dates, venues, eligibility, or policy Deprecated Information type Replacement --- --- Master Events database Live Events. The database contained zero records and was archived on 29 July 2026 Legacy Tasks / My Tasks / Claude Tasks Master Tasks Legacy people directory page Team HQ as the front door; Master People remains the data source Duplicate Salesforce Knowledge Base The original SF KB, which is linked from all hubs How this site relates to the registry This Company Brain is a derived mirror , not a registry entry in its own right. It restates canonical material in a form that is easier to read and easier for an agent to retrieve from. It does not hold record data and it is not authoritative over a source system. If this site and a canonical source disagree, the canonical source wins and this site is out of date. Report the drift to the owner listed above rather than editing around it. Refresh cadences at a glance Cadence What it covers --- --- Live / synced Client and account data, tasks, reporting dashboards Weekly Projects and initiatives Monthly Event information, AI agent documentation, Customer FAQs Quarterly Sales and business processes, people directory, workspace conventions, supplier knowledge, consolidation analysis, training As needed Schema and metadata, SOPs, terminology, prompt library, policies Next Company and Salesforce terms used across this site.","path":"/reference/source-of-truth"},{"title":"Templates","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Reusable templates for knowledge articles, SOPs, projects, and internally governed support content.","headings":["Staff guidance","Customer-safe information","Internal-only context","Limits and escalation"],"body":"- Purpose: Start a new page from an approved shape. - Audience: Page authors. - Canonical source: This section. - Owner: Mac Kitchin. - Source status: Canonical for this knowledge base. - Last reviewed: 27 August 2026. Staff guidance Required page contract and headings. Procedure steps and verification. Company OS project page shape. Exhibitor and hosted-buyer support pages must explicitly distinguish what staff should do, what staff may communicate externally, what remains internal, what must be verified for the event or record, and where exceptions are escalated. Customer-safe information Not applicable. Internal-only context Template only. Limits and escalation Do not fill a template with invented facts.","path":"/reference/templates"},{"title":"Customer FAQs","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Extract of the Canonical Customer FAQ library: 29 grounded supplier and hosted-buyer answers with confidence scores.","headings":["How to use a pair","Hosted buyer and attendee","Supplier packages, appointments, and events","Media, creative, and campaigns","Contracting, payment, and discounts","Next"],"body":"This page is an internal extract of the Notion Customer FAQs database — the Canonical home for approved response pairs. Staff and customer-facing agents should use the Short answer field as customer-safe wording, then check the Event calendar for dates, venues, and policy. Do not rewrite the answers from memory or share the internal annotations. - Source: Notion database 🙋‍♂️ Customer FAQs under the Salesforce Knowledge Base. - Owner: Mac Kitchin. - Refresh: monthly. - Extracted: 12 August 2026. - Last validated in source: 26 June 2026. Registry notes say ~31 pairs; this extract found 29 rows. Every row is still status In progress . Several answers mention billing, discounts, payment timing, or attendee lists. Those rows carry a When not to use rule. Follow it. Escalate amounts, terms, and named lists to a human — see Guardrails. Event facts in this library are restated from email and onboarding copy. If a pair disagrees with the Event calendar or Known issues, report both internally. Do not pick a winner. How to use a pair Field Meaning --- --- Short answer The approved reply. Quote this, do not paraphrase into a new policy. Confidence Source-scored. Surface the score when it is below 0.8, or when the field is blank. Frequency / rank How often the question appeared in the mined corpus. Blank means unranked. When not to use Hard stop. Usually account-specific money or PII. Sensitive review-queue items (specific discounts, invoice schedules, unpublished rates) stay in Knowledge Candidates. They are not on this page. Hosted buyer and attendee Short answer: Add the dates (Aug 24-26, Tampa), book your hotel via the link, watch for your flight invite from TripEasy, and complete your appointment-portal selections. Marketplace 1:1 appointments are the only mandatory requirement (8/25 10am - 8/26 5pm). When to use: Newly approved/secured attendee asking \"what now?\" Frequency 30 · Rank 5 · Confidence 0.85 Short answer: You receive a refund check at the conclusion of your 1:1 appointments — but you must attend ALL of your scheduled 1:1 appointments to receive your deposit back. When to use: Attendee asking about deposit refund timing or conditions. Frequency 14 · Rank 14 · Confidence 0.85 The Hosted Buyer application playbook and the archived VIP playbook also describe an on-site or post-completion refund. Mechanism and timing remain unconfirmed. Report both versions internally and do not invent a single process. Short answer: A flight-booking invitation comes directly from our travel partner, TripEasy ( travelnoreply@tripeasy.com ) — add it to your safe-sender list. The travel team ( connectmeetings@its.com ) can help. When to use: Attendee asking how/when to book flights or who to contact for travel. Frequency 12 · Rank 18 · Confidence 0.82 Short answer: You'll meet roughly 30-40 suppliers/buyers over the event. Closer to the date you'll get appointment-portal access to make your selections — make as many as possible. When to use: Attendee asking how many meetings or how matchmaking works. Frequency 12 · Rank 17 · Confidence 0.80 Short answer: Yes — \"Connect is Better with Friends\". Refer a qualified planner who has never attended a Connect event; if they're approved and attend, you both get \\$250 after the event. When to use: Attendee asking about referrals or incentives for bringing peers. Frequency 8 · Confidence 0.80 Short answer: Yes, with rep approval and if the colleague is qualified (typically director-level or above). When to use: Invitee declines but wants to send a colleague. Confidence blank in the database (source note 0.70). Short answer: Select director-level+ buyers (e.g., DMO/state tourism leaders) are fully hosted — flight and hotel covered — in exchange for taking pre-scheduled appointments. A refundable deposit secures the spot. When to use: Buyer/DMO asking about hosted invitations, eligibility, or what's covered. When not to use: Pricing/financial specifics beyond the refundable deposit — confirm with the rep. Confidence blank (source note 0.80). Short answer: An intimate destination-marketing show (Nov 18-20, 2026, West Palm Beach) with max 75 hosted DMO/state leaders and 75 travel suppliers, built around 35 pre-scheduled 1:1 appointments. When to use: DMO/supplier asking about eTourism Marketplace format, dates, or hosting. The live event site says 50 DMOs on How to Attend and 75 on Who Attends. This FAQ uses 75. See Known issues. Dates and venue: Connect Travel. Public site: informaconnect.com/etourism-marketplace. Supplier packages, appointments, and events Short answer: Connect Marketplace 2026 is August 24-26 in Tampa, FL — the flagship show of the year. When to use: Any supplier asking about dates, location, or which show is the flagship. Confidence blank (source note 0.90). Confirm against Connect Marketplace. Public site: informaconnect.com/connect-marketplace. Short answer: Appointment-based packages (commonly 24, 30, or 40 pre-scheduled 1:1 appointments) with pre-qualified, appointment-ready buyers, plus access to the buyer/appointment portal. When to use: Supplier asking what they get for participating or comparing package tiers. When not to use: For exact current-year pricing — route to the rate card / rep. Published flagship prices live on Business model and Packages and pricing. Short answer: 20x20 exhibit booths are offered in tiers — Basic Turnkey, Turnkey, and fully Activated (with a traffic-driving experience). BizBash/Connect handles booth logistics and coordination; design upgrades cost extra. When to use: Supplier asking about exhibiting/booth options or buildout. When not to use: Exact booth pricing — sensitive; confirm with rep. Frequency 10 · Confidence 0.75 Short answer: Appointments are pre-scheduled before you arrive, so every meeting is booked in advance. Early registrants get early access to the appointment portal. When to use: Supplier asking how matchmaking/appointments work or why to sign early. Short answer: Reps can share a sample of atte","path":"/welcome/customer-faqs"},{"title":"Find a product or published price","sidebarTitle":"Find a product or published price","description":"Staff route to appointment packages, sponsorships, media, and the limits on quoting source-dated prices.","headings":["Staff guidance","Customer-safe information","Internal-only context","Limits and escalation","Related pages"],"body":"- Purpose: Reach published commercial products without inventing or interpolating a rate. - Audience: Sales, operations, and approved internal agents. - Canonical source: Published package tables and source-dated media/sponsorship kits. - Owner: Connect marketplace sales — Suzi Schnell; media and travel — Andrew Dysart; BizBash — Michela Giovannotto. - Source status: Working for unpublished sponsorship prospectus pricing; Canonical only where a package table is published. - Last reviewed: 27 August 2026. Staff guidance Start at Products and services overview. For marketplace appointment packages use Packages and published pricing. Confirm the source date before quoting. Customer-safe information Published package contents and prices on the package tables, with the source date stated. Internal-only context Unpublished sponsorship prospectus rates, availability counts that may be stale, and internal commercial strategy. Limits and escalation Never quote a specific rate beyond published package tables. Never invent a discount or extend an expired offer. Discounts go to the account's sales owner. Billing and invoice amounts go to Stephen Han (Finance). Related pages Commercial offer map. Published appointment packages. Banner, email, and native creative specifications.","path":"/welcome/find-a-product"},{"title":"Find an answer","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Task-led routes to the most common staff questions, with the source and escalation checks to make first.","headings":["Before relying on an answer"],"body":"Start with the task, not the department. These routes lead to the canonical Company Brain page and identify when staff must check a live system instead. Dates, venues, formats, portfolios, and the unresolved conflicts that affect event answers. Homepages, VisitCloud registration, agendas, floor maps, hotel blocks, and Turtl decks. Appointment packages, sponsorships, media, and the limits on quoting source-dated prices. Route a decision, permission, exception, or authoritative-answer request. Internal playbooks for supplier fit, packages, appointments, registration, onsite support, and follow-up. Internal playbooks for eligibility, coverage, approval, registration, attendance, and escalation. Salesforce, Visit Create, Company OS, metrics, dashboards, integrations, and system boundaries. Retrieval boundaries, disclosure limits, agent inventory, MCP tooling, and escalation rules. Known source disagreements, data-quality problems, ownership gaps, and unverified material. Before relying on an answer Use the source-of-truth registry to identify the system that owns the fact. Canonical material is preferred. Working material must be verified. Deprecated material must not be presented as current. If two sources disagree, report both versions and route reconciliation to the source owner. Do not choose a winner. Use the relevant support playbook to distinguish customer-safe information from internal-only context. Billing, invoices, legal, contracts, complaints, specific discounts, and unpublished rates always require a human owner. This site documents structure, meaning, ownership, and process. Query the owning system for live record data; do not infer current customer or deal state from a Company Brain page.","path":"/welcome/find-an-answer"},{"title":"Find an event","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Staff route to dates, venues, formats, portfolios, and unresolved event-source conflicts.","headings":["Staff guidance","Customer-safe information","Internal-only context","Limits and escalation","Related pages"],"body":"- Purpose: Reach the controlled event-fact page without reconstructing dates from marketing copy. - Audience: All Connect staff and approved internal agents. - Canonical source: Live Events wiki, mirrored on the Event calendar. - Owner: Mac Kitchin for this site; event operations — Tinsley Conway. - Source status: Canonical for calendar facts; public event-site conflicts remain on Known issues. - Last reviewed: 27 August 2026. Review when the calendar source date changes. Staff guidance Use the Event calendar. If the question is which event family a planner or supplier belongs in, start from Portfolio overview. Customer-safe information Published dates, venues, formats, and eligibility that appear on the calendar after you have checked Known issues. Internal-only context Unresolved date, city, appointment-count, and contact conflicts; TODOs on public URLs; internal event-operations notes. Limits and escalation Do not invent a date, venue, appointment count, or cancellation rule to close a gap. Event operations exceptions go to Tinsley Conway. Related pages Source-dated event facts. Live event-site conflicts to report rather than reconcile.","path":"/welcome/find-an-event"},{"title":"Find an owner","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Staff route to the person or team who owns a decision, system, dataset, or escalation.","headings":["Staff guidance","Customer-safe information","Internal-only context","Limits and escalation","Related pages"],"body":"- Purpose: Route a question, approval, or exception to the responsible human. - Audience: All Connect staff and approved internal agents. - Canonical source: Master People and Master Areas in Company OS, plus the Who owns what table on this site. - Owner: Mac Kitchin for the directory mirror. - Source status: Working where ownership gaps remain. - Last reviewed: 27 August 2026. Staff guidance Use Who owns what for systems, datasets, and escalations. Use the staff directory for email addresses. Use Departments for area remit. Customer-safe information Named public event or sales contacts only after checking Known issues. Do not present an internal owner as a customer-facing inbox unless the page says so. Internal-only context Ownership gaps, dual recorded managers, and Finance/Customer Success source drift. Limits and escalation Do not invent an owner to fill a gap. Customer Success remains unowned as an area. Billing goes to Stephen Han (Finance). Related pages Authoritative routing table. Role and @informa.com addresses.","path":"/welcome/find-an-owner"},{"title":"How to use this brain","sidebarTitle":"How to use","description":"A short orientation to the internal knowledge base, its source rules, and its staff support playbooks.","headings":["The ten-minute orientation","Choose the page by task","When the answer will be customer-facing","Source and status conventions","What this site does not contain","If a page is wrong or incomplete"],"body":"Use the Company Brain to find the best available answer and its owner , not to bypass the system that owns the underlying fact. The site is written for internal staff and approved internal agents. The ten-minute orientation Read Company overview, How the business works, and Brands. These explain the supplier-and-hosted-buyer economy behind the rest of the site. Use the Event calendar for dates, venues, formats, eligibility, appointment counts, and cancellation information. Event pages preserve discrepancies rather than guessing which version is correct. Public homepages, registration, maps, and decks live on Public event sites and are Working marketing pointers, not Canonical event facts. Exhibitor Support and Hosted Buyer Support are internal playbooks. They combine staff context, safe response wording, and escalation boundaries; they are not customer handbooks. People & Ownership explains the organisation. Who owns what is the routing table for questions, decisions, and escalation. Read Source of truth, then Known issues. Check both before changing a disputed fact or treating a Working source as policy. Choose the page by task Task Go to --- --- Find a page when the terminology is unclear Find an answer Answer a recurring customer question Customer FAQs, then the matching support playbook Confirm event logistics or policy Event calendar Open a public homepage, registration form, map, or deck Public event sites Route an exception or approval Who owns what Understand a Salesforce or Visit Create workflow Systems & Data Define a metric or dashboard Data and reporting Build or operate an internal agent AI & Automation Check the status of an initiative Projects When the answer will be customer-facing Establish whether the person is an exhibitor, supplier prospect, hosted-buyer applicant, approved hosted buyer, attendee, or another audience. Identify the relevant event and product year. Check the Event calendar. Do not reconstruct a date, venue, appointment count, or policy from memory, email, or a project page. Open the matching support page. Use its response guidance as the basis for a concise customer reply; do not forward the full internal page. Escalate billing, invoices, contracts, legal terms, complaints, specific discounts, unpublished prices, and named attendee lists. If sources conflict, report both versions internally and route the discrepancy to the owner. A page being readable does not make every detail customer-safe. Internal rationale, system fields, data defects, owner gaps, and escalation notes stay internal. Source and status conventions Convention Meaning --- --- Canonical The registered source that owns the fact. Working Useful operational material that is not yet authoritative. Disclose the status when it matters. Deprecated Retained for history or migration only. Do not present it as current. Source date When a time-sensitive source was captured or reviewed. It is not a promise that nothing has changed since. Known discrepancy Two sources disagree. Keep both versions and ask the owner to reconcile them. A fact is unconfirmed. Do not fill it from inference. What this site does not contain The Company Brain does not hold record-level customer data, named deal values, attendee rosters, customer contact lists, credentials, or secrets. Those remain in access-controlled systems. This site documents definitions, ownership, process, safeguards, and source locations. If a page is wrong or incomplete 1. Check the owning source in Source of truth. 2. Check Known issues for an already logged discrepancy. 3. Capture the conflicting versions, source URLs, and review date. 4. Route the decision to the owner in Who owns what. 5. Update the Company Brain only after the owning source or a documented decision supports the change. Start from the job you are trying to complete rather than guessing a section name.","path":"/welcome/how-to-use"},{"title":"Use AI safely","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Staff route to retrieval rules, disclosure limits, the agent roster, MCP tooling, and escalation.","headings":["Staff guidance","Customer-safe information","Internal-only context","Limits and escalation","Related pages"],"body":"- Purpose: Reach the operative AI policy before using or deploying an internal agent. - Audience: Staff using agents, and anyone configuring Agentforce, Elysia, or MCP tools. - Canonical source: Guardrails and retrieval rules on this site, grounded in Company OS and the supplier-knowledge governance work. - Owner: Mac Kitchin. - Source status: Canonical for hard limits on this site. - Last reviewed: 27 August 2026. Staff guidance Read Staff and agent guardrails first, then Agent retrieval rules. Inventory is on the agent roster. Tooling is on MCP and tooling. Customer-safe information Approved FAQ short answers and published event or package facts, only after the agent has retrieved the page and checked When-not-to-use rules. Internal-only context Agent configuration, MCP servers, Elysia credentials locations, prompt libraries, and source confidence scores. Limits and escalation Agents must retrieve before answering, cite the page, and stop at billing, legal, contracts, complaints, discounts, and unpublished rates. Those go to a human owner. Related pages Hard limits and answer style. What an agent may ground on.","path":"/welcome/use-ai-safely"},{"title":"Work with systems and data","sidebarTitle":"","description":"Staff route to Salesforce, Visit Create, Company OS, metrics, dashboards, and integrations.","headings":["Staff guidance","Customer-safe information","Internal-only context","Limits and escalation","Related pages"],"body":"- Purpose: Reach the system or definition page without querying live customer records into this site. - Audience: Operations, sales operations, information systems, and approved internal agents. - Canonical source: connect-prod , Visit Create, and Company OS as named on each destination page. - Owner: Salesforce schema — Mac Kitchin; hosted buyer data — Bill Mathis; information systems — Wei Zheng. - Source status: Mixed Canonical and Working; see each page. - Last reviewed: 27 August 2026. Staff guidance Start at Systems overview. For field meaning use the data model. For a number, use Metrics and definitions then query the owning system. Customer-safe information None from system pages by default. Do not describe Salesforce IDs, webhook URLs, or defects to a customer. Internal-only context Org structure, object names, integration contracts, webhook behaviour, and known data-quality defects. Limits and escalation Do not paste visitor, partner, order, or payment records into answers or MDX. Schema questions go to Mac Kitchin. Hosted-buyer datasets go to Bill Mathis. Related pages Landscape of approved systems. Objects and field meaning.","path":"/welcome/work-with-systems"}];
